window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jemas-2023"] = {"id": "jemas-2023", "title": "JEMAS(PG) 2023", "fullTitle": "JEMAS(PG) 2023, West Bengal, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "100 real questions · checked against WBJEEB's official final key · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "The 2 factor theory of motivation was given by:", "options": {"a": "Douglas McGregor.", "b": "Frederick Herzberg.", "c": "Abraham Maslow.", "d": "Victor Vroom."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the two-factor theory of motivation was given by Frederick Herzberg (1959). Frederick Herzberg, with Bernard Mausner and Barbara Snyderman, published The Motivation to Work (1959) after interviewing about 200 engineers and accountants in Pittsburgh. He asked each person to recall a time they had felt exceptionally good or exceptionally bad about their job. The good stories and the bad stories were about different things, so he concluded that satisfaction and dissatisfaction are not two ends of one scale. Motivators (achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, advancement and growth) lie in the content of the job and create satisfaction. Hygiene factors (pay, supervision, company policy, relationships, working conditions and job security) lie in the context of the job; when they are poor they cause dissatisfaction, but improving them only removes the complaint and does not motivate. That is why the theory is also called the motivation-hygiene theory. Example: Riya gets a salary rise at her bank and stops grumbling, but she only starts staying late with enthusiasm when she is given her own project and praised for it. The pay was hygiene; the project and the praise were motivators. Option b is right because Frederick Herzberg is the author of the motivation-hygiene, or two-factor, theory of work motivation. Exam trap: \"two-factor\" is a label shared by several unrelated theories: Charles Spearman's two-factor theory of intelligence (1904), O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory of avoidance learning (1947) and Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's two-factor theory of emotion (1962). Read the field in the stem (motivation at work) before choosing the name.", "topic": "Herzberg's two-factor theory", "fig": "Frederick Herzberg"}, {"q": "The Gestalt school of psychology was founded in Germany by ______ and his colleagues Kurt Koffka & Wolfgang Kohler.", "options": {"a": "James Wundt.", "b": "Max Wertheimer.", "c": "John B.Watson.", "d": "John Dewey."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Max Wertheimer (1912) founded Gestalt psychology in Germany with his colleagues Kurt Koffka and Wolfgang Kohler. The founding date is usually given as 1912, when Max Wertheimer published his study of apparent movement at Frankfurt, with Kurt Koffka and Wolfgang Kohler serving as his observers. He flashed two lines one after the other; at the right interval observers saw a single line moving from one place to the other. He called this the phi phenomenon. The movement is not present in either flash; it exists only in the pattern, so the whole cannot be built up from its parts. This was a direct attack on structuralism, which tried to break experience into elementary sensations. Gestalt (German for form or shape) psychology went on to describe the laws of perceptual organisation (figure-ground, proximity, similarity, closure, Pragnanz), Wolfgang Kohler's (1917) studies of insight in chimpanzees, Kurt Koffka's Principles of Gestalt Psychology (1935) and Kurt Lewin's field theory (1936). Example: when Arjun watches Diwali lights blinking in sequence along a balcony, he sees a light running along the wire, though each bulb only switches on and off in place. Option b is right because Max Wertheimer's phi phenomenon paper launched the school, and Kurt Koffka and Wolfgang Kohler were his co-founders. Exam trap: \"James Wundt\" never existed. The name blends William James with Wilhelm Wundt, who opened the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879 and whose elementism, carried to America by Edward Titchener (1898) as structuralism, is exactly what the Gestalt psychologists opposed. The German setting tempts students towards Wilhelm Wundt.", "topic": "Founder of Gestalt psychology", "fig": "Max Wertheimer"}, {"q": "The large complex structure at the back of the brain, also referred to as the 'little brain' is called:", "options": {"a": "Cerebellum.", "b": "Corpus callosum.", "c": "Thalamus.", "d": "Hypothalamus."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the 'little brain' at the back of the brain is the cerebellum. Cerebellum is Latin for little brain. It sits behind the pons and below the occipital lobes, and with the medulla and pons it forms the hindbrain. It makes up only about 10 percent of the brain's mass but holds more than half of its neurons, packed into a tightly folded surface. The cerebellum does not start movements (the motor cortex does); it makes them smooth and accurate by comparing the intended movement with the actual one and correcting the error. So it governs balance, posture, coordination, timing and motor skill learning, such as learning to ride a bicycle. Damage produces ataxia (an unsteady, wide-based walk), intention tremor and slurred speech. Alcohol depresses the cerebellum early, which is why police ask a suspected drunk driver to walk a straight line or touch the nose. Example: after a small stroke in his cerebellum, Kabir can still move his arm, but when he reaches for a cup his hand shakes and overshoots. Option a is right because only the cerebellum is called the little brain. Exam trap: students who remember the word 'coordination' sometimes pick the corpus callosum because it also links and 'coordinates' the two hemispheres; but it is a band of fibres in the middle of the brain, not a structure that controls movement.", "topic": "Cerebellum, the little brain", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A famous name associated with Existential therapy is:", "options": {"a": "John Dewey.", "b": "Alfred Adler.", "c": "Irvin Yalom.", "d": "Marsha Linehan."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Irvin Yalom (1980) is the famous name associated with existential therapy. Existential therapy grows out of existential philosophy, from Soren Kierkegaard (1844) to Jean-Paul Sartre (1943). It holds that much anxiety comes not from childhood conflicts or faulty learning but from facing the basic conditions of being human. Irvin Yalom, an American psychiatrist at Stanford, set this out in Existential Psychotherapy (1980). He named four givens, or ultimate concerns: death (we will die), freedom (we are responsible for our choices and have no fixed structure to lean on), isolation (however close we are to others, we enter and leave life alone) and meaninglessness (life has no ready-made meaning, so we must create one). Symptoms are seen as defences against these concerns, and therapy helps the client face them honestly through a real, present relationship with the therapist. Other key names are Rollo May (1958), who brought existentialism into American psychology, and Viktor Frankl (1946), whose logotherapy centres on the will to meaning. Example: after a heart attack at 45, Arjun cannot sleep and works late every night; an existential therapist helps him see that his panic is about death and that he can now choose how to spend the years he has. Option c is right because Irvin Yalom wrote the standard textbook of existential psychotherapy and defined its four givens. Exam trap: Irvin Yalom is just as famous for group therapy, through The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy (1970) and its therapeutic factors, so students who know him only from groups may doubt him here; he belongs to both fields.", "topic": "Yalom and existential therapy", "fig": "Irvin Yalom"}, {"q": "The audible range of hearing for human beings is between 20 to ______ Hertz.", "options": {"a": "200.", "b": "150000.", "c": "100.", "d": "20000."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the human ear hears frequencies from about 20 to 20,000 hertz. A sound's frequency (cycles per second, measured in hertz, Hz) is heard as pitch, its amplitude (measured in decibels, dB) as loudness, and its mixture of frequencies as timbre. A healthy young ear responds to about 20 to 20,000 Hz and is most sensitive in the middle of this band, roughly 2,000 to 5,000 Hz, where many speech sounds lie. Sounds below 20 Hz are infrasound; sounds above 20,000 Hz are ultrasound, which dogs, bats and dolphins can hear. The upper limit falls with age as the hair cells at the base of the cochlea, which code high pitches, wear out; this age-related hearing loss is called presbycusis. Example: Meera, aged 18, can hear the high whine of an old television that her grandfather, aged 70, cannot hear. Option d is right because 20,000 Hz is the standard upper limit of human hearing. Exam trap: the options mix up scales. 100 and 200 Hz are low pitches well inside our range (a typical man's voice sits near 120 Hz), while 150,000 Hz lies deep in the ultrasound range used by bats and dolphins.", "topic": "Range of human hearing", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Conservation or the ability to recognise that, although the shape of objects may change, the objects still maintain or conserve other characteristics that enable them to be recognized as the same is found in ____ stage of Piaget's theory.", "options": {"a": "Sensorimotor stage.", "b": "Formal Operational stage.", "c": "Concrete Operational stage.", "d": "Sensorimotor stage."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: conservation appears in the concrete operational stage in the theory of Jean Piaget (1952). He described four stages: sensorimotor (birth to about 2), preoperational (about 2 to 7), concrete operational (about 7 to 11) and formal operational (from about 11 or 12). Conservation is the understanding that an amount stays the same when only its appearance changes. In the classic test, a child agrees that two identical glasses hold the same amount of water; then one is poured into a tall, thin glass. A preoperational child says the tall glass has more, because of centration (attending only to the height) and irreversibility (not mentally pouring the water back). A concrete operational child says it is the same and gives one of three reasons: identity (nothing was added or taken away), reversibility (pour it back and it will look the same) or compensation (it is taller but thinner). Conservation does not arrive all at once: number comes first, around 6 to 7, then liquid and mass, then weight around 9 to 10 and volume only near 11 to 12, a lag Jean Piaget called horizontal decalage. Example: five-year-old Kabir thinks a flattened clay ball has more clay; his eight-year-old sister Meera laughs and says it is the same clay, just spread out. Option c is right because conservation is the hallmark achievement of the concrete operational stage. Exam trap: examiners describe the failure of conservation (centration, being fooled by appearance) and ask for the stage; that is preoperational, not concrete operational. Key note: in the official booklet options a and d are printed identically ('Sensorimotor stage'); one was probably meant to be the preoperational stage. It does not change the answer; c stands.", "topic": "Piaget's conservation", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "AUDIT is a simple screening tool developed by ____ to assess harmful use, hazardous use and dependence of alcohol.", "options": {"a": "World Health Organization.", "b": "American Psychological Association.", "c": "National Institute on Drug Abuse.", "d": "Centre for Substance Abuse Treatment."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: AUDIT, the Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test, was developed by the World Health Organization. The World Health Organization (WHO) built it as a brief screen that primary care workers in any country could use, and published it in 1989 (manual by Thomas Babor and colleagues); the six-country validation study by John Saunders and colleagues appeared in 1993. It has 10 questions, each scored 0 to 4, giving a total of 0 to 40. Items 1 to 3 ask about consumption (how often, how much, how often six or more drinks at once), which signals hazardous use; items 4 to 6 ask about dependence (cannot stop once started, drinking in the morning); and items 7 to 10 ask about alcohol-related harm (guilt, blackouts, injuries, others' concern), which signals harmful use. A score of 8 or more suggests hazardous or harmful drinking, and 20 or more suggests possible dependence and calls for specialist assessment. A three-item version, AUDIT-C, uses only the consumption questions. Example: at a health camp, Arjun, a 35-year-old driver, scores 14 on the AUDIT; the counsellor gives him a brief intervention on cutting down rather than a dependence referral. Option a is right because AUDIT is a WHO instrument, designed for international primary care screening. Exam trap: the distractors are all real organisations that work on alcohol and drugs, and three are American, which makes them sound plausible. Other screens are also confused with it: CAGE (John Ewing, 1984) has four yes or no items, and the Michigan Alcoholism Screening Test (Melvin Selzer, 1971) was developed in the United States.", "topic": "AUDIT alcohol screening test", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The book called \"The Myth of Mental Illness\" was written by ______.", "options": {"a": "Carl Jung.", "b": "Thomas Szasz.", "c": "Karen Horney.", "d": "William James."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: The Myth of Mental Illness (1961) was written by Thomas Szasz. Thomas Szasz, a Hungarian-born American psychiatrist, first argued the case in a 1960 paper and then in this book. His claim was that 'mental illness' is a metaphor. A true disease, he said, is a bodily lesion that a pathologist can find; most psychiatric diagnoses are labels for 'problems in living', ways of behaving that society finds troubling. Calling them illnesses, he argued, lets psychiatry control people, justifies involuntary hospitalisation and the insanity defence, and takes away people's responsibility for their own choices. He wanted psychiatry to be a voluntary, contractual service between consenting adults. The book became a founding text of the anti-psychiatry movement of the 1960s, alongside the work of R. D. Laing (1960) and Michel Foucault (1961), though Thomas Szasz himself rejected that label. His view sits with the sociocultural and labelling critiques of the medical model, such as Thomas Scheff's labelling theory (1966). Example: where a psychiatrist diagnoses Riya's loud, unusual behaviour as an illness needing compulsory treatment, Thomas Szasz would call it a problem in living and insist that Riya herself decides whether to seek help. Option b is right because Thomas Szasz is the author of The Myth of Mental Illness. Exam trap: examiners place other famous critics from the same era beside him; R. D. Laing wrote The Divided Self (1960) and Michel Foucault wrote Madness and Civilization, so learn each critic with his own book.", "topic": "Szasz, The Myth of Mental Illness", "fig": "Thomas Szasz"}, {"q": "The book \"Madness and Civilization\" was written by ______.", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud.", "b": "Anna Freud.", "c": "Michel Foucault.", "d": "Ronald Laing."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Madness and Civilization was written by Michel Foucault (1961). Michel Foucault, a French philosopher and historian of ideas, published Folie et deraison: Histoire de la folie a l'age classique in 1961; an abridged English translation appeared as Madness and Civilization in 1965. He argued that madness is not a fixed natural fact but is defined differently in each era. In the Middle Ages the mad often wandered freely; in the 'Great Confinement' of the seventeenth century (the Hopital General opened in Paris in 1656) they were locked up together with the poor, the idle and criminals, because reason was now prized above all. Even the famous freeing of asylum patients by Philippe Pinel (1793) and the moral treatment of William Tuke's York Retreat (1796) were, for Michel Foucault, not simple kindness but a new kind of moral control, in which the patient was constantly watched, judged and made to feel guilty. The book fed the 1960s anti-psychiatry critique and Michel Foucault's later histories of power, The Birth of the Clinic (1963) and Discipline and Punish (1975). Option c is right because Michel Foucault is the author of Madness and Civilization. Exam trap: the word 'civilization' pulls students towards Sigmund Freud's Civilization and Its Discontents (1930), which is about the conflict between instinct and the demands of society, not about the history of madness. R. D. Laing, another anti-psychiatry figure, wrote The Divided Self (1960), not this book.", "topic": "Foucault, Madness and Civilization", "fig": "Michel Foucault"}, {"q": "According to Kohlberg's theory of moral development, Stage 6, or the stage characterised by ______, is the stage where an individual has developed their own set of moral guidelines which may or may not always fit the law.", "options": {"a": "Maintaining social order.", "b": "Individualism and Exchange.", "c": "Obedience and punishment orientation.", "d": "Universal Principles."}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: in the theory of Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), stage 6 is the stage of universal ethical principles. He studied moral reasoning by giving boys dilemmas, most famously the Heinz dilemma, in which a man must decide whether to steal a drug he cannot afford to save his dying wife. He scored the reasoning behind the answer, not the answer itself, and found three levels with two stages each. Preconventional: stage 1, obedience and punishment orientation (right is what avoids punishment); stage 2, individualism and exchange (right is what serves my interest, a fair deal). Conventional: stage 3, good interpersonal relationships (being a 'good boy' or 'good girl'); stage 4, maintaining social order (obey laws and do one's duty). Postconventional: stage 5, social contract and individual rights (laws are agreements that can be changed for the common good); stage 6, universal ethical principles (self-chosen principles of justice, equal rights and human dignity, followed even when they conflict with the law). Stage 6 reasoning proved so rare that Lawrence Kohlberg later treated it as a theoretical ideal rather than a common stage. Example: Arjun says Heinz should steal the drug because a human life outweighs any property right, and that Heinz should accept prison if it follows; he puts a universal principle above the law. Option d is right because only stage 6 describes a person whose own moral principles may override the law. Exam trap: stage 5 also says laws can be changed, so students confuse it with stage 6; at stage 5 the person still works within a democratic social contract, while at stage 6 conscience and universal principles come first.", "topic": "Kohlberg's stage 6", "fig": "Lawrence Kohlberg"}, {"q": "Amongst the Gestalt laws of perceptual organization, the law of ______ states that every stimulus pattern is seen in such a way that the resulting structure is as simple as possible.", "options": {"a": "Pragnanz.", "b": "Closure.", "c": "Continuity.", "d": "Proximity."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the law of Pragnanz says every stimulus pattern is seen in the way that makes the resulting structure as simple as possible. Pragnanz is German for conciseness or 'good form'. Max Wertheimer (1923) set out the Gestalt laws of perceptual organisation, and Kurt Koffka (1935) stated Pragnanz as the master principle: the perceptual field organises itself into the simplest, most regular and most stable figure that the stimulus allows. That is why it is also called the law of good figure or the law of simplicity. The other grouping laws can be seen as special cases of it: proximity (near items group together), similarity (alike items group together), closure (gaps are filled to make a complete shape) and continuity, or good continuation (we follow smooth lines rather than sharp turns). Example: Riya sees the Olympic logo as five overlapping circles, not as a jumble of curved fragments and odd shapes, because five circles is the simplest reading of the lines. In the same way, a triangle laid over part of a square is seen as two simple shapes, not as one complicated outline. Option a is right because the stem gives the textbook definition of Pragnanz: the simplest possible structure. Exam trap: closure also produces a 'good', complete figure, so it is the near miss; but closure is narrowly about filling gaps in an incomplete figure, while Pragnanz is the general tendency towards simplicity that covers every pattern.", "topic": "Gestalt law of Pragnanz", "fig": "Max Wertheimer"}, {"q": "The concept of the 'animus' archetype, given by Carl Jung, refers to:", "options": {"a": "The masculine side of a female psyche.", "b": "The repressed animalistic part of the mind.", "c": "The feminine side of a male psyche.", "d": "The repressed unpleasant thoughts and memories."}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: in the theory of Carl Jung (1928), the animus is the masculine side of a woman's psyche. Carl Jung divided the unconscious into a personal unconscious, made of forgotten and repressed experiences organised into complexes, and a collective unconscious, inherited by all humans and filled with archetypes: universal patterns or images shaped by the shared experience of our ancestors. Four archetypes are developed most fully. The persona is the social mask. The shadow is the dark, primitive, animal side that we disown. The self is the archetype of wholeness, symbolised by the mandala. The anima and animus carry the other sex within each person: the anima is the feminine side of a man, and the animus is the masculine side of a woman. They come from humanity's long experience of the other sex, and they help explain attraction, since we project our anima or animus onto a partner who seems to match the inner image. Individuation, the lifelong move towards wholeness, needs these sides to be accepted rather than denied. Example: Meera, a gentle and accommodating teacher, discovers a firm, logical and assertive voice when she leads a staff meeting; a Jungian would call this her animus coming into awareness. Option a is right because animus is the term for the masculine element in the female psyche. Exam trap: the pair is easy to reverse (option c describes the anima), and 'animus' sounds like 'animal', which pulls students to option b, a description of the shadow. Memory aid: Latin -us endings are masculine (animus), and -a endings are feminine (anima).", "topic": "Jung's anima and animus", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "The concept of paranoid schizoid position was given by:", "options": {"a": "Melanie Klein.", "b": "Karen Horney.", "c": "Sigmund Freud.", "d": "Carl Jung."}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: the paranoid-schizoid position was described by Melanie Klein (1946). Melanie Klein was an Austrian-British psychoanalyst who analysed very young children through play and helped found object relations theory. She argued that from birth the infant relates to 'objects', above all the mother's breast, and is driven by powerful love and hate. In the paranoid-schizoid position, the first three or four months of life, the infant cannot bear that the same breast both feeds and frustrates. So it splits the object into a 'good breast' that is loved and idealised and a 'bad breast' that is hated and feared. Its own aggression is projected outwards, so the bad object seems to attack it; the main fear is persecutory anxiety (the paranoid part), and the main defence is splitting self and objects apart (the schizoid part). Projective identification, pushing one's feelings into another person, belongs here too. From around four to six months the infant enters the depressive position (Melanie Klein, 1935): it sees the mother as one whole person, both good and bad, feels guilt for its attacks and tries to make reparation. She called these 'positions', not stages, because people move back and forth between them throughout life. Example: Kabir, an adult client, sees his therapist as perfect one week and as cruel and rejecting the next; a Kleinian would say he is functioning in the paranoid-schizoid position. Option a is right because the term and the theory are Melanie Klein's. Exam trap: the clinical words 'paranoid' and 'schizoid' pull students towards Sigmund Freud's Schreber case on paranoia (1911) or Carl Jung's The Psychology of Dementia Praecox (1907), but the term describes normal infant development and belongs to Melanie Klein.", "topic": "Klein's paranoid-schizoid position", "fig": "Melanie Klein"}, {"q": "The 'Direct Perception' theory of perception was given by:", "options": {"a": "Endel Tulving.", "b": "Jerome Bruner.", "c": "J. J. Gibson.", "d": "Gregory Bateson."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the direct perception theory was given by James J. Gibson (1966, 1979). James J. Gibson, an American psychologist, drew on his wartime work on how pilots judge distance when landing. He rejected the classic view of Hermann von Helmholtz (1867) and later Richard Gregory (1970) that perception is an inference, built up when the brain adds knowledge and expectation to poor sensory data (a top-down, constructivist view). For James J. Gibson the light reaching a moving observer, the optic array, is already rich enough to specify the world, so perception is a direct 'pick-up' of information with no inference needed (a bottom-up view). Key ideas are optic flow (as you move forward, the scene streams outwards from the point you are heading towards), texture gradients (surface texture looks finer with distance), invariants (properties that stay constant as you move) and affordances (what an object offers for action: a chair affords sitting, a handle affords pulling). Because he stressed the animal acting in its natural environment, the approach is called ecological, set out in The Ecological Approach to Visual Perception (1979). Example: Kabir runs to catch a high cricket ball without calculating anything; he simply moves so that the ball's image keeps rising steadily in his view, using information directly available in the light. Option c is right because direct, ecological perception is James J. Gibson's theory. Exam trap: his wife, Eleanor Gibson, is famous for the visual cliff (with Richard Walk, 1960), so a 'Gibson' question about depth perception in infants means her, not him.", "topic": "Gibson's direct perception", "fig": "James J. Gibson"}, {"q": "The theme for World Mental Health Day observed in October 2022 was:", "options": {"a": "Make mental health for all a global priority.", "b": "Psychological First Aid.", "c": "Young people and mental health in a changing world.", "d": "Dignity in Mental Health."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the theme for World Mental Health Day 2022 was 'Make mental health and well-being for all a global priority'. World Mental Health Day falls on 10 October every year. It was started in 1992 by the World Federation for Mental Health (WFMH), a global body of professionals, service users and advocates, and it is supported by the World Health Organization; each year a theme focuses world attention on one issue. The 2022 theme came in the wake of the COVID-19 pandemic, which the World Health Organization estimated raised the rates of anxiety and depression worldwide by about a quarter in its first year, while many services were disrupted. The campaign asked governments to treat mental health as a priority within health systems, with more investment and wider access to care. Recent themes are worth learning as a dated list: 2015, dignity in mental health; 2016, psychological first aid; 2018, young people and mental health in a changing world; 2019, suicide prevention; 2021, mental health in an unequal world; 2022, make mental health and well-being for all a global priority; 2023, mental health is a universal human right. Option a is right because it matches the 2022 theme (the option simply drops the words 'and well-being'). Exam trap: every distractor is a real World Mental Health Day theme from an earlier year, so a student who has seen them before recognises them and picks one; the year has to be learnt with each theme.", "topic": "World Mental Health Day 2022", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The concept of 'borderline personality organization' was given by:", "options": {"a": "Heinz Kohut.", "b": "Margaret Mahler.", "c": "Carl Jung.", "d": "Otto Kernberg."}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: the concept of borderline personality organisation was given by Otto Kernberg (1967, 1975). Otto Kernberg, an Austrian-born American psychoanalyst, combined Melanie Klein's (1946) object relations theory with ego psychology. He described three levels of personality organisation, judged on identity, level of defences and reality testing. Neurotic organisation has an integrated identity, higher-level defences such as repression, and intact reality testing. Borderline organisation has identity diffusion (an unstable, contradictory sense of self and others), primitive defences centred on splitting (with projective identification, idealisation, devaluation, denial and omnipotence), and reality testing that is mostly intact but can slip under stress. Psychotic organisation has identity diffusion, primitive defences and a loss of reality testing. Borderline organisation is wider than the DSM diagnosis of borderline personality disorder: it also takes in narcissistic, antisocial, paranoid and schizoid personalities. Otto Kernberg traced it to intense early aggression that keeps 'all good' and 'all bad' images of self and others apart, and later developed transference-focused psychotherapy to treat it. Example: Meera praises her new manager as the best person she has ever worked with, then, after one critical remark, calls her cruel and worthless, yet she stays fully in touch with reality; this is splitting with intact reality testing, the borderline level. Option d is right because borderline personality organisation is Otto Kernberg's structural model. Exam trap: Heinz Kohut (1971) and Otto Kernberg both wrote on narcissism in the 1970s and famously disagreed, and Margaret Mahler's (1975) rapprochement crisis is often linked to the origins of borderline problems, so all three names look plausible; the structural term itself is Otto Kernberg's alone.", "topic": "Kernberg's borderline organisation", "fig": "Otto Kernberg"}, {"q": "Humour, anticipation, sublimation and ________ are all considered as mature defense mechanisms.", "options": {"a": "Somatization.", "b": "Projection.", "c": "Acting Out.", "d": "Altruism."}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: altruism, with humour, anticipation, sublimation and suppression, is a mature defence. George Vaillant (1977) ranked defence mechanisms by maturity using the Grant Study, which followed a group of Harvard men from the late 1930s through adult life. In Adaptation to Life he grouped defences into four levels. Psychotic defences (delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion) bend reality itself. Immature defences (projection, acting out, passive aggression, hypochondriasis, schizoid fantasy) are common in adolescents and in personality disorders, and they push distress onto other people or into the body. Neurotic defences (repression, displacement, reaction formation, intellectualisation, dissociation) are everyday ways of keeping feelings out of awareness. Mature defences (altruism, anticipation, humour, sublimation, suppression) keep the feeling in awareness but channel it usefully. The men who used mature defences had better physical health, careers and marriages decades later. Altruism means coping with one's own distress by serving others in ways that bring real satisfaction. A handy mnemonic for the mature group is SASHA: suppression, altruism, sublimation, humour, anticipation. Example: after his father dies of cancer, Kabir starts volunteering at a cancer helpline; helping other families eases his own grief. Option d is right because altruism is one of the five mature defences named by George Vaillant. Exam trap: all three distractors are immature defences that can sound active or harmless: somatisation turns emotional pain into bodily complaints, projection puts one's own unacceptable feelings onto others, and acting out discharges an impulse in action without reflection.", "topic": "Vaillant's mature defences", "fig": "George Vaillant"}, {"q": "The classification of temperament types into difficult, easy and slow to warm up was given by ________:", "options": {"a": "Alfred Adler.", "b": "Carl Jung.", "c": "Thomas & Chess.", "d": "James Lange."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up temperament types were described by Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (1977). Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess, a husband-and-wife pair of psychiatrists in New York, began the New York Longitudinal Study in 1956 with Herbert Birch, following children from early infancy into adulthood. Temperament means stable, early-appearing differences in emotional and behavioural style. From parent interviews they rated nine dimensions: activity level, rhythmicity (regularity of sleep, hunger and toileting), approach or withdrawal from new things, adaptability, threshold of responsiveness, intensity of reaction, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span and persistence. Combinations of these gave three types, described in Temperament and Development (1977). Easy children (about 40 percent) are regular, adapt quickly and are mostly cheerful. Difficult children (about 10 percent) are irregular, withdraw from new things, adapt slowly and react intensely and negatively. Slow-to-warm-up children (about 15 percent) are mildly negative and slow to adapt at first but warm up with repeated exposure. About 35 percent did not fit any type. Their key idea was goodness of fit: outcomes depend on how well the child's temperament matches the demands and patience of parents and setting. Example: on her first day at nursery, Riya clings to her mother and watches silently, but by the second week she is playing happily; she is slow to warm up. Option c is right because the three-type classification comes from Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess. Exam trap: 'James Lange' is not a person. It garbles the James-Lange theory of emotion, named after two people, William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885), who held that bodily arousal comes before the felt emotion.", "topic": "Thomas and Chess temperament types", "fig": "Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess"}, {"q": "Machiavellianism is a term generally used to denote people who:", "options": {"a": "Believe males should rule the society instead of women.", "b": "Express their power motivation by manipulating and exploiting others in a deceptive and exploitative fashion.", "c": "Are extremely benevolent and always ready to help.", "d": "Believe in the synthesis of thesis and antithesis."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Machiavellianism describes people who express their power motivation by manipulating and exploiting others in a deceptive way. The term comes from Niccolo Machiavelli (1513), the Florentine author of The Prince, who advised rulers that cunning, deceit and fear are acceptable when they keep power: the end justifies the means. Richard Christie and Florence Geis (1970) turned this into a personality trait. They built the Mach IV scale from statements adapted from The Prince, such as 'The best way to handle people is to tell them what they want to hear'. High scorers ('high Machs') are cool, cynical about human nature, emotionally detached and willing to use flattery, lies and strategy to get ahead; in laboratory bargaining games they tend to win more and are less swayed by emotion. Low scorers are more trusting and empathic. Delroy Paulhus and Kevin Williams (2002) placed Machiavellianism in the Dark Triad, three overlapping, socially aversive but subclinical traits, with narcissism and psychopathy; all three share low agreeableness and low honesty-humility. Example: Kabir praises his team leader in meetings, quietly takes credit for a colleague's report and leaks a rival's mistake to the manager, all while seeming friendly; this calculated manipulation is Machiavellian. Option b is right because manipulation and deceptive exploitation in pursuit of power are the defining features of the trait. Exam trap: students mix up the Dark Triad traits. Machiavellianism is cold, planned, long-term manipulation; psychopathy adds impulsivity, thrill seeking and lack of remorse; narcissism centres on grandiosity and the need for admiration.", "topic": "Machiavellianism", "fig": "Richard Christie and Florence Geis"}, {"q": "ERG theory of motivation was given by:", "options": {"a": "Alderfer.", "b": "Maslow.", "c": "Herzberg.", "d": "McClelland."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: ERG theory of motivation was given by Clayton Alderfer (1969). Clayton Alderfer, an American organisational psychologist, reworked Abraham Maslow's (1943) five-level hierarchy and tested it on employees in organisations. He reduced the five needs to three. Existence needs are material and physical (food, water, pay, safe working conditions), matching Abraham Maslow's physiological and safety needs. Relatedness needs are for meaningful relationships with family, friends, colleagues and supervisors, matching belongingness and the social part of esteem. Growth needs are for personal development and full use of one's abilities, matching self-esteem and self-actualisation. ERG theory differs from Abraham Maslow's in three ways. First, the needs are not a rigid ladder, and several can be active at once. Second, a lower need does not have to be satisfied before a higher one can motivate. Third, it adds frustration-regression: when a higher need is blocked, the person falls back and seeks more of a lower need, whereas Abraham Maslow described only satisfaction-progression, moving up once a need is met. Example: Arjun is passed over for promotion and for the chance to lead a project (growth frustrated); instead of pushing for development, he starts demanding a pay rise and spending more time chatting with colleagues (existence and relatedness), which is frustration-regression. Option a is right because Clayton Alderfer proposed the existence, relatedness and growth model. Exam trap: all four names are need theorists of motivation, and David McClelland (1961) also had three needs (achievement, affiliation and power), so 'three needs' alone can pull students away from Clayton Alderfer; Frederick Herzberg (1959) had two factors and Abraham Maslow five levels.", "topic": "Alderfer's ERG theory", "fig": "Clayton Alderfer"}, {"q": "Sundowning, or Sundown Syndrome, is a phenomenon commonly associated with:", "options": {"a": "Alcohol dependence.", "b": "Schizophrenia.", "c": "Insomnia.", "d": "Alzheimer's disease."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: sundowning is most commonly associated with Alzheimer's disease and other dementias. Sundowning, or sundown syndrome, is a pattern in which confusion, restlessness, agitation, anxiety, pacing, wandering and sometimes shouting or aggression increase in the late afternoon, evening and night. It is seen most often in the middle and later stages of Alzheimer's disease, the commonest cause of dementia, and in other dementias; the same evening worsening is also typical of delirium. Several causes probably combine. Damage to the brain's body clock (the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus) disrupts circadian rhythms and the sleep-wake cycle. Fading light and shadows make the surroundings harder to interpret, fatigue builds through the day, and there are fewer orienting cues such as visitors and routines. Hunger, pain, a full bladder, infections and some medicines can add to it. Management is mostly practical: bright light and activity in the daytime, a fixed routine, less caffeine and fewer daytime naps, well-lit rooms in the evening, a calm setting and checks for pain or infection. Example: Meera's 78-year-old grandmother, who has Alzheimer's disease, is calm and chatty in the morning, but as it grows dark she insists she must go home to her parents and tries to leave the house. Option d is right because sundowning is a recognised feature of Alzheimer's disease and related dementias. Exam trap: because sundowning disturbs the night, students may pick insomnia; but insomnia is a sleep disorder of trouble falling or staying asleep, while sundowning is an evening worsening of confusion and behaviour in a damaged brain.", "topic": "Sundowning in dementia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term 'animal magnetism' was coined by:", "options": {"a": "Franz Anton Mesmer.", "b": "Eric Berne.", "c": "Otto Kernberg.", "d": "Alfred Adler."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the term 'animal magnetism' was coined by Franz Anton Mesmer. Franz Anton Mesmer (1734 to 1815) was a German physician who practised in Vienna and then, from 1778, in Paris. In the 1770s he argued that an invisible universal fluid flows through all living bodies and that illness comes from a blockage or imbalance of it. He called this force animal magnetism, 'animal' meaning belonging to living beings. He treated patients by passing his hands or magnets over them, or by seating groups around a tub of 'magnetised' water, the baquet, until they fell into a dramatic convulsive 'crisis' that was meant to restore the flow. A French royal commission (1784) that included Benjamin Franklin and Antoine Lavoisier ran blindfold trials and concluded that the effects came from imagination, not from any fluid. Even so, Mesmer had shown that suggestion and expectation can change bodily states, and his methods became the root of hypnosis. The Scottish surgeon James Braid (1843) later dropped the fluid idea and coined the word 'hypnotism'. The English verb 'mesmerise' keeps Mesmer's name alive. Option a is right because both the phrase and the theory of animal magnetism are Mesmer's own. Exam trap: examiners like to swap Mesmer with James Braid, who named hypnotism, or with Jean-Martin Charcot (1880s), who used hypnosis to study hysteria; remember the chain: Mesmer gave animal magnetism, Braid gave hypnotism, Charcot tied it to hysteria.", "topic": "Mesmer and animal magnetism", "fig": "Franz Anton Mesmer"}, {"q": "The virtue that develops in the 'generativity versus stagnation' stage of Erikson is referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Wisdom.", "b": "Purpose.", "c": "Care.", "d": "Fidelity."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: the virtue of the generativity versus stagnation stage is care. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, described eight psychosocial stages across the whole lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis between two poles, and resolving it well leaves the person with a lasting strength, which Erikson later called a basic virtue. In order the virtues are hope (trust versus mistrust, infancy), will (autonomy versus shame and doubt, toddlerhood), purpose (initiative versus guilt, about 3 to 6 years), competence (industry versus inferiority, school age), fidelity (identity versus role confusion, adolescence), love (intimacy versus isolation, young adulthood), care (generativity versus stagnation, middle adulthood, about 40 to 65) and wisdom (ego integrity versus despair, old age). Generativity means concern for establishing and guiding the next generation: raising children, mentoring juniors, teaching, or building work and institutions that will outlast oneself. Parenthood is neither necessary nor sufficient. When this fails, the person stagnates, feeling bored, self-absorbed and that they have little to give. The virtue that grows out of success is care, a widening commitment to look after the people, products and ideas one has helped to create. Example: Meera, aged 48, starts training the junior nurses on her ward and runs a weekend reading club for children in her colony; she is resolving the generativity crisis and showing care. Option c is right because care is the strength Erikson attached to stage 7. Exam trap: examiners offer the neighbouring virtues, and wisdom is the favourite decoy; it belongs to the final stage (integrity versus despair), so students who think 'middle-aged adult equals wise adult' slip one stage too far.", "topic": "Erikson's virtues: generativity and care", "fig": "Erik Erikson"}, {"q": "The two-factor theory of emotion was proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Cannon-Bard.", "b": "James-Lange.", "c": "Schachter-Singer.", "d": "Richard Lazarus."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the two-factor theory of emotion was proposed by Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer. Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) argued that an emotion needs two ingredients. The first is general physiological arousal (a racing heart, sweating, trembling), which sets how strong the feeling is. The second is a cognitive label, a judgement drawn from the situation about why one is aroused, which decides which emotion it is. Arousal on its own is too similar across emotions to tell anger from joy; the label does that work. In their classic study, students were injected with epinephrine (adrenaline), described as a vitamin called Suproxin. Some were told its true side effects, some were told nothing and some were misinformed; a placebo group got saline. Each then waited with a stooge who acted either euphoric or angry. Those who were aroused but had no explanation for it caught the stooge's mood most, while the informed group, who could blame the injection, did so least. Later replications were mixed, but the idea of misattributed arousal survived, for example in the suspension-bridge study of Donald Dutton and Arthur Aron (1974). Example: Arjun has just run up four flights of stairs and meets a classmate who teases him; still panting, he feels unusually angry, because he reads his leftover arousal as anger. Option c is right because 'two-factor theory' is the standard name for the Schachter and Singer arousal-plus-label account. Exam trap: 'two-factor' is also the name of Frederick Herzberg's (1959) work motivation theory, O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) avoidance learning theory and Charles Spearman's (1904) intelligence theory, so check what the two factors are.", "topic": "Schachter-Singer two-factor theory", "fig": "Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer"}, {"q": "The transtheoretical model of change was given by:", "options": {"a": "Prochaska & DiClemente.", "b": "Dollard & Miller.", "c": "Abraham Maslow.", "d": "Carl Jung."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the transtheoretical model of change was given by James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente. James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente (1983) studied how smokers quit, both on their own and in treatment, and found that people move through a predictable series of stages rather than changing in one leap. The stages are precontemplation (no intention to change in the next six months, often not seeing a problem), contemplation (intending to change but ambivalent), preparation (planning to act within a month, with small steps taken), action (behaviour changed within the last six months) and maintenance (change kept for more than six months); termination, when temptation has gone, was added later. Relapse is common, and people often cycle back through the stages before change lasts. The model is called transtheoretical because it draws its processes of change (consciousness raising, self-liberation, stimulus control and others) from many schools of therapy instead of one. Its practical message is to match the help to the stage: information and gentle questioning for precontemplators, help with the decision for contemplators, concrete plans for those in preparation. Example: Kabir smokes ten cigarettes a day; this month he says he 'might cut down someday' (contemplation), and next month he buys nicotine gum and fixes a quit date (preparation). Option a is right because Prochaska and DiClemente are the authors of the stages-of-change model. Exam trap: the model is usually taught alongside motivational interviewing, so examiners may offer William Miller and Stephen Rollnick (1991) as its authors; and the 'Miller' in Dollard and Miller is Neal Miller, a different person altogether.", "topic": "Transtheoretical stages of change", "fig": "James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente"}, {"q": "The Psychasthenia scale (Scale 7) in the MMPI-2 measures:", "options": {"a": "Masculine and feminine interests.", "b": "Antisocial behaviour.", "c": "Extreme somatic complaints.", "d": "Obsessive-compulsive symptoms and difficulty concentrating."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Psychasthenia scale (Scale 7) measures obsessive-compulsive symptoms and difficulty concentrating. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built the MMPI by empirical criterion keying: an item was kept only if a diagnosed patient group answered it differently from normal people. Scale 7 was keyed on patients then called psychasthenic. The French psychiatrist Pierre Janet (1903) had used 'psychasthenia' for a weakness of mental energy showing as obsessions, compulsions, phobias, doubts and excessive anxiety. Today most of these patients would be called anxious or obsessive-compulsive. High scorers on Scale 7 describe chronic worry, tension, self-doubt, perfectionism, guilt, intrusive thoughts and rituals, and trouble concentrating because their minds keep returning to their worries. The scale is the MMPI-2's best general marker of anxious distress, and it often rises with Scale 2 (Depression) in the 2-7 code type, an anxious, ruminating depression. Example: Riya, a final-year student, checks her door lock five times before leaving, cannot stop worrying about small mistakes and rereads the same page because her focus keeps slipping; her MMPI-2 profile shows Scale 7 at T 72, above the cut-off of 65. Option d is right because obsessions, compulsions, worry and poor concentration are exactly what Scale 7 picks up. Exam trap: the old scale names are diagnostic labels from the 1930s, and examiners rely on students not knowing what 'psychasthenia' meant; it is the anxiety and obsession scale, not a scale of physical weakness or fatigue, which pulls students towards the somatic option.", "topic": "MMPI-2 Scale 7 Psychasthenia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The type of parenting associated with positive outcomes, where parents are both very warm and yet careful to set clear limits and restrictions, is known as:", "options": {"a": "Authoritative parenting.", "b": "Neglectful-uninvolved parenting.", "c": "Permissive-indulgent parenting.", "d": "Authoritarian parenting."}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: warm parents who also set clear limits practise authoritative parenting. Diana Baumrind (1966, 1971) observed preschool children at home and in nursery school and interviewed their parents. She described three styles that differ in how much parents demand and how warmly they respond. Authoritarian parents are strict and cold: rules are fixed, obedience is expected 'because I said so', and punishment is common. Permissive parents are warm but make few demands and leave the child to regulate itself. Authoritative parents combine high warmth with high control: they set firm, consistent limits, but explain their reasons, listen to the child's view and encourage growing independence. Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) recast the styles as two dimensions, demandingness and responsiveness, and split permissiveness into permissive-indulgent (warm, few rules) and neglectful-uninvolved (neither warm nor controlling). Across many studies, children of authoritative parents tend to be the most self-reliant, socially skilled, self-controlled and successful at school, while neglect is linked to the poorest outcomes. Some cross-cultural work suggests that strictness carries different meanings in some families, but the advantage of the authoritative style holds broadly. Example: Meera wants to stay out until midnight; her mother sets 10 o'clock as the limit, explains that the bus route is unsafe late at night, and agrees to 10:30 on weekends if Meera keeps her word. Option a is right because the stem describes exactly this high-warmth, high-control combination. Exam trap: 'authoritative' and 'authoritarian' look almost identical; authoritarian means control without warmth, authoritative means control with warmth and reasons.", "topic": "Baumrind's authoritative parenting", "fig": "Diana Baumrind"}, {"q": "Autokinetic effect and stroboscopic motion are examples of:", "options": {"a": "Space perception.", "b": "Apparent motion.", "c": "Time perception.", "d": "Real motion."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the autokinetic effect and stroboscopic motion are examples of apparent motion. Apparent motion is the perception of movement when nothing in the scene is actually moving in that way. Real motion, by contrast, is an object's image sweeping across the retina, signalled by direction-selective neurons concentrated in area MT (V5) of the visual cortex. In the autokinetic effect, a small, stationary point of light in a completely dark room seems to drift and wander after a few seconds of staring. With no frame of reference to anchor it, tiny eye movements are read as movement of the light. Muzafer Sherif (1935) used the effect to study how group norms form: people judging the light together came to agree on how far it moved. Stroboscopic motion is the illusion of smooth movement from a rapid series of still images, as in films shown at about 24 frames a second. It is closely related to the phi phenomenon of Max Wertheimer (1912), in which two lights flashed one after the other look like a single light jumping between them, the finding that launched Gestalt psychology. Other forms of apparent motion include induced motion (the moon seems to race through the clouds) and the motion aftereffect (the waterfall illusion). Example: in a dark hostel room, Arjun stares at the glowing dot of a mosquito repellent and is sure it is creeping sideways (autokinetic); at the cinema he sees continuous action built from still frames (stroboscopic). Option b is right because in both cases movement is seen where none exists. Exam trap: 'real motion' tempts because the observer genuinely sees movement, but the stimulus itself is still.", "topic": "Apparent motion: autokinetic and stroboscopic", "fig": "Max Wertheimer"}, {"q": "Under the Severe Personality Pathology category of the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory (MCMI-III), the following scale is counted:", "options": {"a": "Scale 2A (Avoidant).", "b": "Scale 3 (Dependent).", "c": "Scale C (Borderline).", "d": "Scale A (Anxiety)."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: Scale C (Borderline) is counted under Severe Personality Pathology on the MCMI-III. Theodore Millon (1994) built the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory-III, a 175-item true-false questionnaire for adults in clinical settings, from his own theory of personality and from DSM-IV. Its clinical scales fall into four groups. The 11 Clinical Personality Patterns are the basic styles: 1 Schizoid, 2A Avoidant, 2B Depressive, 3 Dependent, 4 Histrionic, 5 Narcissistic, 6A Antisocial, 6B Sadistic (Aggressive), 7 Compulsive, 8A Negativistic (Passive-Aggressive) and 8B Masochistic (Self-Defeating). The 3 Severe Personality Pathology scales are S Schizotypal, C Borderline and P Paranoid. Millon saw these as more structurally damaged versions of the basic patterns, with weaker inner cohesion and a greater risk of breaking down under stress. Then come 7 Clinical Syndromes (A Anxiety, H Somatoform, N Bipolar: Manic, D Dysthymia, B Alcohol Dependence, T Drug Dependence, R Post-Traumatic Stress) and 3 Severe Clinical Syndromes (SS Thought Disorder, CC Major Depression, PP Delusional Disorder). Scores are base rate scores, anchored to how common each condition is among patients; BR 75 suggests a trait is present and BR 85 that it is prominent. Example: Kabir, an inpatient with stormy relationships, self-harm and chronic emptiness, scores BR 90 on Scale C. Option c is right because Borderline is one of the three severe personality pathology scales. Exam trap: the single letters S, C and P are the severe personality scales, while the doubled letters SS, CC and PP are the severe clinical syndromes; Scale C is Borderline, but Scale CC is Major Depression.", "topic": "MCMI-III severe personality scales", "fig": "Theodore Millon"}, {"q": "The Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory (MCMI-III) consists of ______ items.", "options": {"a": "175.", "b": "250.", "c": "108.", "d": "567."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the MCMI-III consists of 175 items. Theodore Millon (1994) designed the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory-III as a short self-report test for adults seen in mental health settings. All 175 items are answered true or false, and most people finish in about 25 to 30 minutes, far quicker than the MMPI-2. The items feed 14 personality pattern scales, 10 clinical syndrome scales, 3 modifying indices (Disclosure, Desirability, Debasement) and a Validity index. Many items are scored on more than one scale, which is how so few items can cover so many scales. The count was 175 in the first MCMI (1977) and stayed the same in the MCMI-II (1987) and the MCMI-III; only the MCMI-IV (2015) grew, to 195 items. Example: Arjun, a clinical psychology trainee, chooses the MCMI-III for a tired inpatient because its 175 items take about half an hour. Option a is right because 175 is the MCMI-III item count. Exam trap: the MMPI-2 has 567 items and the MMPI-2-RF has 338, so examiners place 567 among the options to catch students who mix up the two inventories.", "topic": "MCMI-III item count", "fig": "Theodore Millon"}, {"q": "The condition in which one fabricates symptoms of illness in oneself, gets sick on purpose and makes frequent visits to hospitals is called:", "options": {"a": "Munchausen's Syndrome.", "b": "Munchausen's Syndrome by proxy.", "c": "Capgras Syndrome.", "d": "Fregoli Syndrome."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: deliberately fabricating or inducing illness in oneself, with repeated hospital visits, is Munchausen syndrome. Richard Asher (1951) gave the name to patients who invented dramatic medical histories, travelled from hospital to hospital and submitted to tests and even operations. He borrowed it from Baron Munchausen, an eighteenth-century German nobleman famous for wildly exaggerated tall tales. In DSM-5 the condition is factitious disorder imposed on self, and Munchausen syndrome is its most severe, chronic form. The person falsifies signs or symptoms, or causes real injury or disease (for example by taking blood thinners, contaminating a wound or tampering with urine samples), and presents as ill. The motive is internal: to take on the sick role and receive care and attention. There is no obvious external reward, which separates it from malingering, where symptoms are faked for money, drugs, or escape from work or the law. Example: Riya, a 30-year-old laboratory technician, secretly injects herself with insulin to bring on low blood sugar, then turns up at different emergency departments with vague, shifting stories and asks for more tests. Option a is right because the stem describes a person producing illness in themselves, the defining feature of Munchausen syndrome. Exam trap: 'by proxy' (option b) flips the target, since there the illness is produced in someone else, usually a child; examiners also mix factitious disorder with malingering, which needs an external incentive.", "topic": "Munchausen syndrome (factitious disorder)", "fig": "Richard Asher"}, {"q": "The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory has ______ clinical scales apart from the validity scales:", "options": {"a": "15.", "b": "10.", "c": "9.", "d": "8."}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the MMPI has 10 clinical scales besides its validity scales. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota built the inventory by empirical criterion keying: an item stayed on a scale only if a diagnosed patient group answered it differently from normal people, mostly visitors and relatives at the hospital. The ten clinical scales, numbered 1 to 9 and 0, are 1 Hs (Hypochondriasis), 2 D (Depression), 3 Hy (Hysteria), 4 Pd (Psychopathic Deviate), 5 Mf (Masculinity-Femininity), 6 Pa (Paranoia), 7 Pt (Psychasthenia), 8 Sc (Schizophrenia), 9 Ma (Hypomania) and 0 Si (Social Introversion). The original validity scales are L (Lie), F (Infrequency), K (Correction) and the Cannot Say count; the MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) kept the same ten clinical scales and added validity scales such as VRIN and TRIN. Clinicians read the profile as a code type, the two highest clinical scales. Example: Kabir's MMPI-2 profile shows scales 2 and 7 highest, a 2-7 code type, suggesting an anxious, worried depression. Option b is right because the standard profile carries exactly ten clinical scales. Exam trap: the scales are numbered 1 to 9 plus 0, so students who count the digits 1 to 9 answer nine; the MMPI-2-RF also has nine Restructured Clinical scales, and the MMPI-2 has 15 content scales, which is where options c and a come from.", "topic": "MMPI clinical scales", "fig": "Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley"}, {"q": "The concept of 'psychopathic inferiority' was introduced by:", "options": {"a": "Julius Koch.", "b": "Karl Schneider.", "c": "Leon Festinger.", "d": "James Lange."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the concept of 'psychopathic inferiority' was introduced by Julius Koch. Julius Ludwig August Koch (1891), a German psychiatrist, wrote Die psychopathischen Minderwertigkeiten ('The Psychopathic Inferiorities'). He used the term for lasting abnormalities of character that fell short of insanity: people whose intellect and grip on reality were intact, but whose emotions, will or conduct were persistently abnormal. He thought these inferiorities were constitutional, rooted in an inborn or acquired weakness of the nervous system. His category was broad, covering anxious, odd and unstable people as well as antisocial ones, and it was meant to replace the older, morally loaded label 'moral insanity'. The history runs in a clear line. Philippe Pinel (1801) described manie sans délire, mania without delirium, in patients with impulsive rages but intact reasoning. James Cowles Prichard (1835) named moral insanity, a disorder of feelings and conduct. Koch then gave the word 'psychopathic' its personality meaning. Kurt Schneider (1923), in Psychopathic Personalities, described ten types of abnormal personality, and Hervey Cleckley (1941), in The Mask of Sanity, narrowed psychopathy to the charming, remorseless type that Robert D. Hare's Psychopathy Checklist-Revised (1991) measures today. Option a is right because 'psychopathic inferiority' is Koch's own phrase and his step in this lineage. Exam trap: 'Karl Schneider' blends Kurt Schneider, who wrote the 1923 book on psychopathic personalities, with Carl Schneider, a different German psychiatrist, so the name looks right but belongs to nobody in this history; and Schneider came about thirty years after Koch.", "topic": "History of psychopathy: Koch", "fig": "Julius Koch"}, {"q": "The concept of psychodrama was created by ______ in the 1930s.", "options": {"a": "Karl Schneider.", "b": "Jacob L. Moreno.", "c": "Albert Bandura.", "d": "Abraham Maslow."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychodrama was created by Jacob L. Moreno. Jacob Levy Moreno (1889 to 1974), a Romanian-born psychiatrist working in Vienna, noticed how much people revealed and healed when they acted out their lives instead of only talking about them. In psychodrama a client, the protagonist, enacts scenes from the past, present or a feared future on a stage. The therapist acts as director, other group members serve as auxiliary egos who play important people in the protagonist's life, and the rest form an audience. A session moves through a warm-up, the action and a sharing phase in which members say what the drama stirred in them. Key techniques are role reversal (the protagonist plays the other person), doubling (an auxiliary voices the protagonist's unspoken feelings) and mirroring (the protagonist watches someone replay their behaviour). The aim is catharsis plus new, spontaneous ways of acting. Moreno also founded sociometry (Who Shall Survive?, 1934). Example: Meera cannot tell her father she wants to change her college course; in a psychodrama group she rehearses the talk, then reverses roles and, speaking as her father, discovers his fear for her future. Option b is right because Moreno invented psychodrama and built its stage, roles and methods. Exam trap: Moreno is known for both psychodrama and sociometry, and examiners swap the two; the empty chair of Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls, 1951) grew from psychodrama but is a different method. Key note: Jacob L. Moreno ran his spontaneity theatre in Vienna from 1921, so psychodrama's roots are in the 1920s; he coined 'group psychotherapy' (1932) and opened his Beacon, New York, sanatorium and psychodrama stage in 1936, which is where it took formal shape in the 1930s. The answer used here, b, stands either way.", "topic": "Moreno and psychodrama", "fig": "Jacob L. Moreno"}, {"q": "With aging there is a decline of ______ intelligence, but not of ______ intelligence.", "options": {"a": "Fluid, Fixed.", "b": "Fixed, Fluid.", "c": "Fluid, Crystallized.", "d": "Crystallized, Fluid."}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: with ageing, fluid intelligence declines, but crystallised intelligence does not. Raymond Cattell (1963) and his student John Horn (1966) split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on stored knowledge, such as spotting the rule in a matrix puzzle or working out an unfamiliar gadget. It depends heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in the early twenties and then slowly declines. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills built up through education and experience. Because it keeps growing with use, it holds steady or even rises into old age. K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) followed adults for decades and found that most mental abilities peak in middle adulthood, while perceptual speed declines from early adulthood. Timothy Salthouse (1996) traced much of the fluid loss to a general slowing of processing. Example: Riya's grandmother, aged 72, finishes the newspaper crossword before anyone else and knows every rare word (crystallised), but takes far longer than Riya to learn a new phone app (fluid). Option c is right because it puts the declining ability (fluid) in the first blank and the stable one (crystallised) in the second, exactly as the stem requires. Exam trap: examiners reverse the order (option d) to catch students who remember the pair but not which one falls; think 'fluid flows away, crystals last'. They also invent a 'fixed' intelligence, which is not a type in any theory; it echoes Carol Dweck's (2006) fixed mindset, a belief about ability rather than a kind of ability.", "topic": "Fluid and crystallised intelligence in ageing", "fig": "Raymond Cattell and John Horn"}, {"q": "Positive symptoms of schizophrenia are all except:", "options": {"a": "Hearing voices.", "b": "Confused thinking.", "c": "Believing someone is plotting against them.", "d": "Feeling disconnected from emotions."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: feeling disconnected from emotions is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia. Schizophrenia symptoms are sorted into groups. Positive symptoms are additions to normal experience, an excess or distortion of normal function: hallucinations, delusions, disorganised thinking and speech, and grossly disorganised or bizarre behaviour. 'Positive' means something is present that should not be, not that it is good. Negative symptoms are subtractions, a loss or reduction of normal function: affective flattening (blunted emotional expression), alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (lack of drive), anhedonia (loss of pleasure) and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) tied mainly positive symptoms to his Type I schizophrenia and mainly negative ones to Type II, and Nancy Andreasen (1982, 1984) rated the two groups separately with the SANS and SAPS. A sense of being cut off from one's own feelings belongs with the negative side (blunted affect, emotional withdrawal, anhedonia) or, as a strange detached feeling, with depersonalisation, a dissociative experience. Either way it is a loss, not an addition. Example: Arjun, aged 24, hears a voice commenting on his actions, is sure his neighbours are plotting to poison him and jumps from topic to topic when he talks; months later he also says he feels nothing, even at his sister's wedding. The first three are positive symptoms; the last is negative. Option d is right because it is the only option that describes something missing. Exam trap: 'confused thinking' sounds like a deficit, but disorganised thinking, a formal thought disorder, is counted with the positive symptoms in DSM-5, even though Peter Liddle (1987) treated disorganisation as a separate third syndrome.", "topic": "Positive versus negative symptoms", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Internal standards used to judge stimuli are referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Adaptation level.", "b": "Intuition.", "c": "Frames of reference.", "d": "Context."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: internal standards used to judge stimuli are called frames of reference. We never judge a stimulus in absolute terms; we rate it against a standard we carry inside, built up from past experience and from the surroundings, and that standard is the frame of reference. Muzafer Sherif (1935, 1936) showed one forming in the autokinetic experiment: a still point of light in a dark room seems to move, each person judging alone settled on a personal range, groups then converged on a shared norm, and members kept using that norm when later tested alone. Harry Helson (1947, 1964) made the idea measurable in adaptation-level theory; his 1948 paper is titled 'Adaptation-level as a basis for a quantitative theory of frames of reference'. The adaptation level is the neutral point of one such frame, pooled from the focal stimulus, the background stimuli around it and residual stimuli (traces of past experience); a stimulus above it feels heavy, bright or warm, one below it light, dim or cool. A classic demonstration: put one hand in hot water and the other in cold, then both in lukewarm water; the same water feels cool to the first hand and warm to the second. Example: Kabir lifts a 20 kg sack of rice in his uncle's shop, then picks up a 5 kg bag, which feels surprisingly light, because his frame of reference for weight has moved up. Option c is right because 'frames of reference' is the general name for the internal standards, and adaptation level is Helson's name for the neutral point inside one. Exam trap: adaptation level is the planted near miss. Key note: WBJEEB's final answer keys mark c both times this question was set, JEMAS(PG) 2023 and JEMAS(PG) 2024. This site keyed a (adaptation level) before the official keys were found; a is the narrower Helson term, so c is the answer to give.", "topic": "Frames of reference (Sherif, Helson)", "fig": "Harry Helson"}, {"q": "______ theory maximized and ______ theory minimized the role of the unconscious.", "options": {"a": "Trait; Humanistic.", "b": "Psychoanalytic; Behaviourist.", "c": "Psychoanalytic; Humanistic.", "d": "Trait; Behaviourist."}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory maximised the role of the unconscious, and behaviourist theory minimised it. Sigmund Freud (1900), in The Interpretation of Dreams and later work, placed the unconscious at the centre of personality. In his topographic model the conscious mind is only the tip of an iceberg; below lie the preconscious (memories that can easily be recalled) and the vast unconscious, holding repressed wishes, conflicts and drives that shape dreams, slips of the tongue, symptoms and everyday choices. No other approach gives the unconscious so much weight. Behaviourism went to the opposite extreme. John B. Watson (1913) argued that psychology should study only observable behaviour, and B. F. Skinner (1953) explained personality as learned responses shaped by reinforcement from the environment. Since unconscious wishes cannot be observed or measured, behaviourists set them aside altogether as unscientific. Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) also played down the unconscious, stressing conscious experience, choice and growth, but Rogers still allowed that threatening experiences can be denied or distorted out of awareness, so humanism downplays the unconscious rather than discarding it. Example: when Riya keeps arriving late to meetings with her strict manager, a Freudian looks for hidden hostility, while a behaviourist asks what the lateness has been reinforced by, such as escaping an unpleasant conversation. Option b is right because it pairs the approach most built on the unconscious with the approach that removed it from psychology. Exam trap: option c is the near miss, since humanists also rejected Freud's dark unconscious; but the extreme minimum belongs to behaviourism.", "topic": "Unconscious across personality approaches", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "The presentation of an aversive stimulus or the removal of a positive stimulus are both examples of:", "options": {"a": "Negative reinforcement.", "b": "Punishment.", "c": "Positive reinforcement.", "d": "Secondary reinforcement."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: presenting an aversive stimulus and removing a positive stimulus are both forms of punishment. In operant conditioning, Edward Thorndike's (1911) law of effect and B. F. Skinner's (1938) experiments showed that consequences change how often a behaviour occurs. Two questions classify every consequence. First, does the behaviour become more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or taken away (negative)? Here positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant after a behaviour: a scolding, a slap, extra homework. Negative punishment removes something pleasant: time-out from play, a fine (response cost), losing phone time. Both make the behaviour less likely, so both are punishment. By contrast, positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise, money), and negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a headache ends after taking a tablet, the seat-belt buzzer stops when you buckle up); both make the behaviour more likely. Skinner argued that punishment suppresses behaviour only temporarily, teaches nothing about what to do instead and can breed fear and aggression, so reinforcing a better behaviour usually works better. Example: Kabir's mother scolds him when he hits his sister (positive punishment) and also takes away his cricket bat for a day (negative punishment); both aim to reduce hitting. Option b is right because adding an aversive stimulus and removing a pleasant one are the two forms of punishment. Exam trap: negative reinforcement (option a) is the classic confusion; it removes something unpleasant and so increases behaviour, the reverse of punishment.", "topic": "Positive and negative punishment", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT considered to be a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "options": {"a": "'Eccentric' behaviour marked by odd patterns of thinking and communication.", "b": "An enduring pattern of instability in personal relationships.", "c": "A lack of a well-defined and stable self-image.", "d": "Regular and unpredictable changes in mood."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: eccentric behaviour with odd patterns of thinking and communication is not a cardinal feature of borderline personality disorder. It describes schizotypal personality disorder, which sits in Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, alongside paranoid and schizoid personality disorders. Borderline personality disorder (BPD) belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5 (2013) define it as a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood, and require 5 of 9 criteria: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability, with intense mood shifts lasting hours; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and brief, stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organisation, with splitting (seeing people as all good or all bad) as its central defence. Marsha Linehan (1993) built dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD on her biosocial theory: emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment. Example: Riya, 23, idolises a new friend one week and calls her a traitor the next, cuts herself after arguments and says she does not know who she really is; that pattern points to BPD. Option a is right because odd thinking and eccentric communication belong to schizotypal, not borderline, personality disorder. Exam trap: BPD does include transient, stress-related paranoid ideas, so students sometimes accept 'odd thinking' as borderline; but enduring eccentric thought and speech is the schizotypal signature.", "topic": "Borderline personality disorder features", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The first intelligence test was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Binet and Simon.", "b": "Spearman.", "c": "Stanford and Binet.", "d": "Terman and Wechsler."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the first intelligence test was developed by Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905). In 1904 the French Ministry of Education asked Alfred Binet to find a way to identify children who could not keep up in ordinary classes and needed special schooling. Earlier attempts, such as Francis Galton's (1884) anthropometric laboratory in London and James McKeen Cattell's (1890) 'mental tests', measured reaction time and sensory sharpness, and they failed to predict school success. Binet and the physician Simon took a different route, measuring higher mental processes: judgement, comprehension, reasoning and memory. Their 1905 Binet-Simon scale had 30 items arranged in order of difficulty. The 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most children passed them, giving the concept of mental age, and the 1911 revision extended it towards adults. Binet insisted the score was a practical guide for helping children, not a fixed measure of inborn ability. Later, Lewis Terman (1916) adapted it at Stanford University as the Stanford-Binet, using William Stern's (1912) intelligence quotient. Example: a teacher in Kolkata worried about Meera, aged 9, could use a Binet-style scale to find that she passes the items typical of 7-year-olds, a mental age of 7, signalling that she needs extra support. Option a is right because the 1905 Binet-Simon scale is recognised as the first practical intelligence test. Exam trap: option c sounds right because the Stanford-Binet is the most famous descendant, but 'Stanford' is a university, not a person, and Terman's revision came eleven years later.", "topic": "Binet-Simon: the first intelligence test", "fig": "Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon"}, {"q": "Porteus developed an early performance test based on:", "options": {"a": "Block design.", "b": "Mazes.", "c": "Non-verbal cues.", "d": "Drawing a man."}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: Stanley David Porteus (1914) built his early performance test on mazes. Porteus, an Australian teacher who later worked in the United States and Hawaii, wanted to measure the practical intelligence of children labelled feeble-minded, for whom the language-heavy Binet scale seemed unfair. A performance test is one in which the person does something with materials (arranges blocks, fits shapes, traces a path) instead of answering in words, so it reduces the part played by language and schooling. The Porteus Maze Test is a graded series of printed mazes of rising difficulty, each assigned to an age level. The person traces a route from the entrance to the exit with a pencil and must avoid blind alleys; entering one counts as a failed trial and a fresh copy is given. Porteus argued that the mazes tap planning, foresight and prudence, the ability to look ahead before acting, which he felt the Binet missed. Scores give a test age and a test quotient, and a separate qualitative score counts careless errors such as cutting corners, crossing lines and lifting the pencil, which was later linked to impulsivity and delinquency. Because maze planning depends on the frontal lobes, the test was used in the 1940s and 1950s to study the effects of psychosurgery, and today it is seen as an early measure of executive function. Example: ten-year-old Arjun, who barely reads, rushes into dead ends without looking ahead; the maze shows weak planning, not weak vocabulary. Option b is right because mazes are the defining material of the Porteus test. Exam trap: every option names a classic non-verbal material, so match the material to its maker: coloured blocks to Samuel C. Kohs, drawing a person to Florence Goodenough, mazes to Porteus.", "topic": "Porteus Maze Test", "fig": "Stanley Porteus"}, {"q": "What proportion of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of +1?", "options": {"a": "50%.", "b": "16%.", "c": "25%.", "d": "10%."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: about 16% of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z-score of +1. The normal curve is a symmetrical bell in which the mean, median and mode coincide, and the areas under it are fixed. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors in measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. A z-score says how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies from the mean. About 34.13% of cases fall between the mean and +1 SD, and another 34.13% between the mean and minus 1 SD; 13.59% lie between 1 and 2 SD on each side, and 2.14% between 2 and 3 SD. So about 68% fall within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2 SD and 99.7% within 3 SD. To find the area above z = +1, start from 50%, the whole upper half, and subtract the 34.13% between the mean and +1: that leaves 15.87%, about 16%. Equivalently, z = +1 is the 84th percentile, because 50% plus 34% lie below it. On a deviation IQ scale with mean 100 and SD 15, z = +1 is an IQ of 115, so about 16 in every 100 people score above 115. Example: Meera's z-score on a reasoning test is +1; only about 16% of the norm group scored higher than she did. Option b is right because 50% minus 34% leaves about 16%. Exam trap: students who remember 34% or 68% for one SD forget to subtract from 50, or confuse 'above +1' with 'between the mean and +1'; and 50% (option a) is the area above the mean itself, at z = 0.", "topic": "Normal curve areas above z", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of:", "options": {"a": "Verbal fluency.", "b": "Visual perception.", "c": "Memory.", "d": "Attention."}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Stroop Colour-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of attention. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people either to read colour words printed in a conflicting ink, such as the word RED printed in green, or to name the ink colour. Reading was barely affected, but naming the ink took far longer (about 74% longer than naming plain colour patches) and errors rose. This slowing is the Stroop effect. It happens because reading is automatic for literate adults: the word's meaning is processed whether we want it or not, and it competes with the weaker, controlled task of naming the colour. To succeed, a person must select the relevant feature (the ink) and inhibit the automatic response (reading). That is why clinicians use it as a measure of selective attention, response inhibition and interference control, all part of executive function linked to the anterior cingulate and prefrontal cortex. Standard versions such as Charles Golden's (1978) have word-reading, colour-naming and colour-word cards, and an interference score compares them. Poor scores appear after frontal injury and in ADHD, schizophrenia and dementia; an emotional Stroop, using threat words, shows attentional bias in anxiety. Example: Kabir, recovering from a head injury, reads the colour words quickly but slows badly and makes errors when asked to name the ink colours; his neuropsychologist notes poor inhibitory control. Option d is right because the test measures the ability to control attention against an automatic response. Exam trap: because the test uses words and colours, examiners offer verbal fluency and visual perception; but fluency means generating words (as in the FAS test), and normal colour vision is simply assumed.", "topic": "Stroop test and attention", "fig": "John Ridley Stroop"}, {"q": "Emotional responses to a traumatic event are most directly under the control of the:", "options": {"a": "Somatic nervous system.", "b": "Central nervous system.", "c": "Autonomic nervous system.", "d": "Cerebral cortex."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: emotional responses to a traumatic event, such as a pounding heart, fast breathing, sweating and a dry mouth, are most directly controlled by the autonomic nervous system (ANS). The ANS is the part of the peripheral nervous system that runs the internal organs (heart, blood vessels, lungs, gut and glands) largely without conscious control. It has two branches that work in opposite directions. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for emergencies, which Walter Cannon (1915) called the fight-or-flight response: it raises heart rate and blood pressure, widens the pupils, slows digestion and makes the adrenal medulla release adrenaline. The parasympathetic branch calms the body afterwards (rest and digest), slowing the heart and restarting digestion. Imagine Arjun in a road accident: within seconds his heart races, his palms sweat and his stomach knots, all sympathetic effects, and an hour later his parasympathetic branch brings him back to baseline. The hypothalamus and amygdala in the brain trigger these changes, but the ANS is the system that actually produces the bodily side of emotion. The polygraph (lie detector) works by recording exactly these autonomic changes. Option c is right because the stem asks which system most directly controls the emotional reaction, and the involuntary arousal of fear and stress is autonomic. Exam trap: the somatic nervous system sounds bodily, but it carries sensation and controls the voluntary skeletal muscles; the gut-level, involuntary arousal of emotion belongs to the autonomic system.", "topic": "Autonomic system and emotion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Damage to the cerebellum would most likely result in:", "options": {"a": "Heart stoppage or respiratory failure.", "b": "A loss of hearing ability.", "c": "A loss of muscular coordination.", "d": "Reduction in emotional response."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: damage to the cerebellum most likely causes a loss of muscular coordination. The cerebellum ('little brain') sits at the back of the brain, beneath the occipital lobes and behind the pons, as part of the hindbrain. It does not start movements; the motor cortex does that. Instead it compares the movement you intended with the movement actually happening, using feedback from muscles, joints and the balance organs of the inner ear, and smooths out the errors. It also stores well-practised motor skills and timing, such as cycling or typing, and takes part in simple conditioning such as the eyeblink reflex. Damage produces ataxia: an unsteady, wide-based, staggering walk, poor balance, overshooting when reaching for an object (dysmetria), a tremor that grows as the hand nears its target (intention tremor) and slurred speech. Damage on one side affects the same side of the body. Alcohol depresses the cerebellum early, which is why roadside sobriety tests ask a driver to walk a straight line or touch the nose. For example, after a cerebellar stroke Kabir may know exactly what he wants to do and have full strength, yet he sways when he walks and spills tea when he lifts a cup. Option c is right because coordination, balance and the smooth timing of movement are the cerebellum's core jobs. Exam trap: heart and breathing failure tempts students because the cerebellum lies close to the brainstem, but those vital centres sit in the medulla oblongata.", "topic": "Cerebellum and coordination", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In Guilford's approach to intelligence, the ability to generate a variety of hypotheses in a given problem situation is known as:", "options": {"a": "Cognitive memory.", "b": "Convergent production.", "c": "Divergent production.", "d": "Mediational memory."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: in J. P. Guilford's model, the ability to generate a variety of hypotheses is divergent production. Joy Paul Guilford (1956, 1967) rejected the idea of a single general intelligence. In his Structure of Intellect model every ability is a combination of three dimensions: an operation (what the mind does), a content (what it works on) and a product (the form the result takes). The five operations are cognition (recognising and understanding), memory (storing and recalling), divergent production (producing many varied answers to an open problem), convergent production (arriving at the one correct answer) and evaluation (judging whether an answer is right or good). The contents were figural, symbolic, semantic and behavioural, and the products were units, classes, relations, systems, transformations and implications, giving 5 x 4 x 6 = 120 abilities, later revised to 150 and then 180. Divergent production became the basis for the study of creativity, which Guilford had urged in his 1950 address to the American Psychological Association. It is scored for fluency (how many ideas), flexibility (how many kinds), originality (how rare) and elaboration (how much detail). Example: Meera's plant has wilted, and her teacher asks for every possible reason; Meera suggests too little water, too much water, root rot, pests, a sudden cold night and the wrong soil. Producing many hypotheses is divergent production; picking the single correct cause after testing is convergent production. Option c is right because generating a range of possibilities is the definition of divergent production. Exam trap: convergent production also involves problem solving, but it narrows down to one answer instead of opening up many, and examiners rely on students mixing up the two similar-sounding words.", "topic": "Guilford's divergent production", "fig": "J. P. Guilford"}, {"q": "The Ebbinghaus forgetting curve shows:", "options": {"a": "A gradual drop in retention followed by a steep decline.", "b": "A steep initial drop in retention followed by a gradual decline.", "c": "An S-shaped pattern.", "d": "Higher retention for rote learning than for concept learning."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Ebbinghaus forgetting curve shows a steep initial drop in retention followed by a gradual decline. Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885), in Über das Gedächtnis (Memory), was the first to study memory experimentally. Acting as his own only participant, he learned lists of nonsense syllables such as DAX or BUP, chosen so that meaning and old associations could not help him. He learned each list to one perfect recitation, waited a set time from 20 minutes to 31 days, and then relearned it. Relearning was faster even when he could recall nothing, so he measured memory by the savings method: the percentage of the original learning time saved. Plotting savings against time gave the forgetting curve. It falls very steeply at first, with more than half the material lost within about an hour and about two thirds within a day, then flattens into a slow, gradual decline that never quite reaches zero. Jaap Murre and Joeri Dros (2015) repeated the experiment and obtained a very similar curve. Ebbinghaus also noted the spacing effect: practice spread out over time beats cramming. Example: Riya learns 30 new anatomy terms at 9 in the morning; by lunch she has lost many of them, but much of what survives the first day is still there a week later. Spaced revision would soften the early drop. Option b is right because it gives the correct shape: fast loss first, then slow loss. Exam trap: option a reverses the order of the two phases, and examiners rely on students remembering 'steep' and 'gradual' but not which comes first.", "topic": "Ebbinghaus forgetting curve", "fig": "Hermann Ebbinghaus"}, {"q": "Anterograde amnesia results from the injury of the:", "options": {"a": "Hippocampus.", "b": "Hypothalamus.", "c": "Pituitary gland.", "d": "Cerebellum."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: anterograde amnesia results from injury to the hippocampus. Anterograde amnesia is the inability to form new long-term memories after the onset of brain damage; retrograde amnesia is the loss of memories formed before it. The hippocampus, a curved structure inside each medial temporal lobe, is needed to consolidate new facts and events (declarative memory) into lasting storage. The classic evidence is the patient H.M., Henry Molaison. In 1953 the surgeon William Beecher Scoville removed most of both hippocampi, with nearby medial temporal tissue, to stop his severe epilepsy. William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported the result: a profound anterograde amnesia. He reread the same magazines with fresh interest, never learned his new address and met his doctors as strangers for decades. Yet his intelligence, short-term memory (digit span) and childhood memories were largely intact, and over three days he improved at mirror drawing while denying he had ever done the task, which showed that procedural memory does not depend on the hippocampus. Example: after a brain infection damages both of his hippocampi, Arjun can still describe his school days but cannot remember the nurse he met ten minutes ago. Option a is right because hippocampal damage is the textbook cause of anterograde amnesia. Exam trap: the mammillary bodies at the base of the hypothalamus are damaged in Korsakoff's syndrome, which also brings anterograde amnesia, so 'hypothalamus' can look tempting; but the hippocampus is the structure the classic evidence points to, and the cerebellum handles motor skill, not new facts.", "topic": "Hippocampus and anterograde amnesia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Type I error describes the likelihood of an experimenter:", "options": {"a": "Considering the result to be significant when it is not.", "b": "Measuring variability within a distribution.", "c": "Failing to take variance into account.", "d": "Failing to take variable weightings into account."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a Type I error is concluding that a result is significant when it is not. Every significance test starts with a null hypothesis, which says there is no effect or no difference. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) named the two ways a test can go wrong. A Type I error is rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true: the researcher declares an effect that does not exist, a false positive. Its probability is alpha, the significance level the researcher sets in advance, usually 0.05, which means accepting a 5 per cent risk of such a false alarm. A Type II error is the opposite: failing to reject a null hypothesis that is false, so a real effect is missed, a false negative. Its probability is beta, and statistical power is 1 minus beta. Lowering alpha (to 0.01, say) cuts the Type I risk but raises the Type II risk unless the sample is enlarged. Example: Arjun tests whether a herbal tablet improves memory; his result is p = 0.03, so he rejects the null and reports that the tablet works. If in truth the tablet does nothing, he has made a Type I error. If the tablet really helped but his small sample gave p = 0.20, he would have made a Type II error. Option a is right because 'considering the result to be significant when it is not' is a false positive. Exam trap: students reverse the two; think of the boy who cried wolf: the villagers first believe in a wolf that is not there (Type I), then ignore the wolf that is (Type II).", "topic": "Type I error (alpha)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term social loafing refers to:", "options": {"a": "Playing when expected to be working.", "b": "Working harder in groups than individually.", "c": "Working harder individually than in groups.", "d": "The opposite of social inhibition."}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: social loafing means people work harder individually than in groups. Social loafing is the drop in effort that occurs when people's contributions are pooled in a group, compared with when each works alone and can be identified. The first evidence came from the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann (1913): when men pulled on a rope, the group's total pull was less than the sum of the members pulling separately, and each person's share fell as the group grew. Part of this could be poor coordination, but Alan Ingham and colleagues (1974) showed that blindfolded people who merely believed others were pulling with them also pulled less, so motivation drops too. Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: blindfolded students wearing headphones clapped and shouted less loudly per person in supposed groups than alone. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) explained it with the collective effort model: people work hard when they believe their effort matters and will be noticed. Loafing shrinks when individual output is identifiable, the task is meaningful or the group is close-knit. Example: in Riya's four-member college project, two members contribute little because the teacher grades only the final report; when the teacher asks each student to submit a named section, everyone works harder. Option c is right because it captures the essence: less effort in the group than alone. Exam trap: option a uses the everyday meaning of 'loafing' (idling), and option d describes social facilitation, a different group effect.", "topic": "Social loafing", "fig": "Bibb Latane"}, {"q": "A physical stimulus is converted into a neural impulse by a process known as:", "options": {"a": "Transposition.", "b": "Transaction.", "c": "Transduction.", "d": "Transition."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the conversion of a physical stimulus into a neural impulse is called transduction. Every sense organ contains receptors, cells specialised for one form of energy. Their job is to translate that energy into the only language the nervous system understands: changes in membrane voltage that lead to nerve impulses. In the eye, light is absorbed by photopigments such as rhodopsin in the rods and cones; this changes the receptor's membrane potential, and the signal passes through bipolar cells to ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve. In the ear, sound waves bend the stereocilia of hair cells in the cochlea, opening ion channels. In the skin, pressure deforms mechanoreceptors; in the nose and tongue, chemicals bind to receptor proteins. In each case the first change is a graded receptor potential whose size follows the strength of the stimulus; this then triggers all-or-none impulses whose rate codes intensity. Because all impulses look alike, Johannes Peter Muller (1801 to 1858) proposed the doctrine of specific nerve energies: the quality of a sensation depends on which nerve is stimulated, which is why pressing on a closed eye makes you see flashes of light. Sensation is detection and transduction; perception is the later interpretation by the brain. Example: Riya steps into sunlight; within milliseconds her rods and cones turn light into electrical signals, and only then can her brain recognise her friend's face across the road. Option c is right because transduction is the technical term for converting stimulus energy into neural signals at the receptor. Exam trap: all four options begin with \"trans-\"; only transduction is the sensory term, while transposition and transaction belong to other areas of psychology.", "topic": "Sensory transduction", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of:", "options": {"a": "Taste.", "b": "Sight.", "c": "Hearing.", "d": "Touch."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of touch. The primary somatosensory cortex lies in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus, facing the primary motor cortex across that groove. It receives signals from receptors in the skin, muscles and joints, relayed through the spinal cord and the thalamus, and gives us touch, pressure, vibration, temperature, pain and the sense of body position. Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950) stimulated this strip in conscious patients during brain surgery; patients reported tingling in particular body parts, which let them draw the sensory homunculus. The map is contralateral (the left hemisphere feels the right side of the body) and distorted: the lips, tongue and fingertips have huge areas because they are packed with receptors, while the back has a small one. Damage here causes numbness or difficulty recognising objects by touch (astereognosis). Vilayanur Ramachandran (1992) showed that after an arm amputation the face area can take over the hand area, so a touch on the face is felt in the phantom hand. Example: Zoya, with her eyes closed, tells a coin from a key in her right pocket; the signals from her fingertips are analysed in her left postcentral gyrus. Option d is right because touch and body sense are the job of the somatosensory strip; 'somato' means body. Exam trap: each sense has its own primary area, and examiners mix them: vision is in the occipital lobe, hearing in the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus), and taste in the insula and frontal operculum, which sit close to the face area but form a separate gustatory cortex.", "topic": "Somatosensory cortex and touch", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The glands of the body serve as:", "options": {"a": "Receptors.", "b": "Effectors.", "c": "Nodes.", "d": "Afferent nerves."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the glands of the body serve as effectors. In the nervous system, information flows in a loop. Receptors, such as the rods and cones of the eye or the touch receptors in the skin, detect a stimulus and turn it into nerve signals. Afferent (sensory) neurons carry those signals to the brain and spinal cord. After the central nervous system processes them, efferent (motor) neurons carry commands out to the effectors, the organs that actually carry out the response. There are two kinds of effectors: muscles, which contract (skeletal, smooth and cardiac), and glands, which secrete. Exocrine glands, such as the salivary, sweat and tear glands, release their products through ducts; endocrine glands, such as the thyroid and adrenal glands, release hormones into the blood. Together these parts make up the reflex arc: receptor, afferent neuron, interneuron, efferent neuron, effector. Ivan Pavlov's (1897) work on digestion is a reminder that a gland can be the effector of a reflex, since salivation is a glandular response. Example: when Kabir smells his mother's biryani, receptors in his nose fire, signals reach his brain, and efferent nerves set his salivary glands secreting; the gland is the effector. Option b is right because glands carry out responses on command from nerves or hormones rather than detecting stimuli. Exam trap: students picture effectors only as muscles that move, so a gland, which does not move, seems wrong; but secretion is a response just as contraction is.", "topic": "Effectors: muscles and glands", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The principal function of myelin is to:", "options": {"a": "Increase the amount of neurotransmitter released into a synapse.", "b": "Decrease the distance of the synaptic cleft.", "c": "Increase the rate of propagation of an action potential.", "d": "Increase the amplitude of an action potential."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the principal function of myelin is to increase the speed at which an action potential travels along the axon. Myelin is a fatty insulating sheath wrapped in many layers around axons. In the central nervous system it is made by oligodendrocytes, each of which can wrap segments of several axons; in the peripheral nervous system it is made by Schwann cells, each wrapping one segment of one axon. Between the segments are short bare gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, named after Louis-Antoine Ranvier (1835 to 1922), where voltage-gated sodium channels are concentrated. Because ions cannot leak across the myelinated stretches, current spreads quickly inside the axon to the next node, and the action potential is regenerated only at the nodes. The impulse seems to jump from node to node: saltatory conduction (Latin saltare, to leap). Thin unmyelinated axons conduct at about 1 metre per second; large myelinated axons at up to about 100 metres per second. Myelin also saves energy, because fewer ions must be pumped back after each impulse. Myelination continues through childhood and adolescence, and the prefrontal cortex is among the last regions to finish, in the twenties. Example: Meera, 29, develops blurred vision in one eye and tingling in her legs. An MRI shows patches of lost myelin in her brain and spinal cord: multiple sclerosis, in which the immune system attacks CNS myelin, so conduction slows or fails. Option c is right because myelin's job is faster propagation of the action potential. Exam trap: myelin changes the speed, not the size, of the impulse; by the all-or-none law, every action potential in a given neuron has the same amplitude.", "topic": "Myelin and conduction speed", "fig": ""}, {"q": "REM sleep behaviour disorder is associated with movements, occasionally violent, during sleep, sometimes characterised as \"acting out dreams\". What normal feature of REM sleep is absent in individuals with this disorder?", "options": {"a": "Atonia.", "b": "Rapid eye movements.", "c": "High frequency, low amplitude EEG.", "d": "Penile erections (in males)."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: in REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD) the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep is absent. Normal REM sleep has several signature features: rapid eye movements, a desynchronised EEG of high-frequency, low-amplitude waves that looks almost like waking, vivid dreaming, penile or clitoral engorgement, and atonia, a near-complete paralysis of the skeletal muscles. Atonia is produced by circuits in the pons that drive medullary neurons to inhibit the spinal motor neurons. It stops us acting out our dreams, which is why REM is called paradoxical sleep: an active brain in a paralysed body. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed that cats with lesions in this pontine region rose up during REM and stalked, pounced and attacked unseen prey. Carlos Schenck and Mark Mahowald (1986) described the human disorder. People with RBD punch, kick, shout or leap from bed, mostly in the second half of the night, and on waking can recall a dream that matches the movements, often of being chased or attacked. Injuries to self and bed partner are common. Most patients are men over 50, and RBD often precedes Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies by years. Example: Suresh, 64, dreams of fighting off a thief and punches his wife in his sleep. A sleep study shows REM sleep with bursts of muscle activity on the chin and limb recordings. Option a is right because loss of REM atonia is the defining feature of RBD. Exam trap: RBD is a REM parasomnia; sleepwalking and night terrors are NREM parasomnias arising from slow-wave sleep, when the body is not paralysed in the first place.", "topic": "REM sleep behaviour disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a neurotransmitter?", "options": {"a": "Serotonin.", "b": "Dopamine.", "c": "Epinephrine.", "d": "Insulin."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: insulin is not a neurotransmitter; it is a hormone made by the pancreas. A neurotransmitter is a chemical that a neuron stores in vesicles at its axon terminal and releases into the synaptic cleft, where it acts within milliseconds on receptors of the next cell, only a fraction of a micrometre away. A hormone is released by an endocrine (ductless) gland into the blood and travels to distant target cells, acting over minutes to hours. Insulin is a protein hormone released by the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas when blood glucose rises after a meal. It lets muscle, fat and liver cells take up glucose, so blood sugar falls; glucagon from the alpha cells does the opposite. Insulin also reaches the brain from the blood and tells the hypothalamus that the body is fed, but it is not released by neurons at synapses, so it is not counted as a neurotransmitter. Serotonin (from the raphe nuclei: mood, sleep, appetite) and dopamine (from the substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area: movement and reward) are classic monoamine transmitters. Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the tricky one: it is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla, but a few brainstem neurons also release it as a transmitter, so it is both. Example: when Riya eats a plate of jalebis, her pancreas pours insulin into her blood, while inside her brain dopamine is released at synapses of the reward circuit. Option d is right because insulin is the only substance listed that works purely as a blood-borne hormone. Exam trap: epinephrine is the near miss. When a list offers only dopamine, serotonin, noradrenaline and adrenaline, adrenaline is marked as the odd one out because it acts mainly as a hormone; once a pure hormone such as insulin is offered, adrenaline counts as a neurotransmitter and insulin is the answer.", "topic": "Hormones versus neurotransmitters", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Left hemisphere is to right hemisphere as:", "options": {"a": "Hearing is to speaking.", "b": "Language functioning is to spatial ability.", "c": "Balance is to movement.", "d": "Broca's area is to Wernicke's area."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the left hemisphere is to the right hemisphere as language functioning is to spatial ability. Lateralisation means that the two cerebral hemispheres, though they look alike, are specialised for different jobs. The first evidence came from brain damage. Paul Broca (1861) studied a patient, Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan', who understood speech but could say only that one syllable; the autopsy showed a lesion in the left frontal lobe. Carl Wernicke (1874) then linked a left temporal lesion to fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension. In most people speech, grammar, reading and writing depend on the left hemisphere; this holds for about 95 percent of right-handers and for most left-handers. The right hemisphere is better at visuospatial tasks such as reading maps, judging depth, drawing, recognising faces and attending to the left side of space, and it also handles the emotional tone (prosody) of speech. The strongest evidence came from split-brain patients tested by Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga in the 1960s: a word shown to the left hemisphere could be named, while the right hemisphere could not name an object but could pick it out by touch and copy drawings better. Example: after a left-hemisphere stroke, Kabir struggles to find words but still finds his way home; after a right parietal stroke, Meera speaks normally but cannot copy a simple drawing and ignores the food on the left of her plate. Option b is right because language versus spatial ability is the classic left versus right division. Exam trap: option d pairs two famous names, but Broca's area (frontal) and Wernicke's area (temporal) both lie in the left hemisphere, so they cannot stand for left versus right. Remember too that lateralisation is a relative bias; the popular 'logical left-brained versus creative right-brained person' is a myth.", "topic": "Hemispheric lateralisation", "fig": "Roger Sperry"}, {"q": "What part of the brain has been surgically altered in \"split-brain\" patients?", "options": {"a": "Corpus callosum.", "b": "Cerebellum.", "c": "Sylvian fissure.", "d": "Rolandic fissure."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: split-brain patients have had their corpus callosum cut. The corpus callosum is the largest commissure of the brain, a thick band of about 200 million nerve fibres that links the left and right cerebral hemispheres and lets them share information. In people with severe epilepsy that does not respond to drugs, a seizure starting in one hemisphere can race across this bridge to the other. From the 1960s the surgeons Philip Vogel and Joseph Bogen cut the corpus callosum (a corpus callosotomy, often called commissurotomy) to stop the spread; the seizures eased and the patients seemed almost unchanged in daily life. Earlier, Ronald Myers and Roger Sperry (1953) had shown in cats that the corpus callosum transfers learning from one hemisphere to the other. Sperry and his student Michael Gazzaniga then tested the human patients, work that won Sperry the Nobel Prize in 1981. Because the left visual field projects to the right hemisphere, a picture flashed briefly to the left could not be named, since speech sits in the left hemisphere, yet the left hand could find the object by touch. Example: Arjun, who had a callosotomy for epilepsy, fixates a central dot while a spoon is flashed to his left visual field; he says he saw nothing, but his left hand picks the spoon out of a hidden tray. Option a is right because the split brain is defined by cutting the corpus callosum. Exam trap: the two fissures sound surgical but are only grooves on the brain's surface that mark the lobes, and the cerebellum is a separate hindbrain structure that has nothing to do with joining the hemispheres.", "topic": "Split-brain surgery", "fig": "Roger Sperry"}, {"q": "The two major divisions of the autonomic nervous system (ANS) are:", "options": {"a": "Somatic and peripheral.", "b": "Brain and spinal cord.", "c": "Sympathetic and parasympathetic.", "d": "Parietal and occipital."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the two major divisions of the autonomic nervous system are the sympathetic and the parasympathetic. The nervous system divides first into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (all the nerves outside them). The peripheral system has two parts: the somatic system, which carries the senses in and sends commands to the skeletal muscles under voluntary control, and the autonomic system, which runs the heart, smooth muscle and glands largely without our awareness. The autonomic system itself has two divisions that usually act in opposition. The sympathetic division prepares the body for action, which Walter Cannon (1915) called the fight-or-flight response: the heart races, pupils widen, airways open, digestion stops and the liver releases glucose. Its fibres leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord (the thoracolumbar outflow). The parasympathetic division conserves energy, the rest-and-digest state: it slows the heart, narrows the pupils and promotes salivation and digestion. Its fibres leave the brainstem, mostly through the vagus nerve, and the sacral cord (the craniosacral outflow). Most organs receive both, so heart rate reflects the balance between them. Many books add the enteric nervous system of the gut wall as a third division. Example: before her viva, Riya's heart pounds and her mouth goes dry (sympathetic); an hour after a relaxed lunch, her heart slows and she feels drowsy (parasympathetic). Option c is right because sympathetic and parasympathetic are the two branches into which the autonomic system divides. Exam trap: examiners offer pairs from other levels of the hierarchy. Somatic and autonomic are the two parts of the peripheral system, and the brain and spinal cord make up the central system, so neither pair divides the autonomic system.", "topic": "Divisions of the autonomic system", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following chemical substances is NOT classified as an opioid?", "options": {"a": "Cocaine.", "b": "Codeine.", "c": "Heroin.", "d": "Morphine."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: cocaine is not an opioid; it is a stimulant. Opioids are substances that act on the brain's opioid receptors, chiefly the mu receptor, the same receptors used by the body's own endorphins and enkephalins. They relieve pain, suppress coughing and cause euphoria and drowsiness, and in overdose they stop breathing; naloxone reverses an overdose by blocking the receptors. Opioids come in three groups. Natural opiates are taken from the sap of the opium poppy: morphine and codeine, a weaker relative used for cough and mild pain. Semi-synthetic opioids are made by chemically changing a natural one: heroin is diacetylmorphine, morphine with two acetyl groups added so that it enters the brain faster. Fully synthetic opioids, such as methadone and fentanyl, are built in the laboratory. Cocaine comes from a different plant, the coca leaf of South America. It blocks the reuptake of dopamine and noradrenaline (and serotonin), so these transmitters pile up in the synapse, giving alertness, confidence and euphoria, followed by a crash; heavy use can cause paranoia and cocaine psychosis. It is also a local anaesthetic, first used in eye surgery by Carl Koller (1884). Example: Arjun, who misuses codeine cough syrup, becomes drowsy with pinpoint pupils; his friend Kabir, who snorts cocaine at a party, becomes restless and talkative with dilated pupils. Option a is right because cocaine is a stimulant that works on monoamine reuptake, not on opioid receptors. Exam trap: in law, cocaine has long been grouped with heroin and morphine as a 'narcotic' drug, and 'cocaine' sounds like 'codeine', so students lump it with the opioids; pharmacologically the two classes have opposite effects.", "topic": "Opioids versus stimulants", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which structure of the brain is related to emotional processing?", "options": {"a": "Amygdala.", "b": "Hippocampus.", "c": "Prefrontal cortex.", "d": "Anterior cingulate."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the amygdala is the brain structure most closely related to emotional processing. The amygdala (Greek for almond) is a cluster of nuclei deep in each medial temporal lobe, just in front of the hippocampus. It detects threat, gives events emotional importance, triggers fear and defensive responses, and learns which cues predict danger. Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1937) removed the temporal lobes, amygdala included, of rhesus monkeys: the animals became tame and fearless, approached snakes, put objects in their mouths and were hypersexual, a pattern now called Kluver-Bucy syndrome. Joseph LeDoux (from the 1980s) mapped fear conditioning in rats: a tone paired with shock comes to make the rat freeze, and lesions of the amygdala abolish this learned fear. He described a fast 'low road' from the thalamus straight to the amygdala and a slower 'high road' through the sensory cortex. In humans, patient S.M., whose amygdalae were destroyed on both sides by Urbach-Wiethe disease, could not recognise fear in faces (Ralph Adolphs and colleagues, 1994) and barely felt it herself. Example: Meera jumps back from a coiled shape on the path before she realises it is a rope; her amygdala has reacted through the low road before her cortex has identified the object. Option a is right because the amygdala is the core emotional structure in every textbook account. Exam trap: the other three also sit in emotion circuits, so students who know some neuroscience hesitate; choose the structure whose main job is emotion. Key note: the hippocampus, prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate all take part in emotion (James Papez's circuit, 1937; Paul MacLean's limbic system, 1952), so the stem is loose; the amygdala is the structure most tied to emotional processing, and a stands.", "topic": "Amygdala and emotion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In signal-detection research, when no stimulus is presented and the subject reports seeing one, it is called a:", "options": {"a": "Hit.", "b": "Miss.", "c": "False alarm.", "d": "False positive."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in signal-detection research, reporting a stimulus when none was presented is called a false alarm. Classical psychophysics assumed a fixed threshold, but wartime radar operators showed that whether a person says 'yes' depends both on what they can sense and on how willing they are to say yes. Signal detection theory, set out by David Green and John Swets (1966), separates the two. On every trial a signal is either present or absent, and the observer says either 'yes' or 'no', which gives four outcomes. Signal present and 'yes' is a hit. Signal present and 'no' is a miss. Signal absent and 'yes' is a false alarm. Signal absent and 'no' is a correct rejection. From the hit and false alarm rates the researcher works out sensitivity (d prime, how well the person tells signal from noise) and the criterion (beta, how ready they are to say yes). A liberal observer, like a touchy smoke detector, gives many hits but also many false alarms; a conservative one gives few false alarms but more misses. Example: Kabir, an airport security screener, flags a bag as holding a knife when it holds only a steel tiffin box; no target was present but he said yes, so that is a false alarm. Option c is right because 'false alarm' is the signal-detection name for a yes response on a trial with no signal. Exam trap: students swap the two errors. A miss is saying no when the signal was there; a false alarm is saying yes when it was not. Key note: 'false positive' names the same event in diagnostic-testing language, so d is not wrong in plain terms; in signal-detection research the term is false alarm, and c stands.", "topic": "Signal detection outcomes", "fig": "David Green and John Swets"}, {"q": "When a light paired with a bell comes to elicit a conditioned response (CR) such as salivation, the phenomenon is called:", "options": {"a": "Anomalous conditioning.", "b": "Distinctive conditioning.", "c": "Counterconditioning.", "d": "Higher-order conditioning."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: when a light paired with a bell comes to elicit salivation, the phenomenon is higher-order conditioning. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in Conditioned Reflexes, a neutral stimulus (a bell) is repeatedly followed by an unconditioned stimulus (food) that naturally causes an unconditioned response (salivation). After pairing, the bell alone becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces salivation, now a conditioned response (CR). Higher-order conditioning adds a further layer. Once the bell is a strong CS, a new neutral stimulus, a light, is paired with the bell, and no food is given. The light then comes to make the dog salivate as well. The bell has acted as if it were the unconditioned stimulus; this is second-order conditioning, and a third stimulus paired with the light would give third-order conditioning. Each layer is weaker, and because the bell is now presented without food it slowly extinguishes, so higher-order responses fade. The idea explains how human fears and likings spread far beyond any original experience. Example: Arjun once failed a test and felt sick with dread, so the word 'exam' became a CS for anxiety. Later the school's long bell, which always rang just before the teacher announced an exam, came to make his heart race too, although the bell itself was never linked to failure. Option d is right because the light gains its power from a previously conditioned stimulus, not from food. Exam trap: do not confuse it with sensory preconditioning (Wilfred Brogden, 1939), where the two neutral stimuli are paired first and only afterwards is one of them conditioned; in higher-order conditioning the first CS is trained before the new stimulus is added.", "topic": "Higher-order conditioning", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "Which statistic is computed to assess the difference between expected frequencies and observed frequencies?", "options": {"a": "Alpha.", "b": "Pearson's correlation.", "c": "Standardized beta-value.", "d": "Chi-square."}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: chi-square is the statistic that compares observed frequencies with expected frequencies. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square test for nominal data, where there are no means, only counts of people in categories. For each category the researcher compares the observed frequency (O) with the frequency expected (E) if the null hypothesis were true, squares the difference and divides it by the expected value, then adds these up: chi-square = sum of (O minus E) squared over E. A large value means the counts depart from expectation by more than chance would allow. The goodness-of-fit test asks whether one variable's counts fit a hypothesised pattern, with degrees of freedom equal to the number of categories minus 1. The test of independence asks whether two categorical variables in a contingency table are related, with df = (rows minus 1) times (columns minus 1). It is a non-parametric test, needs raw counts rather than percentages, and works best when each expected frequency is at least 5. Example: Riya asks 120 students which of three canteen snacks they prefer. If there is no preference she expects 40 each; she observes 60, 35 and 25. Chi-square = 400/40 + 25/40 + 225/40 = 16.25, with 2 df, well above the 5.99 needed at the 0.05 level, so the preferences are not equal. Option d is right because only chi-square is built from the gap between observed and expected frequencies. Exam trap: Pearson's name also appears in option b, which tempts students who remember that Pearson invented chi-square; but Pearson's correlation measures the linear relationship between two continuous variables, not a gap between counts.", "topic": "Chi-square test", "fig": "Karl Pearson"}, {"q": "What hormone secreted by the pineal gland increases when periods of darkness begin and promotes a feeling of drowsiness?", "options": {"a": "Testosterone.", "b": "Melatonin.", "c": "Cortisol.", "d": "Epinephrine."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: melatonin, secreted by the pineal gland, rises when darkness begins and promotes drowsiness. The pineal gland is a small, pine-cone-shaped gland near the centre of the brain; Rene Descartes (1649) thought it was the seat of the soul. Aaron Lerner (1958) and colleagues isolated its hormone, melatonin, from cattle pineal glands. Melatonin is made from serotonin and is released mainly at night. Its timing is set by the body's master clock, the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus. Light falling on special melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells reaches the SCN through the retinohypothalamic tract; in daylight the SCN keeps the pineal quiet, and as darkness falls the brake comes off, so melatonin climbs through the evening, peaks in the middle of the night and falls by morning. Rising melatonin lowers body temperature and alertness and signals 'night' to the body, so it works as a darkness hormone that opens the gate to sleep rather than as a strong sedative. Bright screens at night can suppress it and delay sleep, and low-dose melatonin is used to shift the clock in jet lag and delayed sleep phase. Example: Aditya scrolls on his phone until 2 a.m.; the bright light holds back his melatonin, so he lies awake even though he is tired. Option b is right because melatonin is the pineal hormone tied to darkness and drowsiness. Exam trap: cortisol also follows a daily rhythm, so it tempts; but cortisol, from the adrenal cortex, peaks in the early morning and helps wake us, the opposite timing.", "topic": "Melatonin and the pineal gland", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Learned helplessness is a feature typically seen in:", "options": {"a": "Anxiety.", "b": "Bipolar Disorder.", "c": "Conversion Disorder.", "d": "Depression."}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: learned helplessness is a feature typically seen in depression. Learned helplessness is the passive giving up that follows experience with events one cannot control. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) gave one group of dogs shocks they could stop by pressing a panel, a yoked group the same shocks with no way to stop them, and a third group no shocks. Later, in a shuttle box where jumping a low barrier ended the shock, most yoked dogs lay down and whined instead of escaping, while the other dogs learned quickly. The lack of control, not the shock itself, produced the passivity. Seligman (1975) proposed this as a model of depression: like the dogs, depressed people show passivity, low motivation, slowness to learn that their actions can work, and sad mood. Because the animal model ignored how people explain events, Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) reformulated it: depression follows when bad events are blamed on internal ('it's me'), stable ('it will last') and global ('it spoils everything') causes. This later grew into the hopelessness theory of depression. Example: Meera fails mathematics three times despite studying hard. She concludes 'I am stupid and nothing I do matters', stops attending classes, even in subjects she enjoys, and feels low most days. Option d is right because passivity after uncontrollable failure is the core of the helplessness model of depression. Exam trap: anxiety is the near miss, since the helpless dogs were also stressed and anxious people often feel out of control; but the classic model links helplessness to the giving up, low motivation and low mood of depression, not to the tense watchfulness of anxiety.", "topic": "Learned helplessness and depression", "fig": "Martin Seligman"}, {"q": "Which area is known as the satiety centre?", "options": {"a": "Lateral hypothalamus.", "b": "Ventromedial hypothalamus.", "c": "Cerebrum.", "d": "Basal ganglia."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) is known as the satiety centre. The hypothalamus, a small structure below the thalamus, controls hunger, thirst, temperature and sexual behaviour. Two classic lesion studies built the dual-centre theory of eating. Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940) destroyed the VMH of rats: the rats overate (hyperphagia) and grew grossly obese, first gaining weight fast and then holding a new, higher weight. The VMH therefore seemed to be the 'stop eating' centre. The Indian physiologist Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck (1951) destroyed the lateral hypothalamus (LH) instead, and their rats refused to eat (aphagia), some starving to death; so the LH seemed to be the 'start eating' or hunger centre. Eliot Stellar (1954) joined the two: the LH switches eating on and the VMH switches it off. The model has since been revised. VMH lesions raise insulin, so food is stored as fat, and part of the effect comes from cutting fibres of the nearby paraventricular nucleus. Today the arcuate nucleus is seen as the hub where signals such as leptin from fat cells, insulin and the hunger hormone ghrelin are combined. Example: at a wedding feast Riya stops eating after the main course even when sweets arrive; signals of fullness acting on her hypothalamus have switched hunger off. Option b is right because the VMH is the structure textbooks label the satiety centre, its lesion causing overeating. Exam trap: the two centres are easily swapped. Lateral means hunger (lesion: the rat will not eat); ventromedial means satiety (lesion: the rat cannot stop). A memory aid: Ventromedial is 'Very Much' full.", "topic": "Satiety centre of the hypothalamus", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Interpersonal therapy was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Adler.", "b": "Carl Jung.", "c": "Gerald Klerman.", "d": "Plato."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: interpersonal therapy (IPT) was developed by Gerald Klerman. Klerman, an American psychiatrist, and his wife Myrna Weissman built IPT as a brief treatment for depression and set it out in the manual Interpersonal Psychotherapy of Depression (1984). Its roots lie in Adolf Meyer's psychobiology (early 1900s), which saw mental illness as a response to life circumstances, in Harry Stack Sullivan's interpersonal theory of psychiatry (1953) and in John Bowlby's attachment theory (1969). IPT assumes that depression, whatever its causes, happens in a social context and is kept going by current relationship problems. It does not dig into childhood or interpret the transference; it treats depression as a medical illness and gives the patient the sick role. Therapy runs about 12 to 16 weekly sessions in three phases. After reviewing important relationships (the interpersonal inventory), therapist and patient choose one or two of four problem areas: grief, role disputes (conflict with a spouse, parent or boss), role transitions (marriage, a new job, retirement) and interpersonal deficits (isolation, few close ties). In the large NIMH depression trial (Irene Elkin and colleagues, 1989) IPT did about as well as cognitive behaviour therapy. Example: Kabir became depressed after retiring from the railways; his therapist names the problem area as a role transition and helps him mourn his old role and build a new one through a neighbourhood group. Option c is right because Klerman, with Weissman, created and manualised IPT. Exam trap: Sullivan's interpersonal theory and Alfred Adler's (1912) stress on social interest both sound interpersonal, so students credit them; Sullivan inspired IPT, but Klerman developed the therapy.", "topic": "Interpersonal psychotherapy origins", "fig": "Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman"}, {"q": "Positive symptoms of schizophrenia are all except:", "options": {"a": "Affective flattening.", "b": "Bizarre behaviour.", "c": "Circumstantiality.", "d": "Delusions."}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: affective flattening is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it is a negative symptom. Symptoms of schizophrenia are sorted by whether something is added to or taken away from normal experience. Positive symptoms are an excess or distortion of normal function: hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech (formal thought disorder) and grossly disorganised or bizarre behaviour. Negative symptoms are a loss or reduction of normal function: affective flattening, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition, anhedonia and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) linked mainly positive symptoms to an acute Type I illness that responds to antipsychotics, and mainly negative symptoms to a chronic Type II illness with a poor drug response. Nancy Andreasen turned the split into two rating scales, the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS, 1982) and the Scale for the Assessment of Positive Symptoms (SAPS, 1984). The SAPS covers hallucinations, delusions, bizarre behaviour and positive formal thought disorder, and the last includes derailment, tangentiality, incoherence and circumstantiality. Affective flattening, the first domain of the SANS, is reduced emotional expression in face, voice and gesture. Example: Arjun, who has had schizophrenia for ten years, speaks in a flat monotone, rarely smiles or makes eye contact, and shows no change of expression when told of his sister's wedding. Option a is right because flattening is the absence of something normal, which makes it negative. Exam trap: circumstantiality looks like the odd one out, since it is mild and is also seen in obsessional personalities and in epilepsy; but Andreasen rates it as a positive formal thought disorder, because it adds needless detail rather than taking something away.", "topic": "Positive versus negative symptoms", "fig": "Nancy Andreasen"}, {"q": "What are the three \"justice rules\" that are employed most often as standards of fairness in social relationships?", "options": {"a": "Equity, need and equality.", "b": "Contribution, equality and facilitation.", "c": "Need, equality and interdependence.", "d": "Contribution, need and conformity."}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the three justice rules used most often as standards of fairness are equity, need and equality. They concern distributive justice, the fairness of how rewards and costs are shared out. Morton Deutsch (1975) argued that people rely on three main rules. Under equity, rewards are proportional to contribution, so whoever puts in more gets more; this is the idea behind J. Stacy Adams's equity theory (1965), in which people compare their ratio of outcomes to inputs with other people's. Under equality, everyone gets the same share whatever they put in. Under need, rewards go to those who need them most. Deutsch's central point was that the rule chosen depends on the goal of the relationship. Equity dominates where economic productivity is the aim, as in a business; equality where the aim is harmony and good relations, as among friends or teammates; and need where the aim is personal welfare and care, as in a family. Distributive justice differs from procedural justice, the fairness of the methods used to decide, studied by John Thibaut and Laurens Walker (1975). Example: Meera, Kabir and Riya earn Rs 3,000 from a stall at their college fest. Meera worked twice as many hours, so equity gives her the largest share; equality gives each Rs 1,000; need gives more to Riya, who must pay her hostel fee this week. Option a is right because equity, equality and need are the three standard distributive rules. Exam trap: each wrong option keeps one or two real rules and adds a familiar social psychology term that is not a justice rule at all (facilitation, interdependence, conformity); 'contribution' is simply equity under another name.", "topic": "Distributive justice rules", "fig": "Morton Deutsch"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a behaviour therapy technique?", "options": {"a": "Flooding.", "b": "De-reflection.", "c": "Counterconditioning.", "d": "Systematic desensitisation."}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: de-reflection is not a behaviour therapy technique; it belongs to Viktor Frankl's logotherapy. Viktor Frankl (1946), a Viennese psychiatrist who survived the Nazi camps and wrote Man's Search for Meaning, founded logotherapy, an existential therapy built on the will to meaning. He described two special techniques. In paradoxical intention the person deliberately wishes for the feared symptom, which breaks the cycle of anticipatory anxiety. In de-reflection the person is helped to stop watching himself. Frankl argued that problems such as insomnia and sexual difficulties are made worse by hyper-reflection, anxious attention to one's own performance; de-reflection turns attention away from the self and the symptom towards meaningful tasks and other people. Its basis is existential, not learning theory. Behaviour therapy, by contrast, applies the laws of classical and operant conditioning. Systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) pairs relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared scenes. Flooding, close to the implosive therapy of Thomas Stampfl (1967), exposes the client to the most feared situation at full strength until anxiety falls. Counterconditioning, first shown by Mary Cover Jones (1924), replaces fear with a response incompatible with it. Example: Arjun lies awake each night checking whether he is falling asleep yet, which keeps him awake. Using de-reflection, his therapist asks him to stop monitoring his sleep and to spend the time planning the story he is writing for his niece; sleep comes once he stops chasing it. Option b is right because de-reflection comes from existential logotherapy, while the other three rest on conditioning. Exam trap: behaviour therapists borrowed Frankl's paradoxical intention for insomnia, so his methods can look behavioural, and 'reflection' may recall Carl Rogers' (1951) reflection of feeling; neither makes de-reflection a behaviour therapy technique.", "topic": "Logotherapy and de-reflection", "fig": "Viktor Frankl"}, {"q": "Token Economy is an influential intervention based upon:", "options": {"a": "Operant conditioning.", "b": "Implicit learning.", "c": "Classical conditioning.", "d": "Client centred therapy."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the token economy is based on operant conditioning. Operant conditioning, set out by B. F. Skinner (1938) in The Behavior of Organisms, holds that behaviour is shaped by its consequences: a behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more frequent. A token economy applies this to a whole group, such as a hospital ward, a classroom or a hostel. Staff define clear target behaviours (making one's bed, attending a group, finishing homework), and each time one occurs the person receives a token: a point, a star or a plastic chip. Tokens have no value in themselves; they are secondary (conditioned) reinforcers that are later exchanged for backup reinforcers such as snacks, outings or privileges. Because they can buy many things they act as generalised reinforcers, like money, and they bridge the delay between a behaviour and the final reward. Tokens can also be taken back for problem behaviour, which is response cost. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) ran the classic programme at Anna State Hospital, Illinois, where long-stay psychiatric patients earned tokens for self-care and ward jobs. When tokens were handed out regardless of work, the work almost stopped; when the contingency returned, so did the work. Example: in a children's home, Kabir earns a star each morning he is dressed and ready for school on time, and ten stars buy an extra hour of cricket on Sunday. Option a is right because the method works through contingent reinforcement, the heart of operant conditioning. Exam trap: tokens gain their value by being paired with backup rewards, which sounds like classical pairing, but the programme as a whole changes voluntary behaviour through its consequences, so it is operant; and Skinner supplied the principles, while Ayllon and Azrin built the token economy.", "topic": "Token economy", "fig": "Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin"}, {"q": "Citing only positive attributes in a self-report measure of personality is a phenomenon referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Social desirability.", "b": "Amplifying.", "c": "Projecting.", "d": "Self-deception."}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: citing only positive attributes in a self-report measure of personality is called social desirability. Self-report inventories ask people to describe themselves, so a score can reflect how a person wants to look as well as what they are really like. Social desirability is the tendency to choose the answers that present oneself favourably, endorsing good qualities and denying faults, often without any deliberate intent. Allen Edwards (1957) showed that how socially desirable an item is strongly predicts whether people endorse it, so the bias can swamp the trait being measured. Douglas Crowne and David Marlowe (1960) built the Marlowe-Crowne Social Desirability Scale from items describing behaviour that is admirable but rare, such as never having disliked anyone, which almost nobody can honestly claim. Delroy Paulhus (1984) split the tendency into two parts: impression management, a conscious effort to look good to others, and self-deceptive enhancement, an honestly held but inflated view of oneself. Test makers control it with forced-choice items matched for desirability, as in the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule, with validity scales such as the MMPI's L (lie) and K (correction) scales, and with assurances of confidentiality. Its deliberate form is faking good; the reverse, exaggerating problems for gain, is faking bad or malingering. Example: Riya, filling in a personality questionnaire for a job she badly wants, marks that she is never late, never loses her temper and always finishes her work. Option a is right because social desirability is the established name for this one-sided positive self-presentation. Exam trap: self-deception (option d) is a real term, but it is only one of Paulhus's two components; the umbrella name for citing only positive attributes is social desirability.", "topic": "Social desirability response bias", "fig": "Allen Edwards"}, {"q": "Functional analysis is a therapy based on:", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning.", "b": "Humanistic principles.", "c": "Operant conditioning.", "d": "Psychodynamic principles."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: functional analysis is based on operant conditioning. Operant conditioning, set out by B. F. Skinner (1938), holds that behaviour is kept going by its consequences. Functional analysis asks what a problem behaviour does for the person: what it gets them, or what it lets them escape. Its simplest form is the ABC model. The antecedent is what happens just before the behaviour (a demand, a setting, a cue); the behaviour is defined so precisely that two observers would agree when it occurs; and the consequence is what follows and maintains it (attention, escape, a tangible reward or sensory stimulation). Frederick Kanfer and George Saslow (1965) widened the model to include the person's own state. Brian Iwata and colleagues (1982) made it experimental: they placed children who injured themselves in conditions offering attention, escape from tasks, being alone or play, and saw which condition raised the behaviour, revealing its function. Treatment then changes the contingencies: the payoff for the problem behaviour is removed (extinction), and a better behaviour that earns the same payoff is taught. Example: Kabir, aged seven, throws a tantrum whenever homework is set, and his mother then lets him watch television instead. The analysis shows that the tantrum is maintained by escape from homework, so she keeps the task in place, allows short breaks only when he asks calmly, and praises him for starting work. Option c is right because the analysis rests on the reinforcing consequences of behaviour, which is operant conditioning. Exam trap: antecedents can look like the stimuli of classical conditioning, but in functional analysis they are discriminative stimuli that signal when a behaviour will be reinforced; the consequences carry the explanation.", "topic": "Functional analysis (ABC model)", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Procedural memory can be affected by damage to the:", "options": {"a": "Cerebrum.", "b": "Cerebellum.", "c": "Motor cortex.", "d": "Limbic system."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: procedural memory can be affected by damage to the cerebellum. Procedural memory is memory for how to do things: motor skills such as cycling, typing or playing an instrument, and habits learned by repetition. It is a kind of implicit (nondeclarative) memory, shown in better performance rather than in conscious recall, and it is separate from declarative memory for facts and events, which depends on the hippocampus. The best evidence comes from patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). After William Scoville removed his medial temporal lobes in 1953 he could form no new conscious memories, yet Brenda Milner (1962) found that he improved day by day at mirror drawing, tracing a star while watching his hand in a mirror, though each time he denied having done the task before. His cerebellum and basal ganglia were intact. The cerebellum compares intended with actual movement and corrects the error, so it is vital for learning and timing motor skills; Richard Thompson (1980s) showed that eyeblink conditioning depends on it. Damage causes ataxia and poor motor learning. Example: after a cerebellar stroke, Meera, a tabla player, can still describe every rhythm she knows, but her strokes become uneven and she struggles to relearn even simple patterns. Option b is right because the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie most closely to procedural skill learning. Exam trap: the limbic system (hippocampus) is the memory answer for facts and events, not for skills; H.M. is the proof. Key note: procedural memory depends mainly on the basal ganglia (striatum) and the cerebellum, with the motor cortex involved; the basal ganglia are not offered, and the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie to motor skill learning (H.M. still learned mirror drawing), so b stands.", "topic": "Procedural memory and cerebellum", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term IQ was invented by:", "options": {"a": "Terman.", "b": "Allport.", "c": "William Stern.", "d": "Watson."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the term intelligence quotient (IQ) was invented by William Stern. The story begins in Paris. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test to pick out children who needed special schooling, using reasoning and judgement items of rising difficulty. In their 1908 revision the items were grouped by age, and a child's mental age was the age level of the items he or she could pass. A mental age alone is hard to compare across ages: being two years behind means far more at 5 than at 15. The German psychologist William Stern (1912) solved this by dividing mental age by chronological age, a ratio he called the intelligence quotient (Intelligenzquotient). Lewis Terman (1916) at Stanford University adapted the Binet scale for American children as the Stanford-Binet, multiplied Stern's ratio by 100 to remove the decimal, and made the abbreviation IQ famous. So ratio IQ = (mental age ÷ chronological age) × 100. Example: Arjun, aged 10, passes the items typical of 12-year-olds, so his ratio IQ is 12 ÷ 10 × 100 = 120. The ratio fails for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ, which has a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15 at every age. Option c is right because Stern coined both the quotient and the term. Exam trap: Terman is the trap, since his Stanford-Binet spread IQ testing and the abbreviation across the world. Key note: William Stern (1912) coined the intelligence quotient (mental age over chronological age); Lewis Terman (1916, Stanford-Binet) multiplied it by 100 and popularised the abbreviation IQ, so Terman is the trap; Stern stands.", "topic": "Origin of the term IQ", "fig": "William Stern"}, {"q": "Skinner box is also known as:", "options": {"a": "Simple latch.", "b": "Operant chamber.", "c": "Omission training.", "d": "Black box."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Skinner box is also known as the operant chamber. B. F. Skinner (1938), in The Behavior of Organisms, needed a way to study operant behaviour, which an animal emits freely rather than as a reflex to a stimulus. His operant conditioning chamber is a small, sound-shielded box with a response device (a lever for a rat, a lit key for a pigeon), a dispenser that delivers food or water as the reinforcer, and often lights, a speaker or an electrified floor for cues or punishment. Every response is logged on a cumulative recorder, which plots the rate of responding over time, so schedules of reinforcement, shaping, extinction and discrimination can be measured precisely. Unlike Edward Thorndike's (1898) puzzle box, where a cat escaped once per trial, the chamber lets the animal respond again and again, so response rate becomes the main measure. Skinner himself preferred the name operant chamber to the nickname. Example: in a college laboratory, Riya watches a rat press a lever for a food pellet; within an hour its cumulative record climbs steeply. Option b is right because operant chamber is the formal name for the Skinner box. Exam trap: every distractor is a real term from learning theory. A simple latch was the release in Thorndike's puzzle box, omission training is a punishment procedure, and the 'black box' is the behaviourists' name for the unobservable mind, not an apparatus.", "topic": "Skinner box (operant chamber)", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Desensitisation is primarily used for:", "options": {"a": "Diminishing hallucinations and delusions.", "b": "Hysteria.", "c": "Treating depression.", "d": "Alleviating phobias."}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: desensitisation is used mainly to alleviate phobias. Systematic desensitisation was developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958) in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition. It rests on classical conditioning: a phobia is a learned fear, so it can be unlearned. The central principle is reciprocal inhibition, the idea that two incompatible states, such as deep relaxation and anxiety, cannot occur at the same time. Treatment has three steps. First, the client learns progressive muscle relaxation, the method of Edmund Jacobson (1929). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a ladder of feared situations rated from least to most frightening on Wolpe's 0 to 100 Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale. Third, the relaxed client imagines each step in turn and moves up only when the current step no longer arouses anxiety. The roots go back to Mary Cover Jones (1924), who removed little Peter's fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit closer while he ate. Example: Meera fears dogs. She first pictures the word 'dog', then a puppy across the road, then a dog on a lead nearby, and finally stroking one, relaxing at every step until the fear fades. Graded exposure, in imagination (in vitro) or in real life (in vivo), is still a first-line treatment for specific phobias. Option d is right because desensitisation was designed for, and works best on, specific learned fears such as phobias. Exam trap: examiners offer 'hysteria' because it sounds like an anxiety problem; hysteria (now conversion and dissociative disorders) was the classic target of hypnosis and the Freudian talking cure, not of graded relaxation. Also keep desensitisation apart from flooding, which puts the client at the top of the hierarchy at once, with no relaxation.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques is a client-centered therapist most likely to use?", "options": {"a": "Analysis.", "b": "Giving advice.", "c": "Reflection.", "d": "Aversive conditioning."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: a client-centred therapist is most likely to use reflection. Carl Rogers (1942, 1951) built client-centred, later person-centred, therapy on the belief that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow, and that distress arises when the self-concept clashes with experience (incongruence). The therapist therefore does not diagnose, interpret or instruct. Instead, Rogers (1957) named the core conditions that let clients heal themselves: empathy (sensing the client's inner world as if it were one's own), unconditional positive regard (accepting the client without judgement) and congruence or genuineness (being real, not hiding behind a professional role). Reflection is the technique that shows empathy in action. The therapist restates the feeling behind the client's words, so the client feels understood and explores further. Example: Kabir says, 'My father keeps comparing me with my cousin.' The therapist replies, 'You sound hurt, and maybe tired of never being enough for him.' No advice, no theory; the direction stays with Kabir. This is why Rogers first called the approach non-directive. Option c is right because reflection of feeling is the signature, non-directive skill of the client-centred method. Exam trap: examiners surround reflection with directive look-alikes. Analysis and interpretation belong to Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, giving advice to directive counselling, and aversive conditioning to behaviour therapy; only reflection keeps the locus of evaluation inside the client.", "topic": "Client-centred reflection", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "In which disorder is the comprehension or the expression of language impaired?", "options": {"a": "Apraxia.", "b": "Agnosia.", "c": "Aphagia.", "d": "Aphasia."}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: aphasia is the disorder in which the comprehension or expression of language is impaired. Aphasia (Greek a, without, and phasis, speech) is an acquired loss of language caused by brain damage, most often a stroke in the left hemisphere, which is dominant for language in about 95% of right-handers. It affects language itself, not just the muscles of speech, so reading, writing and naming usually suffer too. Paul Broca (1861) described expressive (non-fluent) aphasia after damage to the left inferior frontal lobe: halting, effortful speech with fair comprehension. Carl Wernicke (1874) described receptive (fluent) aphasia after damage to the left superior temporal lobe: fluent but empty speech with poor understanding. Global aphasia hits both. Example: after a stroke, Arjun's grandfather understands every word of the news but can only say 'water... go... no', a Broca-type aphasia. Option d is right because only aphasia names a disorder of language comprehension or expression. Exam trap: the four 'a-' words look alike, so learn the root of each. Apraxia (praxis, action) is loss of skilled, purposeful movement despite normal strength; agnosia (gnosis, knowledge) is failure to recognise objects despite working senses; aphagia (phagein, to eat) is inability to swallow. Do not mix aphasia with dysarthria either, which is slurred articulation from weak speech muscles with language intact.", "topic": "Aphasia and look-alike disorders", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Hallucinations\" are examples of disorders of:", "options": {"a": "Judgement.", "b": "Perception.", "c": "Intelligence.", "d": "Thinking."}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: hallucinations are disorders of perception. Descriptive psychopathology, laid out by Karl Jaspers (1913) in General Psychopathology, sorts symptoms by the mental function they disturb: consciousness, perception, thought (form and content), mood, memory and so on. A hallucination is a perception without an object: the person sees, hears, smells, tastes or feels something when no matching external stimulus is present, yet it is as vivid as a normal perception and seems to come from outside. Jean-Etienne Esquirol (1838) gave this modern definition and separated it from an illusion, which is a misperception of a real stimulus (a coat on a hook seen as a man). Auditory hallucinations, such as voices commenting on the person, are the commonest in schizophrenia; visual ones point more towards delirium or other organic causes. Example: Riya hears a man's voice calling her name in an empty room every night. Nothing in the room produced the sound, so it is a hallucination, a fault in perception. Option b is right because a hallucination is by definition a false perception, so in a mental status examination it is recorded under perception. Exam trap: examiners offer 'thinking' because hallucinations and delusions travel together in psychosis. A delusion is a false, fixed belief, a disorder of thought content; a hallucination is a false perception. Judgement (deciding how to act) and intelligence are separate headings altogether.", "topic": "Hallucination as perceptual disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Counterconditioning is an exposure therapy technique which involves:", "options": {"a": "Learning that one negative event may be linked to another.", "b": "Exposing the client to the feared stimulus.", "c": "Learning that an event or situation is no longer threatening.", "d": "Helping the client to see that their behaviour is counterproductive."}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: counterconditioning involves learning that an event or situation is no longer threatening. Counterconditioning is a behaviour therapy method built on the classical conditioning of Ivan Pavlov (Conditioned Reflexes, 1927). A feared stimulus is paired, again and again, with a new response that cannot coexist with fear, such as relaxation or eating something pleasant. The new response gradually replaces the old fear, so the stimulus stops signalling danger. The first clear case came from Mary Cover Jones (1924), a student of John B. Watson. She treated Peter, a boy of almost three who feared rabbits: while he ate a favourite food, a caged rabbit was brought a little closer each day until he let it nibble his fingers. Joseph Wolpe (1958) named the principle reciprocal inhibition and turned it into systematic desensitisation, pairing a graded hierarchy of feared scenes with deep muscle relaxation. Example: Kabir panics in lifts. While practising slow breathing and relaxation, he first stands near a lift, then rides one floor with his brother, then rides alone; calm replaces panic, and he learns that lifts are safe. Option c is right because the end point of counterconditioning is a changed meaning: the once-feared situation now brings calm and is learned to be harmless. Exam trap: option b, exposing the client to the feared stimulus, is the shared ingredient of every exposure therapy, flooding included; it is necessary but does not define counterconditioning. Key note: b describes what every exposure therapy does, so it is not false in plain terms; counterconditioning adds a new response incompatible with fear (relaxation, eating) so that the client learns the situation is no longer threatening, and c stands.", "topic": "Counterconditioning", "fig": "Mary Cover Jones"}, {"q": "Loss of insight is a feature of:", "options": {"a": "Anxiety.", "b": "Depression.", "c": "Schizophrenia.", "d": "Malingering."}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: loss of insight is a feature of schizophrenia. In the mental status examination, insight means the person's awareness that he is ill, that the symptoms come from his own mind, and that he needs treatment. It is graded on six levels, from (1) complete denial, through (3) awareness blamed on others or on physical causes and (5) intellectual insight, to (6) true emotional insight that changes behaviour. Poor insight is one of the commonest features of schizophrenia and other psychoses. The World Health Organization's International Pilot Study of Schizophrenia (1973) found lack of insight in about 97% of patients, the most frequent single symptom. Xavier Amador and colleagues (1991) likened it to anosognosia, the unawareness of deficit that Joseph Babinski (1914) described after right-hemisphere stroke, and poor insight predicts poor adherence to medication. Example: Arjun believes his neighbours are beaming thoughts into his head. When his sister suggests a doctor, he says, 'I am not ill, they are the problem.' That is insight grade 1. Option c is right because psychosis typically removes the ability to recognise one's own symptoms as illness. Exam trap: this is the old neurosis versus psychosis split. In anxiety and most depression (the old neuroses) the person knows something is wrong and often seeks help, so insight is kept; severe psychotic depression can lose it, but that is not the typical picture. Malingering is deliberate faking of symptoms for gain, so the person knows exactly what is going on.", "topic": "Insight in psychosis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The main function of the ascending reticular activating system is:", "options": {"a": "Movement coordination.", "b": "Regulation of various degrees of arousal.", "c": "Regulation of motivated behaviours like hunger and thirst.", "d": "Understanding pressure and pain sensation."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the main function of the ascending reticular activating system is the regulation of various degrees of arousal. The reticular formation (Latin reticulum, little net) is a net-like column of neurons running through the core of the medulla, pons and midbrain. Its ascending part, the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS), projects through the thalamus and directly to the whole cortex and sets the overall level of alertness, from coma and deep sleep through drowsiness to full wakefulness and focused attention. It also filters input, which is why a mother may sleep through traffic noise yet wake at her baby's faint cry. Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949) showed this in cats: electrical stimulation of the brainstem reticular formation switched the EEG from the slow, synchronised waves of sleep to the fast, desynchronised pattern of alertness, while damage to the system leaves an animal or a person in coma. Hans Eysenck (1967) later used ARAS arousal to explain introversion and extraversion. Example: Meera, dozing in a lecture, jolts awake when her name is called; her ARAS has raised cortical arousal in an instant. Option b is right because the ARAS acts as the brain's arousal dial, grading states of wakefulness. Exam trap: each distractor is the job of a nearby structure. Movement coordination belongs to the cerebellum, hunger and thirst to the hypothalamus, and pressure and pain to the dorsal column and spinothalamic pathways that reach the somatosensory cortex.", "topic": "Reticular activating system", "fig": "Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun"}, {"q": "Damage to Wernicke's area, situated in the left temporal lobe, causes:", "options": {"a": "Impaired understanding of speech and written language.", "b": "Impaired vision.", "c": "Impaired speech production and articulation.", "d": "Impaired movement."}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: damage to Wernicke's area causes impaired understanding of speech and written language. Carl Wernicke (1874), a German neurologist, described patients with damage to the back of the left superior temporal gyrus, next to the primary auditory cortex. This area links speech sounds to their meanings. When it is damaged the result is Wernicke's (receptive, fluent or sensory) aphasia. Speech flows easily with normal rhythm, but it is empty or makes little sense: words are swapped (paraphasias), new words are made up (neologisms) and, in severe cases, the result is word salad. The person understands little of what is said or read and is often unaware of the errors. Compare Paul Broca (1861), whose patient Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan', had damage to the left inferior frontal lobe: Broca's aphasia gives slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with fairly good understanding. Norman Geschwind (1965) linked the two areas through the arcuate fasciculus. Example: after a stroke, Kabir's grandmother chats fluently but says, 'The blue glass went to the doctor's cooking,' and cannot follow a simple request to close the door. Option a is right because Wernicke's area is the comprehension area, so its loss hits the understanding of spoken and written language. Exam trap: examiners swap Broca and Wernicke. Remember 'Broca, broken speech (production); Wernicke, wordy but without meaning (comprehension)'. Impaired articulation (option c) is Broca's aphasia or dysarthria.", "topic": "Wernicke's aphasia", "fig": "Carl Wernicke"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a component of executive function?", "options": {"a": "Working memory.", "b": "Set shifting.", "c": "Contralateral neglect.", "d": "Response inhibition."}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: contralateral neglect is not a component of executive function. Executive functions are the top-down control processes, based mainly in the prefrontal cortex, that let us plan, stay on task, switch goals and stop ourselves acting on impulse. Akira Miyake and colleagues (2000) gave adults a battery of tasks and found three related but separable core components: updating (holding and refreshing information in working memory), shifting (switching flexibly between tasks or mental sets) and inhibition (suppressing a strong but wrong response). Classic tests match each one: the n-back for working memory, the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (David Grant and Esta Berg, 1948) for set shifting, and the Stroop test (John Ridley Stroop, 1935) for response inhibition. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that frontal patients keep sorting cards by an old rule, the perseveration of the dysexecutive syndrome. Contralateral, or hemispatial, neglect is something different. After damage, usually to the right parietal lobe, the person ignores the opposite (left) side of space: eats from only the right half of the plate, shaves half the face and crowds all the numbers onto the right of a clock drawing. It is a disorder of spatial attention, not of executive control. Example: Riya's grandfather, after a right parietal stroke, keeps bumping into the left door frame, yet he plans his day and switches tasks normally. Option c is right because neglect is a parietal attention syndrome, outside the frontal executive triad. Exam trap: 'contralateral' sounds technical and brain-related, so students assume it belongs; ask whether the item is a control process (it is not) and which lobe it involves (parietal, not prefrontal).", "topic": "Components of executive function", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Apparent motion seen in movies and on television is:", "options": {"a": "Real motion.", "b": "Visual deprivation.", "c": "Stroboscopic motion.", "d": "Induced movement."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the apparent motion seen in movies and on television is stroboscopic motion. A film is a series of still pictures, each slightly different from the last. Cinema shows about 24 frames a second (television about 25 to 30), and the brain fills the gaps so that we see smooth, continuous movement where there is none. This is one kind of apparent motion, the perception of movement when no object actually moves. Its laboratory version is the phi phenomenon of Max Wertheimer (1912): two lines flashed one after the other in different places, at the right interval (about 60 milliseconds), look like a single line moving from one place to the other. That finding launched Gestalt psychology, because the movement exists in the whole pattern, not in either flash. Example: Arjun makes a flip book, drawing a stick figure with its arm slightly higher on each page; flicked quickly, the figure seems to wave. Option c is right because films and television create motion purely by the rapid succession of still frames, which is the definition of stroboscopic motion. Exam trap: induced movement (option d) is also apparent motion but has a different cause: a still object seems to move because its frame moves, as when the moon seems to race through drifting clouds. The autokinetic effect, a still light in a dark room that seems to wander, which Muzafer Sherif (1935) used to study group norms, is a third type.", "topic": "Stroboscopic motion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Those who argue that innate or inborn processes affect perception are called:", "options": {"a": "Nativist.", "b": "Gestaltist.", "c": "Behaviourist.", "d": "Psychoanalyst."}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: those who argue that innate or inborn processes shape perception are called nativists. The nativism versus empiricism debate is one of psychology's oldest questions: is the ability to see depth, form and size built in, or learned from experience? Nativists hold that the mind arrives with inborn structures. Rene Descartes (1641) wrote of innate ideas, and Immanuel Kant (1781) argued that space and time are built-in forms through which all experience is organised. Empiricists answer that perception is learned: John Locke (1690) called the newborn mind a tabula rasa, a blank slate, and Hermann von Helmholtz (1867) described perception as unconscious inference from past experience. The best-known evidence for the nativist side is the visual cliff of Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960): most crawling infants aged 6 to 14 months refused to cross a glass sheet over an apparent drop, even when their mothers called, and newborn chicks and goats avoided the deep side at once. Example: baby Meera, just crawling, stops at the edge of the stairs without ever having fallen; a nativist says her sense of depth was inborn. Option a is right because 'nativist' is the precise label for the innate, inborn position. Exam trap: the Gestalt psychologists (Max Wertheimer, 1912) leaned nativist, holding that the brain organises wholes by built-in laws, so 'Gestaltist' is the near miss; but the general position is called nativism, and Gestalt is one school that drew on it. Behaviourists were radical empiricists.", "topic": "Nativism in perception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which construct from Sternberg's triarchic theory of intelligence includes the ability to find the best fit between the individual and the environment?", "options": {"a": "Creative.", "b": "Practical.", "c": "Incremental.", "d": "Linguistic."}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: practical intelligence is the triarchic construct that includes finding the best fit between the individual and the environment. Robert Sternberg (1985), in Beyond IQ, proposed that intelligence has three linked parts. Analytical (componential) intelligence is the ability to analyse, compare and evaluate, the skill that ordinary IQ and school tests measure; it runs on metacomponents (planning and monitoring), performance components and knowledge-acquisition components. Creative (experiential) intelligence is the ability to cope with novelty and to make familiar tasks automatic. Practical (contextual) intelligence is 'street smarts': using one's abilities in everyday life through three moves, adapting to the environment, shaping it to suit oneself, or selecting a new one when the first does not fit. Much of it rests on tacit knowledge, the unspoken know-how of a job or setting. Example: Riya joins a busy Mumbai office. First she adjusts to its pace (adapting); then she persuades her manager to let her work early hours (shaping); finally, finding the culture still wrong for her, she moves to a smaller firm (selecting). Option b is right because adapting to, shaping and selecting environments for the best person-environment fit is exactly how Sternberg defines practical intelligence. Exam trap: students confuse creative with practical because both sound like real-world skills; creative intelligence is about novelty and insight, practical intelligence about fitting one's context. 'Linguistic' comes from Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences, and 'incremental' from Carol Dweck's theories of intelligence, not from Sternberg.", "topic": "Sternberg's practical intelligence", "fig": "Robert Sternberg"}, {"q": "Which one of the following is NOT an example of cognitive learning?", "options": {"a": "Cognitive maps.", "b": "Programmed learning.", "c": "Latent learning.", "d": "Insight learning."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: programmed learning is not an example of cognitive learning. Cognitive learning theories hold that learning changes mental representations and understanding, not just stimulus-response bonds, and that it can happen without reinforcement or any visible behaviour. Three classic examples fill the other options. Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930) found that rats allowed to explore a maze without food learned it anyway; once food was added they ran it as well as rats rewarded all along (latent learning). Edward Tolman (1948) argued that the rats had formed a cognitive map, a mental layout of the maze. Wolfgang Kohler (1917) watched the chimpanzee Sultan suddenly join two sticks to reach a banana, insight learning by restructuring the whole problem. Programmed learning comes from the opposite camp. B. F. Skinner (1954), in The Science of Learning and the Art of Teaching, applied operant conditioning to the classroom: material is broken into small steps (frames), the learner responds actively at each step, gets immediate feedback that acts as reinforcement, and moves at his own pace. Teaching machines and many computer drills follow this design. Example: Kabir learns spellings on an app that shows one word at a time and flashes 'Correct!' the moment he answers; that is programmed learning, behaviourist through and through. Option b is right because programmed learning rests on Skinner's operant reinforcement, not on cognitive maps or insight. Exam trap: because programmed learning is used in classrooms and seems mental, students file it under cognitive; check the mechanism instead, which is reinforcement of small responses.", "topic": "Programmed versus cognitive learning", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "The simplest form of thinking is:", "options": {"a": "Abstract thinking.", "b": "Logical thinking.", "c": "Creative thinking.", "d": "Perceptual thinking."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: perceptual thinking is the simplest form of thinking. Thinking is the mental handling of images, concepts and symbols to understand, solve problems or create. Many educational psychology texts used in India arrange the types of thinking as a ladder from simple to complex. At the bottom is perceptual or concrete thinking, which works directly on what the senses present here and now (objects, pictures, events), with little use of language or general ideas. Young children think this way, which echoes the stages of Jean Piaget (1952) in which thought is tied to what can be seen and handled. Next comes conceptual or abstract thinking, which uses general concepts and symbols that are not present, such as 'justice' or 'number'. Higher still is reflective or logical thinking, the careful, step-by-step reasoning that John Dewey (1910) described in How We Think, and at the top is creative thinking, which produces new and useful ideas, the divergent production of J. P. Guilford (1956). Example: five-year-old Meera counts toffees by touching each one (perceptual); her elder brother Arjun works out a sum written only in numbers (abstract). Option d is right because perceptual thinking needs only sense impressions and so comes first and simplest. Exam trap: 'abstract' can sound basic because it is vague, but it is a higher form; logical and creative thinking build on it. Key note: the ranking (perceptual or concrete thinking as the simplest, then conceptual or abstract, reflective or logical, creative) comes from Indian educational psychology texts such as S. K. Mangal, not from Western cognitive psychology, which does not rank thinking this way; perceptual thinking is the answer those texts give, and d stands.", "topic": "Types of thinking", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Self-monitoring is a form of clinical observation involving which of the following?", "options": {"a": "Clients measuring their own galvanic skin responses.", "b": "Clients measuring their own heart rate.", "c": "Clients observing and recording their own behaviour.", "d": "Clients recording their dreams."}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: self-monitoring is a form of clinical observation in which clients observe and record their own behaviour. In behavioural assessment the clinician wants a functional analysis of the problem: what happens just before it (antecedents), the behaviour itself, and what follows (consequences), the ABC model. Watching a client in the clinic shows only a thin slice of life, and many target behaviours (bingeing, purging, urges, worrying thoughts) are private or happen at home. So the client becomes the observer, keeping a structured diary, a counter or an app and noting each episode as it happens, with time, place, intensity, thoughts and what came next. Frederick Kanfer (1970) made self-monitoring the first step of self-regulation, followed by self-evaluation and self-reinforcement. It has a useful side effect called reactivity: recording a behaviour tends to change it, usually reducing unwanted acts and increasing wanted ones. That spoils a clean baseline but helps treatment. Example: Kabir, who smokes, writes down every cigarette, the time and the trigger; within a week he sees that most follow tea breaks with colleagues, and his count is already dropping. Option c is right because self-monitoring is defined as clients systematically observing and recording their own behaviour. Exam trap: two look-alikes. Clients measuring their own heart rate or galvanic skin response is psychophysiological recording, the basis of biofeedback (Neal Miller's operant control of autonomic responses, 1969). And the self-monitoring of Mark Snyder (1974) is a personality trait from social psychology, how much a person tailors self-presentation to the situation, not a clinical recording method.", "topic": "Self-monitoring in behavioural assessment", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The process by which a stimulus is attended to, identified, studied and incorporated into memory is called:", "options": {"a": "Storage.", "b": "Chunking.", "c": "Encoding.", "d": "Reasoning."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the process by which a stimulus is attended to, identified, studied and incorporated into memory is called encoding. Memory is usually described in three stages. Encoding turns incoming information into a form the memory system can hold, a mental code that may be visual (how it looks), acoustic (how it sounds) or semantic (what it means). Storage keeps that code over time, and retrieval brings it back when needed. Encoding needs attention: Raymond Nickerson and Marilyn Adams (1979) found that fewer than half of adults could pick out the correct drawing of a coin they had handled thousands of times, because the details had never been encoded. Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968) built the multi-store model on this flow: attention moves information from sensory memory into short-term memory, and rehearsal transfers it to long-term memory. Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972) showed that depth of encoding matters: judging a word's meaning gives better recall than judging its sound or typeface, the levels of processing effect. Example: Meera meets the term 'prosopagnosia', notices it, sounds it out, links it to the Greek prosopon (face) and thinks of her aunt who cannot recognise faces; each step is encoding, and the deeper links make it last. Option c is right because attending to, identifying and processing a stimulus so that it enters memory is exactly what encoding means. Exam trap: storage is the next stage, the holding of what has already been encoded, so students who latch on to 'incorporated into memory' pick it. Chunking (George Miller, 1956) is one strategy within encoding, and reasoning is a thinking process.", "topic": "Memory encoding", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Memories outside of conscious awareness are called:", "options": {"a": "Proactive memories.", "b": "Reactive memories.", "c": "Explicit Memories.", "d": "Implicit Memories."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: memories that operate outside conscious awareness are called implicit memories. Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) defined implicit memory as an influence of past experience on behaviour, feeling or thought without conscious recollection. Explicit memory is the opposite: you deliberately and consciously retrieve something, as when you recall a list or recognise a face, so it is tested by recall and recognition. Larry Squire (1992) used the parallel terms declarative (explicit) and nondeclarative (implicit) memory. Implicit memory includes procedural skills (typing, cycling), priming (a word seen earlier is completed faster on a word-fragment test even if you do not remember seeing it), classically conditioned responses and habituation. The strongest evidence comes from amnesia. Henry Molaison (patient H.M.) could not consciously remember new events after hippocampal surgery in 1953, yet Brenda Milner (1962) showed that he improved day by day at mirror drawing while insisting he had never done the task. So explicit memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe, while implicit memory relies on other systems such as the striatum and cerebellum. Example: Arjun types his email password perfectly every morning without thinking, but if asked to say the characters aloud he has to mime typing to recover them; his fingers hold an implicit memory. Option d is right because working outside conscious awareness is the defining feature of implicit memory. Exam trap: options a and b borrow from interference theory, where proactive interference (old learning disrupts new) and retroactive interference (new disrupts old) cause forgetting; 'proactive memories' and 'reactive memories' are not memory systems at all.", "topic": "Implicit versus explicit memory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The Bobo doll experiment, famously conducted in the 1960s, was done by:", "options": {"a": "James Lange.", "b": "Albert Bandura.", "c": "Daniel Kahneman.", "d": "Abraham Maslow."}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Bobo doll experiment was conducted by Albert Bandura. Albert Bandura, with Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), tested whether children learn aggression simply by watching. Seventy-two nursery-school children, aged about 3 to 6, were taken one at a time to a playroom where an adult model either attacked a large inflatable Bobo doll (punching it, hitting it with a mallet, kicking it and shouting 'Sock him in the nose!') or played quietly with toys; a control group saw no model. Later, after being mildly frustrated, each child was left with toys that included a Bobo doll. Children who had watched the aggressive model copied many of its exact acts and words, and boys showed more physical aggression than girls. No one had rewarded them. A 1963 follow-up showed that filmed and cartoon models worked almost as well as live ones, and a 1965 study showed that seeing the model punished reduced imitation but not learning: once offered a reward, those children reproduced the acts too. These studies founded social learning theory, later social cognitive theory, with its four processes of attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. Example: after watching an older cousin slam a door in anger, little Arjun slams his toy cupboard the same way the next day. Option b is right because Bandura designed and ran the Bobo doll studies at Stanford in the early 1960s. Exam trap: 'James Lange' is not one person but a merged label for the James-Lange theory of emotion (William James, 1884; Carl Lange, 1885), and the other two names are famous psychologists from unrelated fields.", "topic": "Bobo doll experiment", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "'Ethics' can be defined as the formulation of principles to:", "options": {"a": "Reduce inappropriate behaviour.", "b": "Punish inappropriate behaviour.", "c": "Reinforce appropriate behaviour.", "d": "Guide behaviour."}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: ethics is the formulation of principles that guide behaviour, telling people what they ought and ought not to do. The word comes from the Greek ethos, meaning character or custom. Ethics is wider than any one tool for changing behaviour: it is a set of standards of right conduct that people use to decide how to act, especially when interests conflict. Professional ethics turns these standards into codes. In research, modern codes grew out of abuse: the Nuremberg Code (1947) after Nazi experiments, the Declaration of Helsinki (1964) and the Belmont Report (1979), which named three principles: respect for persons (applied through informed consent), beneficence (do no harm and maximise benefit) and justice (fair selection of participants). Psychologists follow the American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016), and Indian researchers follow the Indian Council of Medical Research's National Ethical Guidelines (2017). These codes do not mainly punish or reward; they guide decisions in advance. Example: Meera plans to study anxiety in college students. Ethical principles guide her to obtain informed consent, tell students they may withdraw at any time, keep their answers confidential, and offer debriefing and help if a questionnaire upsets anyone. Option d is right because guiding behaviour is the purpose of ethical principles; reducing bad behaviour or rewarding good behaviour are at most side effects. Exam trap: the distractors borrow the language of operant conditioning (reinforce, punish, reduce behaviour), which makes them sound psychological and precise; but ethics is a guide to conduct, not a reinforcement schedule.", "topic": "Definition of ethics", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The most basic perceptual organisation is:", "options": {"a": "Shape constancy.", "b": "Depth perception.", "c": "Figure ground.", "d": "Law of similarity."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: figure-ground is the most basic perceptual organisation. When we look at a scene, the first thing the visual system does is split it into a figure, the object that stands out, and a ground, the background it stands against. Only after this split can elements be grouped, depth judged or shapes recognised. Edgar Rubin (1915), a Danish psychologist, described the rules: the figure looks thing-like, has a clear contour, seems nearer and is better remembered; the ground looks shapeless, seems to continue behind the figure and lies farther away. His famous Rubin's vase is a reversible figure: you see either a white vase or two dark faces in profile, never both at once, although the picture never changes. That shows the brain, not the stimulus, does the organising. The Gestalt psychologists, Max Wertheimer (1923) and Kurt Koffka (1935), built their grouping laws of proximity, similarity, closure and continuity on top of this basic split. Example: Riya reads black letters on a white page; the letters are figure and the page is ground. If the page were covered in busy patterns she would struggle to read, which is how camouflage works. Option c is right because figure-ground segregation comes first and every other organisation depends on it. Exam trap: the law of similarity is a real Gestalt principle, but it groups figures that have already been separated from the ground, and shape constancy and depth perception are later processes that need objects to exist first.", "topic": "Figure-ground organisation", "fig": "Edgar Rubin"}, {"q": "The degree of attraction among group members relates to the dimension of:", "options": {"a": "Compatibility.", "b": "Structure.", "c": "Cohesiveness.", "d": "Conformity."}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the degree of attraction among group members relates to the dimension of cohesiveness. Group cohesiveness is the sum of the forces that keep members in a group: liking for one another, commitment to the group's task and pride in belonging. Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950) defined it as the total field of forces acting on members to remain in the group, in their study of student housing at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, where friendships and shared opinions formed most among near neighbours. Cohesive groups have better attendance, more satisfied members and stronger pressure to conform. They perform better only when their norms favour hard work, and very high cohesion is one of the conditions for groupthink, described by Irving Janis (1972), in which a tight group suppresses doubt and makes poor decisions. Example: Kabir's college cricket team practises together, eats together and defends one another; members rarely quit and feel proud wearing the team cap, a sign of high cohesiveness. Option c is right because cohesiveness is specifically the attraction of members to one another and to the group. Exam trap: conformity sounds close, because cohesive groups produce more conformity; but conformity is a member's change in behaviour to match the group, an effect, while cohesiveness is the attraction itself. Structure refers to roles, status and norms, and compatibility to how well members' needs mesh.", "topic": "Group cohesiveness", "fig": ""}, {"q": "To reduce the Type I error, an experimenter:", "options": {"a": "Changes alpha level from 0.05 to 0.01.", "b": "Changes alpha level from 0.05 to 0.10.", "c": "Keeps beta levels constant.", "d": "Increases the sample size."}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: to reduce the Type I error, an experimenter changes the alpha level from 0.05 to 0.01. A Type I error is rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true: a false positive, claiming an effect that does not exist. Its probability is alpha, the significance level the researcher fixes before collecting data. With alpha at 0.05 the researcher accepts a 5% risk of a false positive whenever the null is true; at 0.01 the risk falls to 1%. The two-error framework comes from Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933). A Type II error is the reverse, keeping a false null (a false negative), with probability beta; power, 1 minus beta, is the chance of detecting a real effect. The two trade off: a stricter alpha makes the null harder to reject, so Type I risk falls but Type II risk rises unless the sample is enlarged. Example: Riya tests whether a memory app improves exam scores. If she demands p below 0.01 rather than 0.05, she is less likely to announce a benefit that is not there, but more likely to miss a small real one. Option a is right because the Type I error rate equals alpha, so lowering alpha lowers it directly. Exam trap: option d sounds good because bigger samples improve almost everything, but a larger sample raises power and cuts Type II error; the Type I rate stays at whatever alpha is set. Raising alpha to 0.10 (option b) doubles the Type I risk.", "topic": "Reducing Type I error", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jemas-2023": [{"q": "The 2 factor theory of motivation was given by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the two-factor theory of motivation was given by Frederick Herzberg (1959). Frederick Herzberg, with Bernard Mausner and Barbara Snyderman, published The Motivation to Work (1959) after interviewing about 200 engineers and accountants in Pittsburgh. He asked each person to recall a time they had felt exceptionally good or exceptionally bad about their job. The good stories and the bad stories were about different things, so he concluded that satisfaction and dissatisfaction are not two ends of one scale. Motivators (achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, advancement and growth) lie in the content of the job and create satisfaction. Hygiene factors (pay, supervision, company policy, relationships, working conditions and job security) lie in the context of the job; when they are poor they cause dissatisfaction, but improving them only removes the complaint and does not motivate. That is why the theory is also called the motivation-hygiene theory. Example: Riya gets a salary rise at her bank and stops grumbling, but she only starts staying late with enthusiasm when she is given her own project and praised for it. The pay was hygiene; the project and the praise were motivators. Option b is right because Frederick Herzberg is the author of the motivation-hygiene, or two-factor, theory of work motivation. Exam trap: \"two-factor\" is a label shared by several unrelated theories: Charles Spearman's two-factor theory of intelligence (1904), O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory of avoidance learning (1947) and Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's two-factor theory of emotion (1962). Read the field in the stem (motivation at work) before choosing the name.", "traps": {"a": "Douglas McGregor (1960), in The Human Side of Enterprise, described Theory X (workers dislike work and need control) and Theory Y (workers seek responsibility); these are a manager's assumptions about people, not two classes of job factors.", "c": "Abraham Maslow (1943) proposed a five-level hierarchy of needs from physiological to self-actualisation; it is one ladder of needs, not a split between motivators and hygiene factors, though Frederick Herzberg's motivators roughly match the upper levels of his hierarchy.", "d": "Victor Vroom (1964) gave expectancy theory, in which motivation depends on expectancy, instrumentality and valence; it is a cognitive calculation of effort and reward, not a two-factor model."}}, {"q": "The Gestalt school of psychology was founded in Germany by ______ and his colleagues Kurt Koffka & Wolfgang Kohler.", "concept": "The answer is b: Max Wertheimer (1912) founded Gestalt psychology in Germany with his colleagues Kurt Koffka and Wolfgang Kohler. The founding date is usually given as 1912, when Max Wertheimer published his study of apparent movement at Frankfurt, with Kurt Koffka and Wolfgang Kohler serving as his observers. He flashed two lines one after the other; at the right interval observers saw a single line moving from one place to the other. He called this the phi phenomenon. The movement is not present in either flash; it exists only in the pattern, so the whole cannot be built up from its parts. This was a direct attack on structuralism, which tried to break experience into elementary sensations. Gestalt (German for form or shape) psychology went on to describe the laws of perceptual organisation (figure-ground, proximity, similarity, closure, Pragnanz), Wolfgang Kohler's (1917) studies of insight in chimpanzees, Kurt Koffka's Principles of Gestalt Psychology (1935) and Kurt Lewin's field theory (1936). Example: when Arjun watches Diwali lights blinking in sequence along a balcony, he sees a light running along the wire, though each bulb only switches on and off in place. Option b is right because Max Wertheimer's phi phenomenon paper launched the school, and Kurt Koffka and Wolfgang Kohler were his co-founders. Exam trap: \"James Wundt\" never existed. The name blends William James with Wilhelm Wundt, who opened the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879 and whose elementism, carried to America by Edward Titchener (1898) as structuralism, is exactly what the Gestalt psychologists opposed. The German setting tempts students towards Wilhelm Wundt.", "traps": {"a": "There is no 'James Wundt'; the name garbles Wilhelm Wundt (1879), founder of the first laboratory at Leipzig and of the elementist approach that Edward Titchener called structuralism, which the Gestalt school rejected.", "c": "John B. Watson (1913) founded behaviourism in America with 'Psychology as the behaviorist views it'; he rejected the study of consciousness, whereas Gestalt psychology studied conscious perception as organised wholes.", "d": "John Dewey (1896) was an American functionalist at Chicago whose reflex arc paper studied what the mind does to adapt to the environment; he was not part of the German Gestalt group."}}, {"q": "The large complex structure at the back of the brain, also referred to as the 'little brain' is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: the 'little brain' at the back of the brain is the cerebellum. Cerebellum is Latin for little brain. It sits behind the pons and below the occipital lobes, and with the medulla and pons it forms the hindbrain. It makes up only about 10 percent of the brain's mass but holds more than half of its neurons, packed into a tightly folded surface. The cerebellum does not start movements (the motor cortex does); it makes them smooth and accurate by comparing the intended movement with the actual one and correcting the error. So it governs balance, posture, coordination, timing and motor skill learning, such as learning to ride a bicycle. Damage produces ataxia (an unsteady, wide-based walk), intention tremor and slurred speech. Alcohol depresses the cerebellum early, which is why police ask a suspected drunk driver to walk a straight line or touch the nose. Example: after a small stroke in his cerebellum, Kabir can still move his arm, but when he reaches for a cup his hand shakes and overshoots. Option a is right because only the cerebellum is called the little brain. Exam trap: students who remember the word 'coordination' sometimes pick the corpus callosum because it also links and 'coordinates' the two hemispheres; but it is a band of fibres in the middle of the brain, not a structure that controls movement.", "traps": {"b": "The corpus callosum is the thick band of about 200 million nerve fibres joining the left and right cerebral hemispheres; it lies deep in the middle of the brain, not at the back, and is not called the little brain.", "c": "The thalamus is the paired relay station in the centre of the brain (part of the diencephalon) that passes sensory signals, except smell, on to the cortex.", "d": "The hypothalamus is a small structure below the thalamus that regulates hunger, thirst, temperature, sexual behaviour and the pituitary gland; it is small and central, not a large structure at the back."}}, {"q": "A famous name associated with Existential therapy is:", "concept": "The answer is c: Irvin Yalom (1980) is the famous name associated with existential therapy. Existential therapy grows out of existential philosophy, from Soren Kierkegaard (1844) to Jean-Paul Sartre (1943). It holds that much anxiety comes not from childhood conflicts or faulty learning but from facing the basic conditions of being human. Irvin Yalom, an American psychiatrist at Stanford, set this out in Existential Psychotherapy (1980). He named four givens, or ultimate concerns: death (we will die), freedom (we are responsible for our choices and have no fixed structure to lean on), isolation (however close we are to others, we enter and leave life alone) and meaninglessness (life has no ready-made meaning, so we must create one). Symptoms are seen as defences against these concerns, and therapy helps the client face them honestly through a real, present relationship with the therapist. Other key names are Rollo May (1958), who brought existentialism into American psychology, and Viktor Frankl (1946), whose logotherapy centres on the will to meaning. Example: after a heart attack at 45, Arjun cannot sleep and works late every night; an existential therapist helps him see that his panic is about death and that he can now choose how to spend the years he has. Option c is right because Irvin Yalom wrote the standard textbook of existential psychotherapy and defined its four givens. Exam trap: Irvin Yalom is just as famous for group therapy, through The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy (1970) and its therapeutic factors, so students who know him only from groups may doubt him here; he belongs to both fields.", "traps": {"a": "John Dewey (1896) was an American philosopher of pragmatism, a founder of functionalism and of progressive education ('learning by doing'); he did not develop a therapy.", "b": "Alfred Adler (1912) founded individual psychology, built on inferiority feelings, striving for superiority, style of life and social interest; it is a neo-Freudian, goal-directed approach, not existential therapy.", "d": "Marsha Linehan (1993) developed dialectical behaviour therapy for borderline personality disorder and suicidal behaviour; it is a third-wave cognitive-behavioural therapy that combines skills training with mindfulness."}}, {"q": "The audible range of hearing for human beings is between 20 to ______ Hertz.", "concept": "The answer is d: the human ear hears frequencies from about 20 to 20,000 hertz. A sound's frequency (cycles per second, measured in hertz, Hz) is heard as pitch, its amplitude (measured in decibels, dB) as loudness, and its mixture of frequencies as timbre. A healthy young ear responds to about 20 to 20,000 Hz and is most sensitive in the middle of this band, roughly 2,000 to 5,000 Hz, where many speech sounds lie. Sounds below 20 Hz are infrasound; sounds above 20,000 Hz are ultrasound, which dogs, bats and dolphins can hear. The upper limit falls with age as the hair cells at the base of the cochlea, which code high pitches, wear out; this age-related hearing loss is called presbycusis. Example: Meera, aged 18, can hear the high whine of an old television that her grandfather, aged 70, cannot hear. Option d is right because 20,000 Hz is the standard upper limit of human hearing. Exam trap: the options mix up scales. 100 and 200 Hz are low pitches well inside our range (a typical man's voice sits near 120 Hz), while 150,000 Hz lies deep in the ultrasound range used by bats and dolphins.", "traps": {"a": "200 Hz is a low pitch, roughly the fundamental of a woman's speaking voice; it lies well inside the human range, far below its upper limit.", "b": "150,000 Hz is ultrasound, the range bats and dolphins use for echolocation; human ears cannot hear it at all.", "c": "100 Hz is a deep hum, like that of a large electrical transformer; it is near the bottom of the human range, not the top."}}, {"q": "Conservation or the ability to recognise that, although the shape of objects may change, the objects still maintain or conserve other characteristics that enable them to be recognized as the same is found in ____ stage of Piaget's theory.", "concept": "The answer is c: conservation appears in the concrete operational stage in the theory of Jean Piaget (1952). He described four stages: sensorimotor (birth to about 2), preoperational (about 2 to 7), concrete operational (about 7 to 11) and formal operational (from about 11 or 12). Conservation is the understanding that an amount stays the same when only its appearance changes. In the classic test, a child agrees that two identical glasses hold the same amount of water; then one is poured into a tall, thin glass. A preoperational child says the tall glass has more, because of centration (attending only to the height) and irreversibility (not mentally pouring the water back). A concrete operational child says it is the same and gives one of three reasons: identity (nothing was added or taken away), reversibility (pour it back and it will look the same) or compensation (it is taller but thinner). Conservation does not arrive all at once: number comes first, around 6 to 7, then liquid and mass, then weight around 9 to 10 and volume only near 11 to 12, a lag Jean Piaget called horizontal decalage. Example: five-year-old Kabir thinks a flattened clay ball has more clay; his eight-year-old sister Meera laughs and says it is the same clay, just spread out. Option c is right because conservation is the hallmark achievement of the concrete operational stage. Exam trap: examiners describe the failure of conservation (centration, being fooled by appearance) and ask for the stage; that is preoperational, not concrete operational. Key note: in the official booklet options a and d are printed identically ('Sensorimotor stage'); one was probably meant to be the preoperational stage. It does not change the answer; c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) is when infants learn through senses and actions and achieve object permanence; they have no mental operations yet, so conservation is far beyond them.", "b": "The formal operational stage (from about 11 or 12) adds abstract, hypothetical and systematic reasoning, as in the pendulum problem; conservation is already in place before it begins.", "d": "This repeats option a: the sensorimotor stage belongs to infancy and object permanence, not conservation; the misprint probably replaced 'preoperational', the stage whose children fail conservation tests."}}, {"q": "AUDIT is a simple screening tool developed by ____ to assess harmful use, hazardous use and dependence of alcohol.", "concept": "The answer is a: AUDIT, the Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test, was developed by the World Health Organization. The World Health Organization (WHO) built it as a brief screen that primary care workers in any country could use, and published it in 1989 (manual by Thomas Babor and colleagues); the six-country validation study by John Saunders and colleagues appeared in 1993. It has 10 questions, each scored 0 to 4, giving a total of 0 to 40. Items 1 to 3 ask about consumption (how often, how much, how often six or more drinks at once), which signals hazardous use; items 4 to 6 ask about dependence (cannot stop once started, drinking in the morning); and items 7 to 10 ask about alcohol-related harm (guilt, blackouts, injuries, others' concern), which signals harmful use. A score of 8 or more suggests hazardous or harmful drinking, and 20 or more suggests possible dependence and calls for specialist assessment. A three-item version, AUDIT-C, uses only the consumption questions. Example: at a health camp, Arjun, a 35-year-old driver, scores 14 on the AUDIT; the counsellor gives him a brief intervention on cutting down rather than a dependence referral. Option a is right because AUDIT is a WHO instrument, designed for international primary care screening. Exam trap: the distractors are all real organisations that work on alcohol and drugs, and three are American, which makes them sound plausible. Other screens are also confused with it: CAGE (John Ewing, 1984) has four yes or no items, and the Michigan Alcoholism Screening Test (Melvin Selzer, 1971) was developed in the United States.", "traps": {"b": "The American Psychological Association is the professional body of US psychologists that publishes journals, an ethics code and a style manual; it did not develop the AUDIT (and the DSM comes from the American Psychiatric Association).", "c": "The National Institute on Drug Abuse is a US research institute focused mainly on illicit drugs; it funds research and has adapted brief drug screens, but the AUDIT is a WHO alcohol tool.", "d": "The Center for Substance Abuse Treatment is part of the US Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration and publishes treatment guidelines; it is not the author of the AUDIT."}}, {"q": "The book called \"The Myth of Mental Illness\" was written by ______.", "concept": "The answer is b: The Myth of Mental Illness (1961) was written by Thomas Szasz. Thomas Szasz, a Hungarian-born American psychiatrist, first argued the case in a 1960 paper and then in this book. His claim was that 'mental illness' is a metaphor. A true disease, he said, is a bodily lesion that a pathologist can find; most psychiatric diagnoses are labels for 'problems in living', ways of behaving that society finds troubling. Calling them illnesses, he argued, lets psychiatry control people, justifies involuntary hospitalisation and the insanity defence, and takes away people's responsibility for their own choices. He wanted psychiatry to be a voluntary, contractual service between consenting adults. The book became a founding text of the anti-psychiatry movement of the 1960s, alongside the work of R. D. Laing (1960) and Michel Foucault (1961), though Thomas Szasz himself rejected that label. His view sits with the sociocultural and labelling critiques of the medical model, such as Thomas Scheff's labelling theory (1966). Example: where a psychiatrist diagnoses Riya's loud, unusual behaviour as an illness needing compulsory treatment, Thomas Szasz would call it a problem in living and insist that Riya herself decides whether to seek help. Option b is right because Thomas Szasz is the author of The Myth of Mental Illness. Exam trap: examiners place other famous critics from the same era beside him; R. D. Laing wrote The Divided Self (1960) and Michel Foucault wrote Madness and Civilization, so learn each critic with his own book.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Jung (1921) founded analytical psychology and wrote Psychological Types; he worked within psychiatry and never denied that mental illness exists.", "c": "Karen Horney (1937) wrote The Neurotic Personality of Our Time and later Neurosis and Human Growth (1950), on basic anxiety and neurotic needs; she treated neurosis as real and shaped by culture, not as a myth.", "d": "William James (1890) wrote The Principles of Psychology and The Varieties of Religious Experience (1902); he was a founder of American functionalism, long before the anti-psychiatry debate."}}, {"q": "The book \"Madness and Civilization\" was written by ______.", "concept": "The answer is c: Madness and Civilization was written by Michel Foucault (1961). Michel Foucault, a French philosopher and historian of ideas, published Folie et deraison: Histoire de la folie a l'age classique in 1961; an abridged English translation appeared as Madness and Civilization in 1965. He argued that madness is not a fixed natural fact but is defined differently in each era. In the Middle Ages the mad often wandered freely; in the 'Great Confinement' of the seventeenth century (the Hopital General opened in Paris in 1656) they were locked up together with the poor, the idle and criminals, because reason was now prized above all. Even the famous freeing of asylum patients by Philippe Pinel (1793) and the moral treatment of William Tuke's York Retreat (1796) were, for Michel Foucault, not simple kindness but a new kind of moral control, in which the patient was constantly watched, judged and made to feel guilty. The book fed the 1960s anti-psychiatry critique and Michel Foucault's later histories of power, The Birth of the Clinic (1963) and Discipline and Punish (1975). Option c is right because Michel Foucault is the author of Madness and Civilization. Exam trap: the word 'civilization' pulls students towards Sigmund Freud's Civilization and Its Discontents (1930), which is about the conflict between instinct and the demands of society, not about the history of madness. R. D. Laing, another anti-psychiatry figure, wrote The Divided Self (1960), not this book.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud (1930) wrote Civilization and Its Discontents, on the clash between instinct and society's demands; the shared word 'civilization' is the trap, but he did not write this history of madness.", "b": "Anna Freud (1936) wrote The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence and pioneered child analysis; she has no link with this book.", "d": "Ronald David Laing (1960) wrote The Divided Self and, with Aaron Esterson, Sanity, Madness and the Family (1964); he shared the anti-psychiatry mood but did not write Madness and Civilization."}}, {"q": "According to Kohlberg's theory of moral development, Stage 6, or the stage characterised by ______, is the stage where an individual has developed their own set of moral guidelines which may or may not always fit the law.", "concept": "The answer is d: in the theory of Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), stage 6 is the stage of universal ethical principles. He studied moral reasoning by giving boys dilemmas, most famously the Heinz dilemma, in which a man must decide whether to steal a drug he cannot afford to save his dying wife. He scored the reasoning behind the answer, not the answer itself, and found three levels with two stages each. Preconventional: stage 1, obedience and punishment orientation (right is what avoids punishment); stage 2, individualism and exchange (right is what serves my interest, a fair deal). Conventional: stage 3, good interpersonal relationships (being a 'good boy' or 'good girl'); stage 4, maintaining social order (obey laws and do one's duty). Postconventional: stage 5, social contract and individual rights (laws are agreements that can be changed for the common good); stage 6, universal ethical principles (self-chosen principles of justice, equal rights and human dignity, followed even when they conflict with the law). Stage 6 reasoning proved so rare that Lawrence Kohlberg later treated it as a theoretical ideal rather than a common stage. Example: Arjun says Heinz should steal the drug because a human life outweighs any property right, and that Heinz should accept prison if it follows; he puts a universal principle above the law. Option d is right because only stage 6 describes a person whose own moral principles may override the law. Exam trap: stage 5 also says laws can be changed, so students confuse it with stage 6; at stage 5 the person still works within a democratic social contract, while at stage 6 conscience and universal principles come first.", "traps": {"a": "Maintaining social order is stage 4, the second conventional stage, where right means obeying laws and doing one's duty; it is the opposite of putting personal principles above the law.", "b": "Individualism and exchange is stage 2, preconventional, where right is whatever serves one's own interests or makes a fair trade ('you help me, I help you').", "c": "Obedience and punishment orientation is stage 1, the most basic preconventional stage, where an act is wrong simply because it is punished."}}, {"q": "Amongst the Gestalt laws of perceptual organization, the law of ______ states that every stimulus pattern is seen in such a way that the resulting structure is as simple as possible.", "concept": "The answer is a: the law of Pragnanz says every stimulus pattern is seen in the way that makes the resulting structure as simple as possible. Pragnanz is German for conciseness or 'good form'. Max Wertheimer (1923) set out the Gestalt laws of perceptual organisation, and Kurt Koffka (1935) stated Pragnanz as the master principle: the perceptual field organises itself into the simplest, most regular and most stable figure that the stimulus allows. That is why it is also called the law of good figure or the law of simplicity. The other grouping laws can be seen as special cases of it: proximity (near items group together), similarity (alike items group together), closure (gaps are filled to make a complete shape) and continuity, or good continuation (we follow smooth lines rather than sharp turns). Example: Riya sees the Olympic logo as five overlapping circles, not as a jumble of curved fragments and odd shapes, because five circles is the simplest reading of the lines. In the same way, a triangle laid over part of a square is seen as two simple shapes, not as one complicated outline. Option a is right because the stem gives the textbook definition of Pragnanz: the simplest possible structure. Exam trap: closure also produces a 'good', complete figure, so it is the near miss; but closure is narrowly about filling gaps in an incomplete figure, while Pragnanz is the general tendency towards simplicity that covers every pattern.", "traps": {"b": "Closure is the tendency to fill in gaps so that an incomplete figure, such as a broken circle, is seen as whole; it is one route to a simple figure, not the general law of simplicity.", "c": "Continuity (good continuation) is the tendency to see lines as following the smoothest path, so two crossing curves look like two lines rather than four angles meeting; it concerns direction, not overall simplicity.", "d": "Proximity is the tendency to group elements that are close together, so dots spaced in pairs look like pairs; it concerns distance, not the simplest overall structure."}}, {"q": "The concept of the 'animus' archetype, given by Carl Jung, refers to:", "concept": "The answer is a: in the theory of Carl Jung (1928), the animus is the masculine side of a woman's psyche. Carl Jung divided the unconscious into a personal unconscious, made of forgotten and repressed experiences organised into complexes, and a collective unconscious, inherited by all humans and filled with archetypes: universal patterns or images shaped by the shared experience of our ancestors. Four archetypes are developed most fully. The persona is the social mask. The shadow is the dark, primitive, animal side that we disown. The self is the archetype of wholeness, symbolised by the mandala. The anima and animus carry the other sex within each person: the anima is the feminine side of a man, and the animus is the masculine side of a woman. They come from humanity's long experience of the other sex, and they help explain attraction, since we project our anima or animus onto a partner who seems to match the inner image. Individuation, the lifelong move towards wholeness, needs these sides to be accepted rather than denied. Example: Meera, a gentle and accommodating teacher, discovers a firm, logical and assertive voice when she leads a staff meeting; a Jungian would call this her animus coming into awareness. Option a is right because animus is the term for the masculine element in the female psyche. Exam trap: the pair is easy to reverse (option c describes the anima), and 'animus' sounds like 'animal', which pulls students to option b, a description of the shadow. Memory aid: Latin -us endings are masculine (animus), and -a endings are feminine (anima).", "traps": {"b": "The repressed, animal-like side of the mind is the shadow, Carl Jung's archetype of the primitive and socially unacceptable impulses we disown; 'animus' only sounds like 'animal'.", "c": "The feminine side of a man's psyche is the anima, the partner archetype of the animus; this option reverses the pair.", "d": "Repressed unpleasant thoughts and memories make up Carl Jung's personal unconscious, organised into complexes (close to Sigmund Freud's repressed unconscious); they are personal, not an inherited archetype."}}, {"q": "The concept of paranoid schizoid position was given by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the paranoid-schizoid position was described by Melanie Klein (1946). Melanie Klein was an Austrian-British psychoanalyst who analysed very young children through play and helped found object relations theory. She argued that from birth the infant relates to 'objects', above all the mother's breast, and is driven by powerful love and hate. In the paranoid-schizoid position, the first three or four months of life, the infant cannot bear that the same breast both feeds and frustrates. So it splits the object into a 'good breast' that is loved and idealised and a 'bad breast' that is hated and feared. Its own aggression is projected outwards, so the bad object seems to attack it; the main fear is persecutory anxiety (the paranoid part), and the main defence is splitting self and objects apart (the schizoid part). Projective identification, pushing one's feelings into another person, belongs here too. From around four to six months the infant enters the depressive position (Melanie Klein, 1935): it sees the mother as one whole person, both good and bad, feels guilt for its attacks and tries to make reparation. She called these 'positions', not stages, because people move back and forth between them throughout life. Example: Kabir, an adult client, sees his therapist as perfect one week and as cruel and rejecting the next; a Kleinian would say he is functioning in the paranoid-schizoid position. Option a is right because the term and the theory are Melanie Klein's. Exam trap: the clinical words 'paranoid' and 'schizoid' pull students towards Sigmund Freud's Schreber case on paranoia (1911) or Carl Jung's The Psychology of Dementia Praecox (1907), but the term describes normal infant development and belongs to Melanie Klein.", "traps": {"b": "Karen Horney (1945) described basic anxiety and three neurotic trends, moving towards, against and away from people; she did not write about infant positions.", "c": "Sigmund Freud (1905) described the psychosexual stages (oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital) and wrote on paranoia in the Schreber case; 'positions' were Melanie Klein's revision of his stage idea.", "d": "Carl Jung (1921) founded analytical psychology, with the collective unconscious, archetypes and psychological types; he did not develop object relations theory."}}, {"q": "The 'Direct Perception' theory of perception was given by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the direct perception theory was given by James J. Gibson (1966, 1979). James J. Gibson, an American psychologist, drew on his wartime work on how pilots judge distance when landing. He rejected the classic view of Hermann von Helmholtz (1867) and later Richard Gregory (1970) that perception is an inference, built up when the brain adds knowledge and expectation to poor sensory data (a top-down, constructivist view). For James J. Gibson the light reaching a moving observer, the optic array, is already rich enough to specify the world, so perception is a direct 'pick-up' of information with no inference needed (a bottom-up view). Key ideas are optic flow (as you move forward, the scene streams outwards from the point you are heading towards), texture gradients (surface texture looks finer with distance), invariants (properties that stay constant as you move) and affordances (what an object offers for action: a chair affords sitting, a handle affords pulling). Because he stressed the animal acting in its natural environment, the approach is called ecological, set out in The Ecological Approach to Visual Perception (1979). Example: Kabir runs to catch a high cricket ball without calculating anything; he simply moves so that the ball's image keeps rising steadily in his view, using information directly available in the light. Option c is right because direct, ecological perception is James J. Gibson's theory. Exam trap: his wife, Eleanor Gibson, is famous for the visual cliff (with Richard Walk, 1960), so a 'Gibson' question about depth perception in infants means her, not him.", "traps": {"a": "Endel Tulving (1972) distinguished episodic memory (personal events) from semantic memory (general facts) and later proposed encoding specificity; he worked on memory, not perception.", "b": "Jerome Bruner (1947), with Cecile Goodman, led the New Look in perception, showing that needs and values shape what we see (poorer children overestimated coin sizes); this is a top-down view, the opposite of direct perception.", "d": "Gregory Bateson (1956), with Don Jackson, Jay Haley and John Weakland, proposed the double bind theory of schizophrenia, a theory of family communication unrelated to perception."}}, {"q": "The theme for World Mental Health Day observed in October 2022 was:", "concept": "The answer is a: the theme for World Mental Health Day 2022 was 'Make mental health and well-being for all a global priority'. World Mental Health Day falls on 10 October every year. It was started in 1992 by the World Federation for Mental Health (WFMH), a global body of professionals, service users and advocates, and it is supported by the World Health Organization; each year a theme focuses world attention on one issue. The 2022 theme came in the wake of the COVID-19 pandemic, which the World Health Organization estimated raised the rates of anxiety and depression worldwide by about a quarter in its first year, while many services were disrupted. The campaign asked governments to treat mental health as a priority within health systems, with more investment and wider access to care. Recent themes are worth learning as a dated list: 2015, dignity in mental health; 2016, psychological first aid; 2018, young people and mental health in a changing world; 2019, suicide prevention; 2021, mental health in an unequal world; 2022, make mental health and well-being for all a global priority; 2023, mental health is a universal human right. Option a is right because it matches the 2022 theme (the option simply drops the words 'and well-being'). Exam trap: every distractor is a real World Mental Health Day theme from an earlier year, so a student who has seen them before recognises them and picks one; the year has to be learnt with each theme.", "traps": {"b": "Psychological first aid was the 2016 theme, promoting basic, humane support for people in acute distress after a crisis; it is a real theme but from the wrong year.", "c": "Young people and mental health in a changing world was the 2018 theme, on adolescent mental health; it is a real theme but from the wrong year.", "d": "Dignity in mental health was the 2015 theme, on respect and rights for people with mental illness; it is a real theme but from the wrong year."}}, {"q": "The concept of 'borderline personality organization' was given by:", "concept": "The answer is d: the concept of borderline personality organisation was given by Otto Kernberg (1967, 1975). Otto Kernberg, an Austrian-born American psychoanalyst, combined Melanie Klein's (1946) object relations theory with ego psychology. He described three levels of personality organisation, judged on identity, level of defences and reality testing. Neurotic organisation has an integrated identity, higher-level defences such as repression, and intact reality testing. Borderline organisation has identity diffusion (an unstable, contradictory sense of self and others), primitive defences centred on splitting (with projective identification, idealisation, devaluation, denial and omnipotence), and reality testing that is mostly intact but can slip under stress. Psychotic organisation has identity diffusion, primitive defences and a loss of reality testing. Borderline organisation is wider than the DSM diagnosis of borderline personality disorder: it also takes in narcissistic, antisocial, paranoid and schizoid personalities. Otto Kernberg traced it to intense early aggression that keeps 'all good' and 'all bad' images of self and others apart, and later developed transference-focused psychotherapy to treat it. Example: Meera praises her new manager as the best person she has ever worked with, then, after one critical remark, calls her cruel and worthless, yet she stays fully in touch with reality; this is splitting with intact reality testing, the borderline level. Option d is right because borderline personality organisation is Otto Kernberg's structural model. Exam trap: Heinz Kohut (1971) and Otto Kernberg both wrote on narcissism in the 1970s and famously disagreed, and Margaret Mahler's (1975) rapprochement crisis is often linked to the origins of borderline problems, so all three names look plausible; the structural term itself is Otto Kernberg's alone.", "traps": {"a": "Heinz Kohut (1971) founded self psychology, which explains narcissism as a deficit left by failures of parental mirroring and idealisation (selfobjects); he wrote on narcissistic personality, not borderline organisation.", "b": "Margaret Mahler (1975) described separation-individuation, the infant's 'psychological birth' through subphases such as rapprochement; her work is used to explain borderline problems, but the term borderline personality organisation is not hers.", "c": "Carl Jung (1921) founded analytical psychology, with archetypes and psychological types; he did not describe levels of personality organisation."}}, {"q": "Humour, anticipation, sublimation and ________ are all considered as mature defense mechanisms.", "concept": "The answer is d: altruism, with humour, anticipation, sublimation and suppression, is a mature defence. George Vaillant (1977) ranked defence mechanisms by maturity using the Grant Study, which followed a group of Harvard men from the late 1930s through adult life. In Adaptation to Life he grouped defences into four levels. Psychotic defences (delusional projection, psychotic denial, distortion) bend reality itself. Immature defences (projection, acting out, passive aggression, hypochondriasis, schizoid fantasy) are common in adolescents and in personality disorders, and they push distress onto other people or into the body. Neurotic defences (repression, displacement, reaction formation, intellectualisation, dissociation) are everyday ways of keeping feelings out of awareness. Mature defences (altruism, anticipation, humour, sublimation, suppression) keep the feeling in awareness but channel it usefully. The men who used mature defences had better physical health, careers and marriages decades later. Altruism means coping with one's own distress by serving others in ways that bring real satisfaction. A handy mnemonic for the mature group is SASHA: suppression, altruism, sublimation, humour, anticipation. Example: after his father dies of cancer, Kabir starts volunteering at a cancer helpline; helping other families eases his own grief. Option d is right because altruism is one of the five mature defences named by George Vaillant. Exam trap: all three distractors are immature defences that can sound active or harmless: somatisation turns emotional pain into bodily complaints, projection puts one's own unacceptable feelings onto others, and acting out discharges an impulse in action without reflection.", "traps": {"a": "Somatisation is an immature defence in which emotional conflict is turned into physical symptoms, such as headaches before every exam; it pushes distress into the body rather than channelling it usefully.", "b": "Projection is an immature defence in which one's own unacceptable feelings are attributed to others ('he hates me' instead of 'I hate him'); it is a typical immature mechanism in George Vaillant's scheme.", "c": "Acting out is an immature defence in which an unconscious wish or feeling is expressed directly in impulsive action, such as a tantrum or self-harm, so that it does not have to be felt."}}, {"q": "The classification of temperament types into difficult, easy and slow to warm up was given by ________:", "concept": "The answer is c: the easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up temperament types were described by Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (1977). Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess, a husband-and-wife pair of psychiatrists in New York, began the New York Longitudinal Study in 1956 with Herbert Birch, following children from early infancy into adulthood. Temperament means stable, early-appearing differences in emotional and behavioural style. From parent interviews they rated nine dimensions: activity level, rhythmicity (regularity of sleep, hunger and toileting), approach or withdrawal from new things, adaptability, threshold of responsiveness, intensity of reaction, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span and persistence. Combinations of these gave three types, described in Temperament and Development (1977). Easy children (about 40 percent) are regular, adapt quickly and are mostly cheerful. Difficult children (about 10 percent) are irregular, withdraw from new things, adapt slowly and react intensely and negatively. Slow-to-warm-up children (about 15 percent) are mildly negative and slow to adapt at first but warm up with repeated exposure. About 35 percent did not fit any type. Their key idea was goodness of fit: outcomes depend on how well the child's temperament matches the demands and patience of parents and setting. Example: on her first day at nursery, Riya clings to her mother and watches silently, but by the second week she is playing happily; she is slow to warm up. Option c is right because the three-type classification comes from Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess. Exam trap: 'James Lange' is not a person. It garbles the James-Lange theory of emotion, named after two people, William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885), who held that bodily arousal comes before the felt emotion.", "traps": {"a": "Alfred Adler (1912) founded individual psychology and wrote on inferiority feelings and birth order; he described styles of life, not infant temperament types.", "b": "Carl Jung (1921) classified people as introverts or extraverts with four functions (thinking, feeling, sensation, intuition) in Psychological Types; these are adult personality types, not infant temperaments.", "d": "'James Lange' garbles the James-Lange theory of emotion of William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885), which says we feel an emotion after perceiving our bodily changes; it has nothing to do with temperament."}}, {"q": "Machiavellianism is a term generally used to denote people who:", "concept": "The answer is b: Machiavellianism describes people who express their power motivation by manipulating and exploiting others in a deceptive way. The term comes from Niccolo Machiavelli (1513), the Florentine author of The Prince, who advised rulers that cunning, deceit and fear are acceptable when they keep power: the end justifies the means. Richard Christie and Florence Geis (1970) turned this into a personality trait. They built the Mach IV scale from statements adapted from The Prince, such as 'The best way to handle people is to tell them what they want to hear'. High scorers ('high Machs') are cool, cynical about human nature, emotionally detached and willing to use flattery, lies and strategy to get ahead; in laboratory bargaining games they tend to win more and are less swayed by emotion. Low scorers are more trusting and empathic. Delroy Paulhus and Kevin Williams (2002) placed Machiavellianism in the Dark Triad, three overlapping, socially aversive but subclinical traits, with narcissism and psychopathy; all three share low agreeableness and low honesty-humility. Example: Kabir praises his team leader in meetings, quietly takes credit for a colleague's report and leaks a rival's mistake to the manager, all while seeming friendly; this calculated manipulation is Machiavellian. Option b is right because manipulation and deceptive exploitation in pursuit of power are the defining features of the trait. Exam trap: students mix up the Dark Triad traits. Machiavellianism is cold, planned, long-term manipulation; psychopathy adds impulsivity, thrill seeking and lack of remorse; narcissism centres on grandiosity and the need for admiration.", "traps": {"a": "Believing that men should rule society rather than women describes patriarchal or sexist attitudes (male supremacy); it is an ideology about gender, not a manipulative personality trait.", "c": "Being extremely benevolent and ready to help is altruism, or high agreeableness and honesty-humility, the very opposite of the Machiavellian trait.", "d": "The synthesis of thesis and antithesis is the dialectic usually credited to the philosopher Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel (1807); it is a theory of how ideas develop, not a personality trait."}}, {"q": "ERG theory of motivation was given by:", "concept": "The answer is a: ERG theory of motivation was given by Clayton Alderfer (1969). Clayton Alderfer, an American organisational psychologist, reworked Abraham Maslow's (1943) five-level hierarchy and tested it on employees in organisations. He reduced the five needs to three. Existence needs are material and physical (food, water, pay, safe working conditions), matching Abraham Maslow's physiological and safety needs. Relatedness needs are for meaningful relationships with family, friends, colleagues and supervisors, matching belongingness and the social part of esteem. Growth needs are for personal development and full use of one's abilities, matching self-esteem and self-actualisation. ERG theory differs from Abraham Maslow's in three ways. First, the needs are not a rigid ladder, and several can be active at once. Second, a lower need does not have to be satisfied before a higher one can motivate. Third, it adds frustration-regression: when a higher need is blocked, the person falls back and seeks more of a lower need, whereas Abraham Maslow described only satisfaction-progression, moving up once a need is met. Example: Arjun is passed over for promotion and for the chance to lead a project (growth frustrated); instead of pushing for development, he starts demanding a pay rise and spending more time chatting with colleagues (existence and relatedness), which is frustration-regression. Option a is right because Clayton Alderfer proposed the existence, relatedness and growth model. Exam trap: all four names are need theorists of motivation, and David McClelland (1961) also had three needs (achievement, affiliation and power), so 'three needs' alone can pull students away from Clayton Alderfer; Frederick Herzberg (1959) had two factors and Abraham Maslow five levels.", "traps": {"b": "Abraham Maslow (1943) proposed the five-level hierarchy (physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem, self-actualisation) with satisfaction-progression; ERG is Clayton Alderfer's three-level revision of it.", "c": "Frederick Herzberg (1959) gave the two-factor, or motivation-hygiene, theory of job satisfaction, splitting motivators from hygiene factors; it does not name existence, relatedness and growth needs.", "d": "David McClelland (1961) proposed learned needs for achievement, affiliation and power, measured with the Thematic Apperception Test; it also has three needs, but not existence, relatedness and growth."}}, {"q": "Sundowning, or Sundown Syndrome, is a phenomenon commonly associated with:", "concept": "The answer is d: sundowning is most commonly associated with Alzheimer's disease and other dementias. Sundowning, or sundown syndrome, is a pattern in which confusion, restlessness, agitation, anxiety, pacing, wandering and sometimes shouting or aggression increase in the late afternoon, evening and night. It is seen most often in the middle and later stages of Alzheimer's disease, the commonest cause of dementia, and in other dementias; the same evening worsening is also typical of delirium. Several causes probably combine. Damage to the brain's body clock (the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus) disrupts circadian rhythms and the sleep-wake cycle. Fading light and shadows make the surroundings harder to interpret, fatigue builds through the day, and there are fewer orienting cues such as visitors and routines. Hunger, pain, a full bladder, infections and some medicines can add to it. Management is mostly practical: bright light and activity in the daytime, a fixed routine, less caffeine and fewer daytime naps, well-lit rooms in the evening, a calm setting and checks for pain or infection. Example: Meera's 78-year-old grandmother, who has Alzheimer's disease, is calm and chatty in the morning, but as it grows dark she insists she must go home to her parents and tries to leave the house. Option d is right because sundowning is a recognised feature of Alzheimer's disease and related dementias. Exam trap: because sundowning disturbs the night, students may pick insomnia; but insomnia is a sleep disorder of trouble falling or staying asleep, while sundowning is an evening worsening of confusion and behaviour in a damaged brain.", "traps": {"a": "Alcohol dependence brings tolerance, craving and withdrawal; its severe withdrawal state, delirium tremens, begins about 2 to 3 days after the last drink, but sundowning is not a recognised feature of dependence itself.", "b": "Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder of hallucinations, delusions, disorganisation and negative symptoms; its symptoms are not tied to the time of day.", "c": "Insomnia is difficulty falling or staying asleep, or unrefreshing sleep; sundowning can disturb sleep, but it is a pattern of evening confusion and agitation, not a primary sleep disorder."}}, {"q": "The term 'animal magnetism' was coined by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the term 'animal magnetism' was coined by Franz Anton Mesmer. Franz Anton Mesmer (1734 to 1815) was a German physician who practised in Vienna and then, from 1778, in Paris. In the 1770s he argued that an invisible universal fluid flows through all living bodies and that illness comes from a blockage or imbalance of it. He called this force animal magnetism, 'animal' meaning belonging to living beings. He treated patients by passing his hands or magnets over them, or by seating groups around a tub of 'magnetised' water, the baquet, until they fell into a dramatic convulsive 'crisis' that was meant to restore the flow. A French royal commission (1784) that included Benjamin Franklin and Antoine Lavoisier ran blindfold trials and concluded that the effects came from imagination, not from any fluid. Even so, Mesmer had shown that suggestion and expectation can change bodily states, and his methods became the root of hypnosis. The Scottish surgeon James Braid (1843) later dropped the fluid idea and coined the word 'hypnotism'. The English verb 'mesmerise' keeps Mesmer's name alive. Option a is right because both the phrase and the theory of animal magnetism are Mesmer's own. Exam trap: examiners like to swap Mesmer with James Braid, who named hypnotism, or with Jean-Martin Charcot (1880s), who used hypnosis to study hysteria; remember the chain: Mesmer gave animal magnetism, Braid gave hypnotism, Charcot tied it to hysteria.", "traps": {"b": "Eric Berne (1961, 1964) founded transactional analysis, with its Parent, Adult and Child ego states and the 'games' of Games People Play; he worked in the twentieth century and had nothing to do with magnetic fluids.", "c": "Otto Kernberg (1967, 1975) is a modern psychoanalyst known for borderline personality organisation and transference-focused psychotherapy, nearly two centuries after Mesmer.", "d": "Alfred Adler (1912) founded individual psychology, with the inferiority complex, striving for superiority and birth order; he was a follower and then a rival of Sigmund Freud, not a magnetist."}}, {"q": "The virtue that develops in the 'generativity versus stagnation' stage of Erikson is referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is c: the virtue of the generativity versus stagnation stage is care. Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, described eight psychosocial stages across the whole lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis between two poles, and resolving it well leaves the person with a lasting strength, which Erikson later called a basic virtue. In order the virtues are hope (trust versus mistrust, infancy), will (autonomy versus shame and doubt, toddlerhood), purpose (initiative versus guilt, about 3 to 6 years), competence (industry versus inferiority, school age), fidelity (identity versus role confusion, adolescence), love (intimacy versus isolation, young adulthood), care (generativity versus stagnation, middle adulthood, about 40 to 65) and wisdom (ego integrity versus despair, old age). Generativity means concern for establishing and guiding the next generation: raising children, mentoring juniors, teaching, or building work and institutions that will outlast oneself. Parenthood is neither necessary nor sufficient. When this fails, the person stagnates, feeling bored, self-absorbed and that they have little to give. The virtue that grows out of success is care, a widening commitment to look after the people, products and ideas one has helped to create. Example: Meera, aged 48, starts training the junior nurses on her ward and runs a weekend reading club for children in her colony; she is resolving the generativity crisis and showing care. Option c is right because care is the strength Erikson attached to stage 7. Exam trap: examiners offer the neighbouring virtues, and wisdom is the favourite decoy; it belongs to the final stage (integrity versus despair), so students who think 'middle-aged adult equals wise adult' slip one stage too far.", "traps": {"a": "Wisdom is the virtue of Erik Erikson's eighth and last stage, ego integrity versus despair, when the older adult looks back and accepts the life lived; it comes after generativity, not from it.", "b": "Purpose is the virtue of Erik Erikson's third stage, initiative versus guilt (about 3 to 6 years), when the child learns to plan and pursue goals in play.", "d": "Fidelity is the virtue of Erik Erikson's fifth stage, identity versus role confusion in adolescence: loyalty to freely chosen values and people."}}, {"q": "The two-factor theory of emotion was proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the two-factor theory of emotion was proposed by Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer. Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962) argued that an emotion needs two ingredients. The first is general physiological arousal (a racing heart, sweating, trembling), which sets how strong the feeling is. The second is a cognitive label, a judgement drawn from the situation about why one is aroused, which decides which emotion it is. Arousal on its own is too similar across emotions to tell anger from joy; the label does that work. In their classic study, students were injected with epinephrine (adrenaline), described as a vitamin called Suproxin. Some were told its true side effects, some were told nothing and some were misinformed; a placebo group got saline. Each then waited with a stooge who acted either euphoric or angry. Those who were aroused but had no explanation for it caught the stooge's mood most, while the informed group, who could blame the injection, did so least. Later replications were mixed, but the idea of misattributed arousal survived, for example in the suspension-bridge study of Donald Dutton and Arthur Aron (1974). Example: Arjun has just run up four flights of stairs and meets a classmate who teases him; still panting, he feels unusually angry, because he reads his leftover arousal as anger. Option c is right because 'two-factor theory' is the standard name for the Schachter and Singer arousal-plus-label account. Exam trap: 'two-factor' is also the name of Frederick Herzberg's (1959) work motivation theory, O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) avoidance learning theory and Charles Spearman's (1904) intelligence theory, so check what the two factors are.", "traps": {"a": "Walter Cannon (1927) and Philip Bard held that the thalamus sends signals to the cortex (the feeling) and to the body (the arousal) at the same moment, so the two occur together; there is no cognitive labelling step.", "b": "William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885) said an emotion is our perception of bodily changes: we feel afraid because we tremble. That is a body-first theory, not arousal plus a cognitive label.", "d": "Richard Lazarus (1991) built cognitive appraisal theory, in which the judgement of what an event means for one's well-being produces the emotion; appraisal is the starting point, not a label added to unexplained arousal."}}, {"q": "The transtheoretical model of change was given by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the transtheoretical model of change was given by James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente. James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente (1983) studied how smokers quit, both on their own and in treatment, and found that people move through a predictable series of stages rather than changing in one leap. The stages are precontemplation (no intention to change in the next six months, often not seeing a problem), contemplation (intending to change but ambivalent), preparation (planning to act within a month, with small steps taken), action (behaviour changed within the last six months) and maintenance (change kept for more than six months); termination, when temptation has gone, was added later. Relapse is common, and people often cycle back through the stages before change lasts. The model is called transtheoretical because it draws its processes of change (consciousness raising, self-liberation, stimulus control and others) from many schools of therapy instead of one. Its practical message is to match the help to the stage: information and gentle questioning for precontemplators, help with the decision for contemplators, concrete plans for those in preparation. Example: Kabir smokes ten cigarettes a day; this month he says he 'might cut down someday' (contemplation), and next month he buys nicotine gum and fixes a quit date (preparation). Option a is right because Prochaska and DiClemente are the authors of the stages-of-change model. Exam trap: the model is usually taught alongside motivational interviewing, so examiners may offer William Miller and Stephen Rollnick (1991) as its authors; and the 'Miller' in Dollard and Miller is Neal Miller, a different person altogether.", "traps": {"b": "John Dollard and Neal Miller (1950), in Personality and Psychotherapy, translated Sigmund Freud's ideas into learning theory (drive, cue, response, reward) and analysed approach-avoidance conflict; they did not describe stages of readiness to change.", "c": "Abraham Maslow (1943) proposed the hierarchy of needs, from physiological needs up to self-actualisation; it is a theory of motivation and growth, not a model of how people change a problem behaviour.", "d": "Carl Jung (1921) founded analytical psychology, with the collective unconscious, archetypes and psychological types; his individuation is lifelong growth of the self, not a stages-of-change model."}}, {"q": "The Psychasthenia scale (Scale 7) in the MMPI-2 measures:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Psychasthenia scale (Scale 7) measures obsessive-compulsive symptoms and difficulty concentrating. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) built the MMPI by empirical criterion keying: an item was kept only if a diagnosed patient group answered it differently from normal people. Scale 7 was keyed on patients then called psychasthenic. The French psychiatrist Pierre Janet (1903) had used 'psychasthenia' for a weakness of mental energy showing as obsessions, compulsions, phobias, doubts and excessive anxiety. Today most of these patients would be called anxious or obsessive-compulsive. High scorers on Scale 7 describe chronic worry, tension, self-doubt, perfectionism, guilt, intrusive thoughts and rituals, and trouble concentrating because their minds keep returning to their worries. The scale is the MMPI-2's best general marker of anxious distress, and it often rises with Scale 2 (Depression) in the 2-7 code type, an anxious, ruminating depression. Example: Riya, a final-year student, checks her door lock five times before leaving, cannot stop worrying about small mistakes and rereads the same page because her focus keeps slipping; her MMPI-2 profile shows Scale 7 at T 72, above the cut-off of 65. Option d is right because obsessions, compulsions, worry and poor concentration are exactly what Scale 7 picks up. Exam trap: the old scale names are diagnostic labels from the 1930s, and examiners rely on students not knowing what 'psychasthenia' meant; it is the anxiety and obsession scale, not a scale of physical weakness or fatigue, which pulls students towards the somatic option.", "traps": {"a": "Masculine and feminine interests are measured by Scale 5, Masculinity-Femininity (Mf), which reflects interests typical of one's sex and is not a measure of pathology.", "b": "Antisocial behaviour, impulsivity and conflict with family and authority are measured by Scale 4, Psychopathic Deviate (Pd).", "c": "Extreme somatic complaints and bodily preoccupation are measured by Scale 1, Hypochondriasis (Hs); Scale 3, Hysteria (Hy), adds physical symptoms under stress with denial of problems."}}, {"q": "The type of parenting associated with positive outcomes, where parents are both very warm and yet careful to set clear limits and restrictions, is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: warm parents who also set clear limits practise authoritative parenting. Diana Baumrind (1966, 1971) observed preschool children at home and in nursery school and interviewed their parents. She described three styles that differ in how much parents demand and how warmly they respond. Authoritarian parents are strict and cold: rules are fixed, obedience is expected 'because I said so', and punishment is common. Permissive parents are warm but make few demands and leave the child to regulate itself. Authoritative parents combine high warmth with high control: they set firm, consistent limits, but explain their reasons, listen to the child's view and encourage growing independence. Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) recast the styles as two dimensions, demandingness and responsiveness, and split permissiveness into permissive-indulgent (warm, few rules) and neglectful-uninvolved (neither warm nor controlling). Across many studies, children of authoritative parents tend to be the most self-reliant, socially skilled, self-controlled and successful at school, while neglect is linked to the poorest outcomes. Some cross-cultural work suggests that strictness carries different meanings in some families, but the advantage of the authoritative style holds broadly. Example: Meera wants to stay out until midnight; her mother sets 10 o'clock as the limit, explains that the bus route is unsafe late at night, and agrees to 10:30 on weekends if Meera keeps her word. Option a is right because the stem describes exactly this high-warmth, high-control combination. Exam trap: 'authoritative' and 'authoritarian' look almost identical; authoritarian means control without warmth, authoritative means control with warmth and reasons.", "traps": {"b": "Neglectful-uninvolved parenting, added by Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983), is low on both warmth and limits, the opposite of the stem, and is tied to the poorest child outcomes.", "c": "Permissive-indulgent parenting is warm but sets few rules or demands, so it has the warmth the stem describes but not the clear limits.", "d": "Authoritarian parenting, in Diana Baumrind's scheme, sets strict limits with little warmth or explanation and demands obedience; it has the limits but not the warmth."}}, {"q": "Autokinetic effect and stroboscopic motion are examples of:", "concept": "The answer is b: the autokinetic effect and stroboscopic motion are examples of apparent motion. Apparent motion is the perception of movement when nothing in the scene is actually moving in that way. Real motion, by contrast, is an object's image sweeping across the retina, signalled by direction-selective neurons concentrated in area MT (V5) of the visual cortex. In the autokinetic effect, a small, stationary point of light in a completely dark room seems to drift and wander after a few seconds of staring. With no frame of reference to anchor it, tiny eye movements are read as movement of the light. Muzafer Sherif (1935) used the effect to study how group norms form: people judging the light together came to agree on how far it moved. Stroboscopic motion is the illusion of smooth movement from a rapid series of still images, as in films shown at about 24 frames a second. It is closely related to the phi phenomenon of Max Wertheimer (1912), in which two lights flashed one after the other look like a single light jumping between them, the finding that launched Gestalt psychology. Other forms of apparent motion include induced motion (the moon seems to race through the clouds) and the motion aftereffect (the waterfall illusion). Example: in a dark hostel room, Arjun stares at the glowing dot of a mosquito repellent and is sure it is creeping sideways (autokinetic); at the cinema he sees continuous action built from still frames (stroboscopic). Option b is right because in both cases movement is seen where none exists. Exam trap: 'real motion' tempts because the observer genuinely sees movement, but the stimulus itself is still.", "traps": {"a": "Space perception covers depth and distance cues (binocular disparity, convergence, linear perspective, the visual cliff), not the illusory movement of a stationary stimulus.", "c": "Time perception is the judgement of the duration and order of events; the autokinetic and stroboscopic effects are about seeing movement, not timing.", "d": "Real motion is the perception of an object that actually moves across the visual field; in the autokinetic effect the light is still, and in a film each frame is still."}}, {"q": "Under the Severe Personality Pathology category of the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory (MCMI-III), the following scale is counted:", "concept": "The answer is c: Scale C (Borderline) is counted under Severe Personality Pathology on the MCMI-III. Theodore Millon (1994) built the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory-III, a 175-item true-false questionnaire for adults in clinical settings, from his own theory of personality and from DSM-IV. Its clinical scales fall into four groups. The 11 Clinical Personality Patterns are the basic styles: 1 Schizoid, 2A Avoidant, 2B Depressive, 3 Dependent, 4 Histrionic, 5 Narcissistic, 6A Antisocial, 6B Sadistic (Aggressive), 7 Compulsive, 8A Negativistic (Passive-Aggressive) and 8B Masochistic (Self-Defeating). The 3 Severe Personality Pathology scales are S Schizotypal, C Borderline and P Paranoid. Millon saw these as more structurally damaged versions of the basic patterns, with weaker inner cohesion and a greater risk of breaking down under stress. Then come 7 Clinical Syndromes (A Anxiety, H Somatoform, N Bipolar: Manic, D Dysthymia, B Alcohol Dependence, T Drug Dependence, R Post-Traumatic Stress) and 3 Severe Clinical Syndromes (SS Thought Disorder, CC Major Depression, PP Delusional Disorder). Scores are base rate scores, anchored to how common each condition is among patients; BR 75 suggests a trait is present and BR 85 that it is prominent. Example: Kabir, an inpatient with stormy relationships, self-harm and chronic emptiness, scores BR 90 on Scale C. Option c is right because Borderline is one of the three severe personality pathology scales. Exam trap: the single letters S, C and P are the severe personality scales, while the doubled letters SS, CC and PP are the severe clinical syndromes; Scale C is Borderline, but Scale CC is Major Depression.", "traps": {"a": "Scale 2A (Avoidant) is one of Theodore Millon's 11 Clinical Personality Patterns, the basic personality styles, not the severe pathology group.", "b": "Scale 3 (Dependent) is also a Clinical Personality Pattern; Theodore Millon placed it among the basic styles rather than the structurally severe ones.", "d": "Scale A (Anxiety) is a Clinical Syndrome scale, measuring a current symptom state rather than an enduring personality pattern."}}, {"q": "The Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory (MCMI-III) consists of ______ items.", "concept": "The answer is a: the MCMI-III consists of 175 items. Theodore Millon (1994) designed the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory-III as a short self-report test for adults seen in mental health settings. All 175 items are answered true or false, and most people finish in about 25 to 30 minutes, far quicker than the MMPI-2. The items feed 14 personality pattern scales, 10 clinical syndrome scales, 3 modifying indices (Disclosure, Desirability, Debasement) and a Validity index. Many items are scored on more than one scale, which is how so few items can cover so many scales. The count was 175 in the first MCMI (1977) and stayed the same in the MCMI-II (1987) and the MCMI-III; only the MCMI-IV (2015) grew, to 195 items. Example: Arjun, a clinical psychology trainee, chooses the MCMI-III for a tired inpatient because its 175 items take about half an hour. Option a is right because 175 is the MCMI-III item count. Exam trap: the MMPI-2 has 567 items and the MMPI-2-RF has 338, so examiners place 567 among the options to catch students who mix up the two inventories.", "traps": {"b": "250 is not the length of any edition of Theodore Millon's inventory; the first three editions all had 175 items and the MCMI-IV has 195.", "c": "108 items would be far too few for the MCMI-III's 28 scales and indices, and it matches no edition of the test.", "d": "567 is the item count of the MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989), the revision of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley's inventory, not of Theodore Millon's test."}}, {"q": "The condition in which one fabricates symptoms of illness in oneself, gets sick on purpose and makes frequent visits to hospitals is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: deliberately fabricating or inducing illness in oneself, with repeated hospital visits, is Munchausen syndrome. Richard Asher (1951) gave the name to patients who invented dramatic medical histories, travelled from hospital to hospital and submitted to tests and even operations. He borrowed it from Baron Munchausen, an eighteenth-century German nobleman famous for wildly exaggerated tall tales. In DSM-5 the condition is factitious disorder imposed on self, and Munchausen syndrome is its most severe, chronic form. The person falsifies signs or symptoms, or causes real injury or disease (for example by taking blood thinners, contaminating a wound or tampering with urine samples), and presents as ill. The motive is internal: to take on the sick role and receive care and attention. There is no obvious external reward, which separates it from malingering, where symptoms are faked for money, drugs, or escape from work or the law. Example: Riya, a 30-year-old laboratory technician, secretly injects herself with insulin to bring on low blood sugar, then turns up at different emergency departments with vague, shifting stories and asks for more tests. Option a is right because the stem describes a person producing illness in themselves, the defining feature of Munchausen syndrome. Exam trap: 'by proxy' (option b) flips the target, since there the illness is produced in someone else, usually a child; examiners also mix factitious disorder with malingering, which needs an external incentive.", "traps": {"b": "Munchausen syndrome by proxy, named by Roy Meadow (1977) and now called factitious disorder imposed on another, is a carer producing illness in a dependent person, usually a child; the stem describes illness produced in oneself.", "c": "Capgras syndrome, described by Joseph Capgras and Jean Reboul-Lachaux (1923), is the delusion that a familiar person has been replaced by an identical impostor; nothing is faked by the patient.", "d": "Fregoli syndrome, described by Paul Courbon and Gustave Fail (1927), is the delusion that different strangers are one familiar person in disguise; it is a misidentification delusion, not fabricated illness."}}, {"q": "The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory has ______ clinical scales apart from the validity scales:", "concept": "The answer is b: the MMPI has 10 clinical scales besides its validity scales. Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota built the inventory by empirical criterion keying: an item stayed on a scale only if a diagnosed patient group answered it differently from normal people, mostly visitors and relatives at the hospital. The ten clinical scales, numbered 1 to 9 and 0, are 1 Hs (Hypochondriasis), 2 D (Depression), 3 Hy (Hysteria), 4 Pd (Psychopathic Deviate), 5 Mf (Masculinity-Femininity), 6 Pa (Paranoia), 7 Pt (Psychasthenia), 8 Sc (Schizophrenia), 9 Ma (Hypomania) and 0 Si (Social Introversion). The original validity scales are L (Lie), F (Infrequency), K (Correction) and the Cannot Say count; the MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) kept the same ten clinical scales and added validity scales such as VRIN and TRIN. Clinicians read the profile as a code type, the two highest clinical scales. Example: Kabir's MMPI-2 profile shows scales 2 and 7 highest, a 2-7 code type, suggesting an anxious, worried depression. Option b is right because the standard profile carries exactly ten clinical scales. Exam trap: the scales are numbered 1 to 9 plus 0, so students who count the digits 1 to 9 answer nine; the MMPI-2-RF also has nine Restructured Clinical scales, and the MMPI-2 has 15 content scales, which is where options c and a come from.", "traps": {"a": "15 is the number of MMPI-2 content scales (such as Anxiety, Fears, Depression, Health Concerns and Bizarre Mentation) developed by James Butcher and colleagues (1990), not the basic clinical scales.", "c": "Nine is the number of Restructured Clinical (RC) scales on the MMPI-2-RF (Auke Tellegen and colleagues, 2003); it is also what students get by counting scales 1 to 9 and forgetting scale 0, Social Introversion.", "d": "Eight counts only the clinical scales keyed on psychiatric diagnostic groups (Hs, D, Hy, Pd, Pa, Pt, Sc, Ma); adding Masculinity-Femininity and Social Introversion gives the full ten."}}, {"q": "The concept of 'psychopathic inferiority' was introduced by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the concept of 'psychopathic inferiority' was introduced by Julius Koch. Julius Ludwig August Koch (1891), a German psychiatrist, wrote Die psychopathischen Minderwertigkeiten ('The Psychopathic Inferiorities'). He used the term for lasting abnormalities of character that fell short of insanity: people whose intellect and grip on reality were intact, but whose emotions, will or conduct were persistently abnormal. He thought these inferiorities were constitutional, rooted in an inborn or acquired weakness of the nervous system. His category was broad, covering anxious, odd and unstable people as well as antisocial ones, and it was meant to replace the older, morally loaded label 'moral insanity'. The history runs in a clear line. Philippe Pinel (1801) described manie sans délire, mania without delirium, in patients with impulsive rages but intact reasoning. James Cowles Prichard (1835) named moral insanity, a disorder of feelings and conduct. Koch then gave the word 'psychopathic' its personality meaning. Kurt Schneider (1923), in Psychopathic Personalities, described ten types of abnormal personality, and Hervey Cleckley (1941), in The Mask of Sanity, narrowed psychopathy to the charming, remorseless type that Robert D. Hare's Psychopathy Checklist-Revised (1991) measures today. Option a is right because 'psychopathic inferiority' is Koch's own phrase and his step in this lineage. Exam trap: 'Karl Schneider' blends Kurt Schneider, who wrote the 1923 book on psychopathic personalities, with Carl Schneider, a different German psychiatrist, so the name looks right but belongs to nobody in this history; and Schneider came about thirty years after Koch.", "traps": {"b": "There is no 'Karl Schneider' in this history: the name blends Kurt Schneider, whose Psychopathic Personalities (1923) came three decades after Julius Koch, with Carl Schneider, another German psychiatrist; neither coined psychopathic inferiority.", "c": "Leon Festinger (1954, 1957) was a social psychologist known for social comparison theory and cognitive dissonance, not for the history of personality disorder.", "d": "'James Lange' garbles the James-Lange theory of emotion, proposed separately by William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885); neither wrote on psychopathy."}}, {"q": "The concept of psychodrama was created by ______ in the 1930s.", "concept": "The answer is b: psychodrama was created by Jacob L. Moreno. Jacob Levy Moreno (1889 to 1974), a Romanian-born psychiatrist working in Vienna, noticed how much people revealed and healed when they acted out their lives instead of only talking about them. In psychodrama a client, the protagonist, enacts scenes from the past, present or a feared future on a stage. The therapist acts as director, other group members serve as auxiliary egos who play important people in the protagonist's life, and the rest form an audience. A session moves through a warm-up, the action and a sharing phase in which members say what the drama stirred in them. Key techniques are role reversal (the protagonist plays the other person), doubling (an auxiliary voices the protagonist's unspoken feelings) and mirroring (the protagonist watches someone replay their behaviour). The aim is catharsis plus new, spontaneous ways of acting. Moreno also founded sociometry (Who Shall Survive?, 1934). Example: Meera cannot tell her father she wants to change her college course; in a psychodrama group she rehearses the talk, then reverses roles and, speaking as her father, discovers his fear for her future. Option b is right because Moreno invented psychodrama and built its stage, roles and methods. Exam trap: Moreno is known for both psychodrama and sociometry, and examiners swap the two; the empty chair of Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls, 1951) grew from psychodrama but is a different method. Key note: Jacob L. Moreno ran his spontaneity theatre in Vienna from 1921, so psychodrama's roots are in the 1920s; he coined 'group psychotherapy' (1932) and opened his Beacon, New York, sanatorium and psychodrama stage in 1936, which is where it took formal shape in the 1930s. The answer used here, b, stands either way.", "traps": {"a": "'Karl Schneider' founded no therapy; the name garbles Kurt Schneider (1923), the descriptive psychiatrist of psychopathic personalities and first-rank symptoms, who did not work with groups or drama.", "c": "Albert Bandura (1961, 1977) is known for observational learning, the Bobo doll studies and self-efficacy; his modelling therapy works through watching others, not enacting one's own life on a stage.", "d": "Abraham Maslow (1943, 1954) is the humanistic theorist of the hierarchy of needs and self-actualisation; he did not create a dramatic group method."}}, {"q": "With aging there is a decline of ______ intelligence, but not of ______ intelligence.", "concept": "The answer is c: with ageing, fluid intelligence declines, but crystallised intelligence does not. Raymond Cattell (1963) and his student John Horn (1966) split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on stored knowledge, such as spotting the rule in a matrix puzzle or working out an unfamiliar gadget. It depends heavily on processing speed and working memory, peaks in the early twenties and then slowly declines. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of knowledge, vocabulary and skills built up through education and experience. Because it keeps growing with use, it holds steady or even rises into old age. K. Warner Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study (begun 1956) followed adults for decades and found that most mental abilities peak in middle adulthood, while perceptual speed declines from early adulthood. Timothy Salthouse (1996) traced much of the fluid loss to a general slowing of processing. Example: Riya's grandmother, aged 72, finishes the newspaper crossword before anyone else and knows every rare word (crystallised), but takes far longer than Riya to learn a new phone app (fluid). Option c is right because it puts the declining ability (fluid) in the first blank and the stable one (crystallised) in the second, exactly as the stem requires. Exam trap: examiners reverse the order (option d) to catch students who remember the pair but not which one falls; think 'fluid flows away, crystals last'. They also invent a 'fixed' intelligence, which is not a type in any theory; it echoes Carol Dweck's (2006) fixed mindset, a belief about ability rather than a kind of ability.", "traps": {"a": "Fluid is correct for the first blank, but 'fixed' intelligence is not a type in Raymond Cattell and John Horn's theory or any other; the word borrows from Carol Dweck's fixed mindset, which is a belief about ability.", "b": "This invents a 'fixed' intelligence and also places fluid intelligence in the stable slot, when fluid reasoning is precisely the ability that declines with age.", "d": "This reverses the finding of Raymond Cattell and John Horn: crystallised knowledge holds or grows into old age, while fluid reasoning is what declines."}}, {"q": "Positive symptoms of schizophrenia are all except:", "concept": "The answer is d: feeling disconnected from emotions is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia. Schizophrenia symptoms are sorted into groups. Positive symptoms are additions to normal experience, an excess or distortion of normal function: hallucinations, delusions, disorganised thinking and speech, and grossly disorganised or bizarre behaviour. 'Positive' means something is present that should not be, not that it is good. Negative symptoms are subtractions, a loss or reduction of normal function: affective flattening (blunted emotional expression), alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (lack of drive), anhedonia (loss of pleasure) and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) tied mainly positive symptoms to his Type I schizophrenia and mainly negative ones to Type II, and Nancy Andreasen (1982, 1984) rated the two groups separately with the SANS and SAPS. A sense of being cut off from one's own feelings belongs with the negative side (blunted affect, emotional withdrawal, anhedonia) or, as a strange detached feeling, with depersonalisation, a dissociative experience. Either way it is a loss, not an addition. Example: Arjun, aged 24, hears a voice commenting on his actions, is sure his neighbours are plotting to poison him and jumps from topic to topic when he talks; months later he also says he feels nothing, even at his sister's wedding. The first three are positive symptoms; the last is negative. Option d is right because it is the only option that describes something missing. Exam trap: 'confused thinking' sounds like a deficit, but disorganised thinking, a formal thought disorder, is counted with the positive symptoms in DSM-5, even though Peter Liddle (1987) treated disorganisation as a separate third syndrome.", "traps": {"a": "Hearing voices is an auditory hallucination, a perception without an external object and the commonest hallucination in schizophrenia, so it is a positive symptom, not the exception.", "b": "Confused thinking is disorganised thinking or formal thought disorder (derailment, loose associations, incoherence), which DSM-5 lists with the positive symptoms, so it belongs to the group.", "c": "Believing someone is plotting against you is a persecutory delusion, a fixed false belief held against evidence and a classic positive symptom."}}, {"q": "Internal standards used to judge stimuli are referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is c: internal standards used to judge stimuli are called frames of reference. We never judge a stimulus in absolute terms; we rate it against a standard we carry inside, built up from past experience and from the surroundings, and that standard is the frame of reference. Muzafer Sherif (1935, 1936) showed one forming in the autokinetic experiment: a still point of light in a dark room seems to move, each person judging alone settled on a personal range, groups then converged on a shared norm, and members kept using that norm when later tested alone. Harry Helson (1947, 1964) made the idea measurable in adaptation-level theory; his 1948 paper is titled 'Adaptation-level as a basis for a quantitative theory of frames of reference'. The adaptation level is the neutral point of one such frame, pooled from the focal stimulus, the background stimuli around it and residual stimuli (traces of past experience); a stimulus above it feels heavy, bright or warm, one below it light, dim or cool. A classic demonstration: put one hand in hot water and the other in cold, then both in lukewarm water; the same water feels cool to the first hand and warm to the second. Example: Kabir lifts a 20 kg sack of rice in his uncle's shop, then picks up a 5 kg bag, which feels surprisingly light, because his frame of reference for weight has moved up. Option c is right because 'frames of reference' is the general name for the internal standards, and adaptation level is Helson's name for the neutral point inside one. Exam trap: adaptation level is the planted near miss. Key note: WBJEEB's final answer keys mark c both times this question was set, JEMAS(PG) 2023 and JEMAS(PG) 2024. This site keyed a (adaptation level) before the official keys were found; a is the narrower Helson term, so c is the answer to give.", "traps": {"a": "Adaptation level is Harry Helson's name for the neutral point inside one frame of reference (his 1948 paper calls it a quantitative theory of frames of reference); it is a part of the frame, not the general name for internal standards, and WBJEEB's final key does not accept it.", "b": "Intuition is knowing or judging something quickly without conscious reasoning; it is not a standard or reference point against which stimuli are rated.", "d": "Context is the external setting around a stimulus; it feeds the frame of reference as background stimulation, but it is not itself the internal standard."}}, {"q": "______ theory maximized and ______ theory minimized the role of the unconscious.", "concept": "The answer is b: psychoanalytic theory maximised the role of the unconscious, and behaviourist theory minimised it. Sigmund Freud (1900), in The Interpretation of Dreams and later work, placed the unconscious at the centre of personality. In his topographic model the conscious mind is only the tip of an iceberg; below lie the preconscious (memories that can easily be recalled) and the vast unconscious, holding repressed wishes, conflicts and drives that shape dreams, slips of the tongue, symptoms and everyday choices. No other approach gives the unconscious so much weight. Behaviourism went to the opposite extreme. John B. Watson (1913) argued that psychology should study only observable behaviour, and B. F. Skinner (1953) explained personality as learned responses shaped by reinforcement from the environment. Since unconscious wishes cannot be observed or measured, behaviourists set them aside altogether as unscientific. Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers (1951) and Abraham Maslow (1954) also played down the unconscious, stressing conscious experience, choice and growth, but Rogers still allowed that threatening experiences can be denied or distorted out of awareness, so humanism downplays the unconscious rather than discarding it. Example: when Riya keeps arriving late to meetings with her strict manager, a Freudian looks for hidden hostility, while a behaviourist asks what the lateness has been reinforced by, such as escaping an unpleasant conversation. Option b is right because it pairs the approach most built on the unconscious with the approach that removed it from psychology. Exam trap: option c is the near miss, since humanists also rejected Freud's dark unconscious; but the extreme minimum belongs to behaviourism.", "traps": {"a": "Trait theorists such as Gordon Allport and Raymond Cattell describe stable, measurable dispositions and say little about the unconscious, so trait theory cannot be the approach that maximised it.", "c": "Psychoanalytic is right for the first blank, but humanistic theory (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) only played down the unconscious; Rogers still accepted that experiences can be denied to awareness, whereas behaviourism set the unconscious aside entirely.", "d": "Behaviourist fits the second blank, but trait theory never maximised the unconscious; Gordon Allport even stressed that healthy adults are guided by conscious, present motives."}}, {"q": "The presentation of an aversive stimulus or the removal of a positive stimulus are both examples of:", "concept": "The answer is b: presenting an aversive stimulus and removing a positive stimulus are both forms of punishment. In operant conditioning, Edward Thorndike's (1911) law of effect and B. F. Skinner's (1938) experiments showed that consequences change how often a behaviour occurs. Two questions classify every consequence. First, does the behaviour become more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment)? Second, is a stimulus added (positive) or taken away (negative)? Here positive and negative mean plus and minus, not good and bad. Positive punishment adds something unpleasant after a behaviour: a scolding, a slap, extra homework. Negative punishment removes something pleasant: time-out from play, a fine (response cost), losing phone time. Both make the behaviour less likely, so both are punishment. By contrast, positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise, money), and negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a headache ends after taking a tablet, the seat-belt buzzer stops when you buckle up); both make the behaviour more likely. Skinner argued that punishment suppresses behaviour only temporarily, teaches nothing about what to do instead and can breed fear and aggression, so reinforcing a better behaviour usually works better. Example: Kabir's mother scolds him when he hits his sister (positive punishment) and also takes away his cricket bat for a day (negative punishment); both aim to reduce hitting. Option b is right because adding an aversive stimulus and removing a pleasant one are the two forms of punishment. Exam trap: negative reinforcement (option a) is the classic confusion; it removes something unpleasant and so increases behaviour, the reverse of punishment.", "traps": {"a": "Negative reinforcement removes an unpleasant stimulus when the behaviour occurs (switching off a shock, ending a nagging), so the behaviour increases; punishment does the opposite and decreases it.", "c": "Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus (praise, a sweet) after a behaviour to make it more likely; neither half of the stem adds anything pleasant.", "d": "Secondary (conditioned) reinforcement is a stimulus that gains its power by being paired with a primary reinforcer, such as money or tokens in a token economy; it strengthens behaviour rather than weakening it."}}, {"q": "Which of these is NOT considered to be a cardinal feature of Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "concept": "The answer is a: eccentric behaviour with odd patterns of thinking and communication is not a cardinal feature of borderline personality disorder. It describes schizotypal personality disorder, which sits in Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, alongside paranoid and schizoid personality disorders. Borderline personality disorder (BPD) belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. DSM-IV-TR (2000) and DSM-5 (2013) define it as a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood, and require 5 of 9 criteria: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability, with intense mood shifts lasting hours; chronic emptiness; intense, poorly controlled anger; and brief, stress-related paranoid ideas or dissociation. Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organisation, with splitting (seeing people as all good or all bad) as its central defence. Marsha Linehan (1993) built dialectical behaviour therapy for BPD on her biosocial theory: emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment. Example: Riya, 23, idolises a new friend one week and calls her a traitor the next, cuts herself after arguments and says she does not know who she really is; that pattern points to BPD. Option a is right because odd thinking and eccentric communication belong to schizotypal, not borderline, personality disorder. Exam trap: BPD does include transient, stress-related paranoid ideas, so students sometimes accept 'odd thinking' as borderline; but enduring eccentric thought and speech is the schizotypal signature.", "traps": {"b": "An enduring pattern of unstable, intense relationships, swinging between idealisation and devaluation, is a core DSM criterion for BPD, so it is a cardinal feature, not the exception.", "c": "A lack of a stable self-image is the DSM identity disturbance criterion and matches Otto Kernberg's identity diffusion, so it is a cardinal BPD feature.", "d": "Frequent, unpredictable mood swings describe affective instability (marked reactivity of mood lasting hours, rarely days), a core BPD criterion."}}, {"q": "The first intelligence test was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the first intelligence test was developed by Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905). In 1904 the French Ministry of Education asked Alfred Binet to find a way to identify children who could not keep up in ordinary classes and needed special schooling. Earlier attempts, such as Francis Galton's (1884) anthropometric laboratory in London and James McKeen Cattell's (1890) 'mental tests', measured reaction time and sensory sharpness, and they failed to predict school success. Binet and the physician Simon took a different route, measuring higher mental processes: judgement, comprehension, reasoning and memory. Their 1905 Binet-Simon scale had 30 items arranged in order of difficulty. The 1908 revision grouped items by the age at which most children passed them, giving the concept of mental age, and the 1911 revision extended it towards adults. Binet insisted the score was a practical guide for helping children, not a fixed measure of inborn ability. Later, Lewis Terman (1916) adapted it at Stanford University as the Stanford-Binet, using William Stern's (1912) intelligence quotient. Example: a teacher in Kolkata worried about Meera, aged 9, could use a Binet-style scale to find that she passes the items typical of 7-year-olds, a mental age of 7, signalling that she needs extra support. Option a is right because the 1905 Binet-Simon scale is recognised as the first practical intelligence test. Exam trap: option c sounds right because the Stanford-Binet is the most famous descendant, but 'Stanford' is a university, not a person, and Terman's revision came eleven years later.", "traps": {"b": "Charles Spearman (1904) proposed the two-factor theory and general intelligence (g) using factor analysis; he theorised about intelligence but did not build the first test.", "c": "'Stanford' is Stanford University, where Lewis Terman revised the Binet-Simon scale as the Stanford-Binet (1916); it is a later adaptation, and no person named Stanford co-wrote it.", "d": "Lewis Terman (Stanford-Binet, 1916) and David Wechsler (Wechsler-Bellevue, 1939) built later, influential tests, never together, and both came after Binet and Simon's 1905 scale."}}, {"q": "Porteus developed an early performance test based on:", "concept": "The answer is b: Stanley David Porteus (1914) built his early performance test on mazes. Porteus, an Australian teacher who later worked in the United States and Hawaii, wanted to measure the practical intelligence of children labelled feeble-minded, for whom the language-heavy Binet scale seemed unfair. A performance test is one in which the person does something with materials (arranges blocks, fits shapes, traces a path) instead of answering in words, so it reduces the part played by language and schooling. The Porteus Maze Test is a graded series of printed mazes of rising difficulty, each assigned to an age level. The person traces a route from the entrance to the exit with a pencil and must avoid blind alleys; entering one counts as a failed trial and a fresh copy is given. Porteus argued that the mazes tap planning, foresight and prudence, the ability to look ahead before acting, which he felt the Binet missed. Scores give a test age and a test quotient, and a separate qualitative score counts careless errors such as cutting corners, crossing lines and lifting the pencil, which was later linked to impulsivity and delinquency. Because maze planning depends on the frontal lobes, the test was used in the 1940s and 1950s to study the effects of psychosurgery, and today it is seen as an early measure of executive function. Example: ten-year-old Arjun, who barely reads, rushes into dead ends without looking ahead; the maze shows weak planning, not weak vocabulary. Option b is right because mazes are the defining material of the Porteus test. Exam trap: every option names a classic non-verbal material, so match the material to its maker: coloured blocks to Samuel C. Kohs, drawing a person to Florence Goodenough, mazes to Porteus.", "traps": {"a": "Block design belongs to Samuel C. Kohs (1920), whose coloured cubes are arranged to copy printed patterns; David Wechsler later borrowed it as a subtest. It is a performance test, but not the one Porteus built.", "c": "Non-verbal cues are body language and facial signals studied in communication research; no classic performance test is built on them, and the phrase only sounds like \"non-verbal test\".", "d": "Drawing a person is Florence Goodenough's Draw-a-Man test (1926), revised by Dale Harris (1963); it estimates intellectual maturity from the details in a drawing, not from maze tracing."}}, {"q": "What proportion of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z score of +1?", "concept": "The answer is b: about 16% of scores in a normal distribution lie above a z-score of +1. The normal curve is a symmetrical bell in which the mean, median and mode coincide, and the areas under it are fixed. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first derived it, and Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) used it for errors in measurement, hence the name Gaussian curve. A z-score says how many standard deviations (SDs) a score lies from the mean. About 34.13% of cases fall between the mean and +1 SD, and another 34.13% between the mean and minus 1 SD; 13.59% lie between 1 and 2 SD on each side, and 2.14% between 2 and 3 SD. So about 68% fall within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2 SD and 99.7% within 3 SD. To find the area above z = +1, start from 50%, the whole upper half, and subtract the 34.13% between the mean and +1: that leaves 15.87%, about 16%. Equivalently, z = +1 is the 84th percentile, because 50% plus 34% lie below it. On a deviation IQ scale with mean 100 and SD 15, z = +1 is an IQ of 115, so about 16 in every 100 people score above 115. Example: Meera's z-score on a reasoning test is +1; only about 16% of the norm group scored higher than she did. Option b is right because 50% minus 34% leaves about 16%. Exam trap: students who remember 34% or 68% for one SD forget to subtract from 50, or confuse 'above +1' with 'between the mean and +1'; and 50% (option a) is the area above the mean itself, at z = 0.", "traps": {"a": "50% is the area above the mean (z = 0), the whole upper half of the curve; by z = +1 another 34% has been passed, leaving about 16%.", "c": "25% is the area above the third quartile, which lies at about z = +0.67, not at z = +1.", "d": "10% is the area above about z = +1.28 (the 90th percentile), so it is too small for z = +1."}}, {"q": "The Stroop Color-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Stroop Colour-Word Interference Test is usually used as a test of attention. John Ridley Stroop (1935) asked people either to read colour words printed in a conflicting ink, such as the word RED printed in green, or to name the ink colour. Reading was barely affected, but naming the ink took far longer (about 74% longer than naming plain colour patches) and errors rose. This slowing is the Stroop effect. It happens because reading is automatic for literate adults: the word's meaning is processed whether we want it or not, and it competes with the weaker, controlled task of naming the colour. To succeed, a person must select the relevant feature (the ink) and inhibit the automatic response (reading). That is why clinicians use it as a measure of selective attention, response inhibition and interference control, all part of executive function linked to the anterior cingulate and prefrontal cortex. Standard versions such as Charles Golden's (1978) have word-reading, colour-naming and colour-word cards, and an interference score compares them. Poor scores appear after frontal injury and in ADHD, schizophrenia and dementia; an emotional Stroop, using threat words, shows attentional bias in anxiety. Example: Kabir, recovering from a head injury, reads the colour words quickly but slows badly and makes errors when asked to name the ink colours; his neuropsychologist notes poor inhibitory control. Option d is right because the test measures the ability to control attention against an automatic response. Exam trap: because the test uses words and colours, examiners offer verbal fluency and visual perception; but fluency means generating words (as in the FAS test), and normal colour vision is simply assumed.", "traps": {"a": "Verbal fluency tests, such as the FAS (Controlled Oral Word Association) test, ask the person to generate as many words as possible from a letter or category in a minute; the Stroop asks for no word generation.", "b": "Visual perception tests, such as Arthur Benton's Judgment of Line Orientation, assess how a person sees shape and space; the Stroop assumes normal colour vision and measures the control of attention instead.", "c": "Memory is tested by tools such as the Wechsler Memory Scale or the Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test; the Stroop stimuli stay in front of the person throughout, so nothing has to be remembered."}}, {"q": "Emotional responses to a traumatic event are most directly under the control of the:", "concept": "The answer is c: emotional responses to a traumatic event, such as a pounding heart, fast breathing, sweating and a dry mouth, are most directly controlled by the autonomic nervous system (ANS). The ANS is the part of the peripheral nervous system that runs the internal organs (heart, blood vessels, lungs, gut and glands) largely without conscious control. It has two branches that work in opposite directions. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for emergencies, which Walter Cannon (1915) called the fight-or-flight response: it raises heart rate and blood pressure, widens the pupils, slows digestion and makes the adrenal medulla release adrenaline. The parasympathetic branch calms the body afterwards (rest and digest), slowing the heart and restarting digestion. Imagine Arjun in a road accident: within seconds his heart races, his palms sweat and his stomach knots, all sympathetic effects, and an hour later his parasympathetic branch brings him back to baseline. The hypothalamus and amygdala in the brain trigger these changes, but the ANS is the system that actually produces the bodily side of emotion. The polygraph (lie detector) works by recording exactly these autonomic changes. Option c is right because the stem asks which system most directly controls the emotional reaction, and the involuntary arousal of fear and stress is autonomic. Exam trap: the somatic nervous system sounds bodily, but it carries sensation and controls the voluntary skeletal muscles; the gut-level, involuntary arousal of emotion belongs to the autonomic system.", "traps": {"a": "The somatic nervous system carries sensory input and commands to the voluntary skeletal muscles; it lets you run from danger but does not produce the involuntary racing heart and sweating of emotion.", "b": "The central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) contains the hypothalamus and amygdala that start an emotional reaction, but it acts on the body through the autonomic system, which is the more direct controller the stem asks for.", "d": "The cerebral cortex appraises and labels an emotion and can partly regulate it, but it does not directly drive heart rate, breathing or sweating."}}, {"q": "Damage to the cerebellum would most likely result in:", "concept": "The answer is c: damage to the cerebellum most likely causes a loss of muscular coordination. The cerebellum ('little brain') sits at the back of the brain, beneath the occipital lobes and behind the pons, as part of the hindbrain. It does not start movements; the motor cortex does that. Instead it compares the movement you intended with the movement actually happening, using feedback from muscles, joints and the balance organs of the inner ear, and smooths out the errors. It also stores well-practised motor skills and timing, such as cycling or typing, and takes part in simple conditioning such as the eyeblink reflex. Damage produces ataxia: an unsteady, wide-based, staggering walk, poor balance, overshooting when reaching for an object (dysmetria), a tremor that grows as the hand nears its target (intention tremor) and slurred speech. Damage on one side affects the same side of the body. Alcohol depresses the cerebellum early, which is why roadside sobriety tests ask a driver to walk a straight line or touch the nose. For example, after a cerebellar stroke Kabir may know exactly what he wants to do and have full strength, yet he sways when he walks and spills tea when he lifts a cup. Option c is right because coordination, balance and the smooth timing of movement are the cerebellum's core jobs. Exam trap: heart and breathing failure tempts students because the cerebellum lies close to the brainstem, but those vital centres sit in the medulla oblongata.", "traps": {"a": "Heart stoppage or respiratory failure follows damage to the medulla oblongata, the brainstem structure that holds the cardiac and respiratory centres, not the cerebellum.", "b": "Loss of hearing follows damage to the ear, the auditory nerve or the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus), not the cerebellum.", "d": "Reduced emotional response points to the limbic system, especially the amygdala, or to the prefrontal cortex; the cerebellum is a motor coordination structure."}}, {"q": "In Guilford's approach to intelligence, the ability to generate a variety of hypotheses in a given problem situation is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: in J. P. Guilford's model, the ability to generate a variety of hypotheses is divergent production. Joy Paul Guilford (1956, 1967) rejected the idea of a single general intelligence. In his Structure of Intellect model every ability is a combination of three dimensions: an operation (what the mind does), a content (what it works on) and a product (the form the result takes). The five operations are cognition (recognising and understanding), memory (storing and recalling), divergent production (producing many varied answers to an open problem), convergent production (arriving at the one correct answer) and evaluation (judging whether an answer is right or good). The contents were figural, symbolic, semantic and behavioural, and the products were units, classes, relations, systems, transformations and implications, giving 5 x 4 x 6 = 120 abilities, later revised to 150 and then 180. Divergent production became the basis for the study of creativity, which Guilford had urged in his 1950 address to the American Psychological Association. It is scored for fluency (how many ideas), flexibility (how many kinds), originality (how rare) and elaboration (how much detail). Example: Meera's plant has wilted, and her teacher asks for every possible reason; Meera suggests too little water, too much water, root rot, pests, a sudden cold night and the wrong soil. Producing many hypotheses is divergent production; picking the single correct cause after testing is convergent production. Option c is right because generating a range of possibilities is the definition of divergent production. Exam trap: convergent production also involves problem solving, but it narrows down to one answer instead of opening up many, and examiners rely on students mixing up the two similar-sounding words.", "traps": {"a": "'Cognitive memory' blends two separate operations in J. P. Guilford's model, cognition (understanding information) and memory (retaining it); neither involves producing many new possibilities.", "b": "Convergent production, in J. P. Guilford's model, is reasoning towards the single best or correct answer, as in most IQ test items; the stem asks for a variety of hypotheses, which is the opposite.", "d": "'Mediational memory' is not one of J. P. Guilford's operations; it borrows the idea of mediators (linking words or images that aid recall) from verbal learning research and names nothing in his model."}}, {"q": "The Ebbinghaus forgetting curve shows:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Ebbinghaus forgetting curve shows a steep initial drop in retention followed by a gradual decline. Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885), in Über das Gedächtnis (Memory), was the first to study memory experimentally. Acting as his own only participant, he learned lists of nonsense syllables such as DAX or BUP, chosen so that meaning and old associations could not help him. He learned each list to one perfect recitation, waited a set time from 20 minutes to 31 days, and then relearned it. Relearning was faster even when he could recall nothing, so he measured memory by the savings method: the percentage of the original learning time saved. Plotting savings against time gave the forgetting curve. It falls very steeply at first, with more than half the material lost within about an hour and about two thirds within a day, then flattens into a slow, gradual decline that never quite reaches zero. Jaap Murre and Joeri Dros (2015) repeated the experiment and obtained a very similar curve. Ebbinghaus also noted the spacing effect: practice spread out over time beats cramming. Example: Riya learns 30 new anatomy terms at 9 in the morning; by lunch she has lost many of them, but much of what survives the first day is still there a week later. Spaced revision would soften the early drop. Option b is right because it gives the correct shape: fast loss first, then slow loss. Exam trap: option a reverses the order of the two phases, and examiners rely on students remembering 'steep' and 'gradual' but not which comes first.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses Hermann Ebbinghaus's finding: forgetting is fastest right after learning and slows later, not gradual first and steep afterwards.", "c": "An S-shaped (sigmoid) curve, with a slow start, a rapid rise and a plateau, describes some learning curves; the forgetting curve is a falling curve that drops fast and then levels off, not an S.", "d": "Hermann Ebbinghaus used nonsense syllables precisely to remove meaning, so his curve says nothing about concept learning; later work, such as Frederic Bartlett's (1932), shows meaningful material is retained better than rote material, so d is also backwards."}}, {"q": "Anterograde amnesia results from the injury of the:", "concept": "The answer is a: anterograde amnesia results from injury to the hippocampus. Anterograde amnesia is the inability to form new long-term memories after the onset of brain damage; retrograde amnesia is the loss of memories formed before it. The hippocampus, a curved structure inside each medial temporal lobe, is needed to consolidate new facts and events (declarative memory) into lasting storage. The classic evidence is the patient H.M., Henry Molaison. In 1953 the surgeon William Beecher Scoville removed most of both hippocampi, with nearby medial temporal tissue, to stop his severe epilepsy. William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported the result: a profound anterograde amnesia. He reread the same magazines with fresh interest, never learned his new address and met his doctors as strangers for decades. Yet his intelligence, short-term memory (digit span) and childhood memories were largely intact, and over three days he improved at mirror drawing while denying he had ever done the task, which showed that procedural memory does not depend on the hippocampus. Example: after a brain infection damages both of his hippocampi, Arjun can still describe his school days but cannot remember the nurse he met ten minutes ago. Option a is right because hippocampal damage is the textbook cause of anterograde amnesia. Exam trap: the mammillary bodies at the base of the hypothalamus are damaged in Korsakoff's syndrome, which also brings anterograde amnesia, so 'hypothalamus' can look tempting; but the hippocampus is the structure the classic evidence points to, and the cerebellum handles motor skill, not new facts.", "traps": {"b": "The hypothalamus regulates homeostasis: hunger, thirst, temperature, sex and hormone release through the pituitary. Its mammillary bodies are damaged in Korsakoff's syndrome, but injury to the hypothalamus as such is not the classic cause of anterograde amnesia.", "c": "The pituitary gland is the 'master' endocrine gland that releases hormones such as growth hormone and ACTH under hypothalamic control; it plays no direct role in forming new memories.", "d": "The cerebellum coordinates movement and supports motor and procedural learning, such as eyeblink conditioning; its damage causes ataxia and clumsiness, not a loss of new facts and events."}}, {"q": "The Type I error describes the likelihood of an experimenter:", "concept": "The answer is a: a Type I error is concluding that a result is significant when it is not. Every significance test starts with a null hypothesis, which says there is no effect or no difference. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) named the two ways a test can go wrong. A Type I error is rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true: the researcher declares an effect that does not exist, a false positive. Its probability is alpha, the significance level the researcher sets in advance, usually 0.05, which means accepting a 5 per cent risk of such a false alarm. A Type II error is the opposite: failing to reject a null hypothesis that is false, so a real effect is missed, a false negative. Its probability is beta, and statistical power is 1 minus beta. Lowering alpha (to 0.01, say) cuts the Type I risk but raises the Type II risk unless the sample is enlarged. Example: Arjun tests whether a herbal tablet improves memory; his result is p = 0.03, so he rejects the null and reports that the tablet works. If in truth the tablet does nothing, he has made a Type I error. If the tablet really helped but his small sample gave p = 0.20, he would have made a Type II error. Option a is right because 'considering the result to be significant when it is not' is a false positive. Exam trap: students reverse the two; think of the boy who cried wolf: the villagers first believe in a wolf that is not there (Type I), then ignore the wolf that is (Type II).", "traps": {"b": "Measuring variability within a distribution is the job of descriptive statistics such as the range, variance and standard deviation; it is not an error of hypothesis testing.", "c": "Failing to take variance into account is a general flaw in analysis (for example, ignoring unequal variances between groups), not the defined meaning of a Type I error.", "d": "Failing to take variable weightings into account describes a problem in combining predictors, as with regression weights; it has nothing to do with the false-positive error."}}, {"q": "The term social loafing refers to:", "concept": "The answer is c: social loafing means people work harder individually than in groups. Social loafing is the drop in effort that occurs when people's contributions are pooled in a group, compared with when each works alone and can be identified. The first evidence came from the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann (1913): when men pulled on a rope, the group's total pull was less than the sum of the members pulling separately, and each person's share fell as the group grew. Part of this could be poor coordination, but Alan Ingham and colleagues (1974) showed that blindfolded people who merely believed others were pulling with them also pulled less, so motivation drops too. Bibb Latane, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named the effect: blindfolded students wearing headphones clapped and shouted less loudly per person in supposed groups than alone. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) explained it with the collective effort model: people work hard when they believe their effort matters and will be noticed. Loafing shrinks when individual output is identifiable, the task is meaningful or the group is close-knit. Example: in Riya's four-member college project, two members contribute little because the teacher grades only the final report; when the teacher asks each student to submit a named section, everyone works harder. Option c is right because it captures the essence: less effort in the group than alone. Exam trap: option a uses the everyday meaning of 'loafing' (idling), and option d describes social facilitation, a different group effect.", "traps": {"a": "Playing when expected to be working is the everyday sense of 'loafing' (idling or slacking); the social psychology term is specifically about reduced effort when efforts are pooled in a group.", "b": "Working harder in groups than alone is the reverse of social loafing; it resembles social compensation (Kipling Williams and Steven Karau, 1991), where people work harder to make up for weak teammates.", "d": "The opposite of social inhibition is social facilitation, better performance on simple tasks in the presence of others (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965), not loafing."}}, {"q": "A physical stimulus is converted into a neural impulse by a process known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: the conversion of a physical stimulus into a neural impulse is called transduction. Every sense organ contains receptors, cells specialised for one form of energy. Their job is to translate that energy into the only language the nervous system understands: changes in membrane voltage that lead to nerve impulses. In the eye, light is absorbed by photopigments such as rhodopsin in the rods and cones; this changes the receptor's membrane potential, and the signal passes through bipolar cells to ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve. In the ear, sound waves bend the stereocilia of hair cells in the cochlea, opening ion channels. In the skin, pressure deforms mechanoreceptors; in the nose and tongue, chemicals bind to receptor proteins. In each case the first change is a graded receptor potential whose size follows the strength of the stimulus; this then triggers all-or-none impulses whose rate codes intensity. Because all impulses look alike, Johannes Peter Muller (1801 to 1858) proposed the doctrine of specific nerve energies: the quality of a sensation depends on which nerve is stimulated, which is why pressing on a closed eye makes you see flashes of light. Sensation is detection and transduction; perception is the later interpretation by the brain. Example: Riya steps into sunlight; within milliseconds her rods and cones turn light into electrical signals, and only then can her brain recognise her friend's face across the road. Option c is right because transduction is the technical term for converting stimulus energy into neural signals at the receptor. Exam trap: all four options begin with \"trans-\"; only transduction is the sensory term, while transposition and transaction belong to other areas of psychology.", "traps": {"a": "Transposition is a Gestalt idea: a melody keeps its identity when every note is shifted (Christian von Ehrenfels, 1890), and the animals of Wolfgang Kohler (1918) learned relations such as \"the lighter one\" rather than absolute stimuli. It is not sensory coding.", "b": "Transaction appears in the transactional analysis of Eric Berne (1961) and the transactional model of stress of Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984); it means an exchange between people, or between person and environment, not energy conversion.", "d": "Transition is an everyday word for any change from one state to another, as in life transitions; it is not the name for how receptors code a stimulus."}}, {"q": "The somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the somatosensory area of the cortex is responsible for our sense of touch. The primary somatosensory cortex lies in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, just behind the central sulcus, facing the primary motor cortex across that groove. It receives signals from receptors in the skin, muscles and joints, relayed through the spinal cord and the thalamus, and gives us touch, pressure, vibration, temperature, pain and the sense of body position. Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950) stimulated this strip in conscious patients during brain surgery; patients reported tingling in particular body parts, which let them draw the sensory homunculus. The map is contralateral (the left hemisphere feels the right side of the body) and distorted: the lips, tongue and fingertips have huge areas because they are packed with receptors, while the back has a small one. Damage here causes numbness or difficulty recognising objects by touch (astereognosis). Vilayanur Ramachandran (1992) showed that after an arm amputation the face area can take over the hand area, so a touch on the face is felt in the phantom hand. Example: Zoya, with her eyes closed, tells a coin from a key in her right pocket; the signals from her fingertips are analysed in her left postcentral gyrus. Option d is right because touch and body sense are the job of the somatosensory strip; 'somato' means body. Exam trap: each sense has its own primary area, and examiners mix them: vision is in the occipital lobe, hearing in the temporal lobe (Heschl's gyrus), and taste in the insula and frontal operculum, which sit close to the face area but form a separate gustatory cortex.", "traps": {"a": "Taste is processed in the gustatory cortex of the insula and frontal operculum, reached through cranial nerves VII, IX and X and the thalamus, not in the postcentral gyrus.", "b": "Sight is processed in the primary visual (striate) cortex around the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe.", "c": "Hearing is processed in the primary auditory cortex in Heschl's gyrus of the temporal lobe."}}, {"q": "The glands of the body serve as:", "concept": "The answer is b: the glands of the body serve as effectors. In the nervous system, information flows in a loop. Receptors, such as the rods and cones of the eye or the touch receptors in the skin, detect a stimulus and turn it into nerve signals. Afferent (sensory) neurons carry those signals to the brain and spinal cord. After the central nervous system processes them, efferent (motor) neurons carry commands out to the effectors, the organs that actually carry out the response. There are two kinds of effectors: muscles, which contract (skeletal, smooth and cardiac), and glands, which secrete. Exocrine glands, such as the salivary, sweat and tear glands, release their products through ducts; endocrine glands, such as the thyroid and adrenal glands, release hormones into the blood. Together these parts make up the reflex arc: receptor, afferent neuron, interneuron, efferent neuron, effector. Ivan Pavlov's (1897) work on digestion is a reminder that a gland can be the effector of a reflex, since salivation is a glandular response. Example: when Kabir smells his mother's biryani, receptors in his nose fire, signals reach his brain, and efferent nerves set his salivary glands secreting; the gland is the effector. Option b is right because glands carry out responses on command from nerves or hormones rather than detecting stimuli. Exam trap: students picture effectors only as muscles that move, so a gland, which does not move, seems wrong; but secretion is a response just as contraction is.", "traps": {"a": "Receptors are the specialised cells that detect stimuli (photoreceptors, hair cells, touch receptors) at the start of the reflex arc; glands sit at the end and carry out the response.", "c": "'Nodes' usually means the nodes of Ranvier, the gaps in an axon's myelin sheath where the impulse jumps during saltatory conduction, or the lymph nodes of the immune system; neither describes what glands do.", "d": "Afferent nerves carry sensory signals towards the central nervous system; glands receive commands through efferent nerves and are not nerves at all."}}, {"q": "The principal function of myelin is to:", "concept": "The answer is c: the principal function of myelin is to increase the speed at which an action potential travels along the axon. Myelin is a fatty insulating sheath wrapped in many layers around axons. In the central nervous system it is made by oligodendrocytes, each of which can wrap segments of several axons; in the peripheral nervous system it is made by Schwann cells, each wrapping one segment of one axon. Between the segments are short bare gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, named after Louis-Antoine Ranvier (1835 to 1922), where voltage-gated sodium channels are concentrated. Because ions cannot leak across the myelinated stretches, current spreads quickly inside the axon to the next node, and the action potential is regenerated only at the nodes. The impulse seems to jump from node to node: saltatory conduction (Latin saltare, to leap). Thin unmyelinated axons conduct at about 1 metre per second; large myelinated axons at up to about 100 metres per second. Myelin also saves energy, because fewer ions must be pumped back after each impulse. Myelination continues through childhood and adolescence, and the prefrontal cortex is among the last regions to finish, in the twenties. Example: Meera, 29, develops blurred vision in one eye and tingling in her legs. An MRI shows patches of lost myelin in her brain and spinal cord: multiple sclerosis, in which the immune system attacks CNS myelin, so conduction slows or fails. Option c is right because myelin's job is faster propagation of the action potential. Exam trap: myelin changes the speed, not the size, of the impulse; by the all-or-none law, every action potential in a given neuron has the same amplitude.", "traps": {"a": "The amount of transmitter released depends on calcium entry and firing rate at the axon terminal, not on myelin; myelin covers the axon, not the synapse.", "b": "The synaptic cleft is a fixed gap of about 20 to 40 nanometres between neurons, and myelin neither reaches nor changes it.", "d": "Action potentials are all-or-none: their size is fixed for a given neuron, so myelin speeds conduction without making the spike any larger."}}, {"q": "REM sleep behaviour disorder is associated with movements, occasionally violent, during sleep, sometimes characterised as \"acting out dreams\". What normal feature of REM sleep is absent in individuals with this disorder?", "concept": "The answer is a: in REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD) the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep is absent. Normal REM sleep has several signature features: rapid eye movements, a desynchronised EEG of high-frequency, low-amplitude waves that looks almost like waking, vivid dreaming, penile or clitoral engorgement, and atonia, a near-complete paralysis of the skeletal muscles. Atonia is produced by circuits in the pons that drive medullary neurons to inhibit the spinal motor neurons. It stops us acting out our dreams, which is why REM is called paradoxical sleep: an active brain in a paralysed body. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed that cats with lesions in this pontine region rose up during REM and stalked, pounced and attacked unseen prey. Carlos Schenck and Mark Mahowald (1986) described the human disorder. People with RBD punch, kick, shout or leap from bed, mostly in the second half of the night, and on waking can recall a dream that matches the movements, often of being chased or attacked. Injuries to self and bed partner are common. Most patients are men over 50, and RBD often precedes Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies by years. Example: Suresh, 64, dreams of fighting off a thief and punches his wife in his sleep. A sleep study shows REM sleep with bursts of muscle activity on the chin and limb recordings. Option a is right because loss of REM atonia is the defining feature of RBD. Exam trap: RBD is a REM parasomnia; sleepwalking and night terrors are NREM parasomnias arising from slow-wave sleep, when the body is not paralysed in the first place.", "traps": {"b": "Rapid eye movements continue in RBD; they are how the stage is recognised on the sleep study, so they are not the missing feature.", "c": "The desynchronised, high-frequency, low-amplitude EEG of REM is still present in RBD; the brain is in genuine REM, and only the muscle paralysis fails.", "d": "Penile erections are a normal feature of REM sleep in males and are not what is lost in RBD; their presence during sleep is used instead in nocturnal penile tumescence testing for erectile dysfunction."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a neurotransmitter?", "concept": "The answer is d: insulin is not a neurotransmitter; it is a hormone made by the pancreas. A neurotransmitter is a chemical that a neuron stores in vesicles at its axon terminal and releases into the synaptic cleft, where it acts within milliseconds on receptors of the next cell, only a fraction of a micrometre away. A hormone is released by an endocrine (ductless) gland into the blood and travels to distant target cells, acting over minutes to hours. Insulin is a protein hormone released by the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas when blood glucose rises after a meal. It lets muscle, fat and liver cells take up glucose, so blood sugar falls; glucagon from the alpha cells does the opposite. Insulin also reaches the brain from the blood and tells the hypothalamus that the body is fed, but it is not released by neurons at synapses, so it is not counted as a neurotransmitter. Serotonin (from the raphe nuclei: mood, sleep, appetite) and dopamine (from the substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area: movement and reward) are classic monoamine transmitters. Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the tricky one: it is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla, but a few brainstem neurons also release it as a transmitter, so it is both. Example: when Riya eats a plate of jalebis, her pancreas pours insulin into her blood, while inside her brain dopamine is released at synapses of the reward circuit. Option d is right because insulin is the only substance listed that works purely as a blood-borne hormone. Exam trap: epinephrine is the near miss. When a list offers only dopamine, serotonin, noradrenaline and adrenaline, adrenaline is marked as the odd one out because it acts mainly as a hormone; once a pure hormone such as insulin is offered, adrenaline counts as a neurotransmitter and insulin is the answer.", "traps": {"a": "Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) is a monoamine neurotransmitter released by neurons of the raphe nuclei; it regulates mood, sleep and appetite and is the target of SSRIs, so it is a true neurotransmitter.", "b": "Dopamine is a catecholamine neurotransmitter of the nigrostriatal (movement) and mesolimbic (reward) pathways, linked to Parkinson's disease and schizophrenia, so it cannot be the exception.", "c": "Epinephrine (adrenaline) is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla, but some brainstem neurons release it at synapses, so it is both a hormone and a neurotransmitter; that dual role is why it tempts, yet insulin is the purer exception."}}, {"q": "Left hemisphere is to right hemisphere as:", "concept": "The answer is b: the left hemisphere is to the right hemisphere as language functioning is to spatial ability. Lateralisation means that the two cerebral hemispheres, though they look alike, are specialised for different jobs. The first evidence came from brain damage. Paul Broca (1861) studied a patient, Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan', who understood speech but could say only that one syllable; the autopsy showed a lesion in the left frontal lobe. Carl Wernicke (1874) then linked a left temporal lesion to fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension. In most people speech, grammar, reading and writing depend on the left hemisphere; this holds for about 95 percent of right-handers and for most left-handers. The right hemisphere is better at visuospatial tasks such as reading maps, judging depth, drawing, recognising faces and attending to the left side of space, and it also handles the emotional tone (prosody) of speech. The strongest evidence came from split-brain patients tested by Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga in the 1960s: a word shown to the left hemisphere could be named, while the right hemisphere could not name an object but could pick it out by touch and copy drawings better. Example: after a left-hemisphere stroke, Kabir struggles to find words but still finds his way home; after a right parietal stroke, Meera speaks normally but cannot copy a simple drawing and ignores the food on the left of her plate. Option b is right because language versus spatial ability is the classic left versus right division. Exam trap: option d pairs two famous names, but Broca's area (frontal) and Wernicke's area (temporal) both lie in the left hemisphere, so they cannot stand for left versus right. Remember too that lateralisation is a relative bias; the popular 'logical left-brained versus creative right-brained person' is a myth.", "traps": {"a": "Hearing is not lateralised in this way: each ear sends input to the auditory cortex of both temporal lobes, so both hemispheres hear. Only speaking is a left-hemisphere skill, so the pair does not map onto left versus right.", "c": "Balance and the smooth coordination of movement depend mainly on the cerebellum, and each hemisphere's motor cortex moves the opposite side of the body, so neither function belongs to one hemisphere.", "d": "Broca's area (left inferior frontal gyrus, speech production; Paul Broca, 1861) and Wernicke's area (left superior temporal gyrus, comprehension; Carl Wernicke, 1874) are both in the left hemisphere, so this contrasts two left-sided language areas, not the two hemispheres."}}, {"q": "What part of the brain has been surgically altered in \"split-brain\" patients?", "concept": "The answer is a: split-brain patients have had their corpus callosum cut. The corpus callosum is the largest commissure of the brain, a thick band of about 200 million nerve fibres that links the left and right cerebral hemispheres and lets them share information. In people with severe epilepsy that does not respond to drugs, a seizure starting in one hemisphere can race across this bridge to the other. From the 1960s the surgeons Philip Vogel and Joseph Bogen cut the corpus callosum (a corpus callosotomy, often called commissurotomy) to stop the spread; the seizures eased and the patients seemed almost unchanged in daily life. Earlier, Ronald Myers and Roger Sperry (1953) had shown in cats that the corpus callosum transfers learning from one hemisphere to the other. Sperry and his student Michael Gazzaniga then tested the human patients, work that won Sperry the Nobel Prize in 1981. Because the left visual field projects to the right hemisphere, a picture flashed briefly to the left could not be named, since speech sits in the left hemisphere, yet the left hand could find the object by touch. Example: Arjun, who had a callosotomy for epilepsy, fixates a central dot while a spoon is flashed to his left visual field; he says he saw nothing, but his left hand picks the spoon out of a hidden tray. Option a is right because the split brain is defined by cutting the corpus callosum. Exam trap: the two fissures sound surgical but are only grooves on the brain's surface that mark the lobes, and the cerebellum is a separate hindbrain structure that has nothing to do with joining the hemispheres.", "traps": {"b": "The cerebellum (little brain) sits beneath the occipital lobes in the hindbrain and coordinates movement, balance and motor learning; damage causes ataxia, not the split-brain effects.", "c": "The Sylvian fissure (lateral sulcus) is a deep groove separating the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes; it is a surface landmark, not the fibre bridge between the hemispheres.", "d": "The Rolandic fissure (central sulcus) separates the frontal from the parietal lobe, with the primary motor cortex in front and the somatosensory cortex behind; it is a landmark within each hemisphere and is not cut in split-brain surgery."}}, {"q": "The two major divisions of the autonomic nervous system (ANS) are:", "concept": "The answer is c: the two major divisions of the autonomic nervous system are the sympathetic and the parasympathetic. The nervous system divides first into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (all the nerves outside them). The peripheral system has two parts: the somatic system, which carries the senses in and sends commands to the skeletal muscles under voluntary control, and the autonomic system, which runs the heart, smooth muscle and glands largely without our awareness. The autonomic system itself has two divisions that usually act in opposition. The sympathetic division prepares the body for action, which Walter Cannon (1915) called the fight-or-flight response: the heart races, pupils widen, airways open, digestion stops and the liver releases glucose. Its fibres leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord (the thoracolumbar outflow). The parasympathetic division conserves energy, the rest-and-digest state: it slows the heart, narrows the pupils and promotes salivation and digestion. Its fibres leave the brainstem, mostly through the vagus nerve, and the sacral cord (the craniosacral outflow). Most organs receive both, so heart rate reflects the balance between them. Many books add the enteric nervous system of the gut wall as a third division. Example: before her viva, Riya's heart pounds and her mouth goes dry (sympathetic); an hour after a relaxed lunch, her heart slows and she feels drowsy (parasympathetic). Option c is right because sympathetic and parasympathetic are the two branches into which the autonomic system divides. Exam trap: examiners offer pairs from other levels of the hierarchy. Somatic and autonomic are the two parts of the peripheral system, and the brain and spinal cord make up the central system, so neither pair divides the autonomic system.", "traps": {"a": "The somatic system is the voluntary branch of the peripheral nervous system, a sibling of the autonomic system, and 'peripheral' is the larger system that contains them both; neither is a division of the autonomic system.", "b": "The brain and spinal cord together form the central nervous system, which issues autonomic commands but is not itself a division of the autonomic system.", "d": "The parietal lobe (touch, spatial attention) and the occipital lobe (vision) are lobes of the cerebral cortex in the central nervous system, not branches of the autonomic system."}}, {"q": "Which of the following chemical substances is NOT classified as an opioid?", "concept": "The answer is a: cocaine is not an opioid; it is a stimulant. Opioids are substances that act on the brain's opioid receptors, chiefly the mu receptor, the same receptors used by the body's own endorphins and enkephalins. They relieve pain, suppress coughing and cause euphoria and drowsiness, and in overdose they stop breathing; naloxone reverses an overdose by blocking the receptors. Opioids come in three groups. Natural opiates are taken from the sap of the opium poppy: morphine and codeine, a weaker relative used for cough and mild pain. Semi-synthetic opioids are made by chemically changing a natural one: heroin is diacetylmorphine, morphine with two acetyl groups added so that it enters the brain faster. Fully synthetic opioids, such as methadone and fentanyl, are built in the laboratory. Cocaine comes from a different plant, the coca leaf of South America. It blocks the reuptake of dopamine and noradrenaline (and serotonin), so these transmitters pile up in the synapse, giving alertness, confidence and euphoria, followed by a crash; heavy use can cause paranoia and cocaine psychosis. It is also a local anaesthetic, first used in eye surgery by Carl Koller (1884). Example: Arjun, who misuses codeine cough syrup, becomes drowsy with pinpoint pupils; his friend Kabir, who snorts cocaine at a party, becomes restless and talkative with dilated pupils. Option a is right because cocaine is a stimulant that works on monoamine reuptake, not on opioid receptors. Exam trap: in law, cocaine has long been grouped with heroin and morphine as a 'narcotic' drug, and 'cocaine' sounds like 'codeine', so students lump it with the opioids; pharmacologically the two classes have opposite effects.", "traps": {"b": "Codeine is a natural opiate alkaloid of the opium poppy, a weaker relative of morphine used in cough syrups and painkillers; the liver converts part of it to morphine, so it is a true opioid.", "c": "Heroin (diacetylmorphine) is a semi-synthetic opioid made by adding two acetyl groups to morphine; it acts at mu opioid receptors and is the classic opioid of dependence.", "d": "Morphine is the main natural alkaloid of opium and the reference opioid against which other painkillers are measured, so it cannot be the exception."}}, {"q": "Which structure of the brain is related to emotional processing?", "concept": "The answer is a: the amygdala is the brain structure most closely related to emotional processing. The amygdala (Greek for almond) is a cluster of nuclei deep in each medial temporal lobe, just in front of the hippocampus. It detects threat, gives events emotional importance, triggers fear and defensive responses, and learns which cues predict danger. Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1937) removed the temporal lobes, amygdala included, of rhesus monkeys: the animals became tame and fearless, approached snakes, put objects in their mouths and were hypersexual, a pattern now called Kluver-Bucy syndrome. Joseph LeDoux (from the 1980s) mapped fear conditioning in rats: a tone paired with shock comes to make the rat freeze, and lesions of the amygdala abolish this learned fear. He described a fast 'low road' from the thalamus straight to the amygdala and a slower 'high road' through the sensory cortex. In humans, patient S.M., whose amygdalae were destroyed on both sides by Urbach-Wiethe disease, could not recognise fear in faces (Ralph Adolphs and colleagues, 1994) and barely felt it herself. Example: Meera jumps back from a coiled shape on the path before she realises it is a rope; her amygdala has reacted through the low road before her cortex has identified the object. Option a is right because the amygdala is the core emotional structure in every textbook account. Exam trap: the other three also sit in emotion circuits, so students who know some neuroscience hesitate; choose the structure whose main job is emotion. Key note: the hippocampus, prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate all take part in emotion (James Papez's circuit, 1937; Paul MacLean's limbic system, 1952), so the stem is loose; the amygdala is the structure most tied to emotional processing, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The hippocampus, beside the amygdala in the medial temporal lobe, is chiefly for forming new declarative memories and spatial maps (patient H.M.); it was part of James Papez's circuit (1937) but is not the core emotional structure.", "c": "The prefrontal cortex plans, decides and regulates emotion from above, as Phineas Gage's personality change after his 1848 injury showed; it controls emotional responses rather than generating them.", "d": "The anterior cingulate cortex, on the medial surface of the frontal lobe, monitors conflict and errors and adds the distress to pain; it contributes to emotional awareness but is not the structure most tied to emotional processing."}}, {"q": "In signal-detection research, when no stimulus is presented and the subject reports seeing one, it is called a:", "concept": "The answer is c: in signal-detection research, reporting a stimulus when none was presented is called a false alarm. Classical psychophysics assumed a fixed threshold, but wartime radar operators showed that whether a person says 'yes' depends both on what they can sense and on how willing they are to say yes. Signal detection theory, set out by David Green and John Swets (1966), separates the two. On every trial a signal is either present or absent, and the observer says either 'yes' or 'no', which gives four outcomes. Signal present and 'yes' is a hit. Signal present and 'no' is a miss. Signal absent and 'yes' is a false alarm. Signal absent and 'no' is a correct rejection. From the hit and false alarm rates the researcher works out sensitivity (d prime, how well the person tells signal from noise) and the criterion (beta, how ready they are to say yes). A liberal observer, like a touchy smoke detector, gives many hits but also many false alarms; a conservative one gives few false alarms but more misses. Example: Kabir, an airport security screener, flags a bag as holding a knife when it holds only a steel tiffin box; no target was present but he said yes, so that is a false alarm. Option c is right because 'false alarm' is the signal-detection name for a yes response on a trial with no signal. Exam trap: students swap the two errors. A miss is saying no when the signal was there; a false alarm is saying yes when it was not. Key note: 'false positive' names the same event in diagnostic-testing language, so d is not wrong in plain terms; in signal-detection research the term is false alarm, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "A hit is a correct 'yes' when the signal really is present, the opposite cell of the table from a false alarm, which happens on a trial with no signal.", "b": "A miss is saying 'no' when a signal was present, a failure to detect; the stem describes the reverse error, a 'yes' when nothing was there.", "d": "False positive is the medical and statistical name (as in a Type I error) for the same kind of event, so it is a near synonym; but the stem asks for the signal-detection term, which is false alarm."}}, {"q": "When a light paired with a bell comes to elicit a conditioned response (CR) such as salivation, the phenomenon is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: when a light paired with a bell comes to elicit salivation, the phenomenon is higher-order conditioning. In classical conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in Conditioned Reflexes, a neutral stimulus (a bell) is repeatedly followed by an unconditioned stimulus (food) that naturally causes an unconditioned response (salivation). After pairing, the bell alone becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that produces salivation, now a conditioned response (CR). Higher-order conditioning adds a further layer. Once the bell is a strong CS, a new neutral stimulus, a light, is paired with the bell, and no food is given. The light then comes to make the dog salivate as well. The bell has acted as if it were the unconditioned stimulus; this is second-order conditioning, and a third stimulus paired with the light would give third-order conditioning. Each layer is weaker, and because the bell is now presented without food it slowly extinguishes, so higher-order responses fade. The idea explains how human fears and likings spread far beyond any original experience. Example: Arjun once failed a test and felt sick with dread, so the word 'exam' became a CS for anxiety. Later the school's long bell, which always rang just before the teacher announced an exam, came to make his heart race too, although the bell itself was never linked to failure. Option d is right because the light gains its power from a previously conditioned stimulus, not from food. Exam trap: do not confuse it with sensory preconditioning (Wilfred Brogden, 1939), where the two neutral stimuli are paired first and only afterwards is one of them conditioned; in higher-order conditioning the first CS is trained before the new stimulus is added.", "traps": {"a": "'Anomalous conditioning' is not a recognised type of conditioning; it is an invented distractor that borrows the word 'anomalous' (irregular) to sound technical.", "b": "'Distinctive conditioning' is not a standard term; the nearest real idea is discrimination, where a subject learns to respond to one stimulus and not to a similar one, which is not what happens with the light.", "c": "Counterconditioning, shown by Mary Cover Jones (1924) with Peter and the rabbit, replaces an existing CR with an incompatible one (fear with relaxation or pleasure); it removes a response rather than spreading it to a new stimulus."}}, {"q": "Which statistic is computed to assess the difference between expected frequencies and observed frequencies?", "concept": "The answer is d: chi-square is the statistic that compares observed frequencies with expected frequencies. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square test for nominal data, where there are no means, only counts of people in categories. For each category the researcher compares the observed frequency (O) with the frequency expected (E) if the null hypothesis were true, squares the difference and divides it by the expected value, then adds these up: chi-square = sum of (O minus E) squared over E. A large value means the counts depart from expectation by more than chance would allow. The goodness-of-fit test asks whether one variable's counts fit a hypothesised pattern, with degrees of freedom equal to the number of categories minus 1. The test of independence asks whether two categorical variables in a contingency table are related, with df = (rows minus 1) times (columns minus 1). It is a non-parametric test, needs raw counts rather than percentages, and works best when each expected frequency is at least 5. Example: Riya asks 120 students which of three canteen snacks they prefer. If there is no preference she expects 40 each; she observes 60, 35 and 25. Chi-square = 400/40 + 25/40 + 225/40 = 16.25, with 2 df, well above the 5.99 needed at the 0.05 level, so the preferences are not equal. Option d is right because only chi-square is built from the gap between observed and expected frequencies. Exam trap: Pearson's name also appears in option b, which tempts students who remember that Pearson invented chi-square; but Pearson's correlation measures the linear relationship between two continuous variables, not a gap between counts.", "traps": {"a": "Alpha is the significance level, the probability of a Type I error that the researcher sets in advance (often 0.05); the same letter also names Lee Cronbach's (1951) coefficient of internal consistency. Neither compares frequencies.", "b": "Pearson's product-moment correlation (r), also from Karl Pearson, measures the strength and direction of the linear relationship between two interval or ratio variables; it uses scores, not counts in categories.", "c": "A standardised beta-value is a regression coefficient in standard deviation units, showing how much the outcome changes for a one SD change in a predictor; it says nothing about observed versus expected frequencies."}}, {"q": "What hormone secreted by the pineal gland increases when periods of darkness begin and promotes a feeling of drowsiness?", "concept": "The answer is b: melatonin, secreted by the pineal gland, rises when darkness begins and promotes drowsiness. The pineal gland is a small, pine-cone-shaped gland near the centre of the brain; Rene Descartes (1649) thought it was the seat of the soul. Aaron Lerner (1958) and colleagues isolated its hormone, melatonin, from cattle pineal glands. Melatonin is made from serotonin and is released mainly at night. Its timing is set by the body's master clock, the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus. Light falling on special melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells reaches the SCN through the retinohypothalamic tract; in daylight the SCN keeps the pineal quiet, and as darkness falls the brake comes off, so melatonin climbs through the evening, peaks in the middle of the night and falls by morning. Rising melatonin lowers body temperature and alertness and signals 'night' to the body, so it works as a darkness hormone that opens the gate to sleep rather than as a strong sedative. Bright screens at night can suppress it and delay sleep, and low-dose melatonin is used to shift the clock in jet lag and delayed sleep phase. Example: Aditya scrolls on his phone until 2 a.m.; the bright light holds back his melatonin, so he lies awake even though he is tired. Option b is right because melatonin is the pineal hormone tied to darkness and drowsiness. Exam trap: cortisol also follows a daily rhythm, so it tempts; but cortisol, from the adrenal cortex, peaks in the early morning and helps wake us, the opposite timing.", "traps": {"a": "Testosterone is an androgen made mainly by the testes (with small amounts from the adrenal glands and ovaries); it governs male sexual development and libido, not sleep onset.", "c": "Cortisol, from the adrenal cortex under the HPA axis, peaks soon after waking (the cortisol awakening response) and promotes alertness and coping with stress.", "d": "Epinephrine (adrenaline), from the adrenal medulla, drives the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915), raising heart rate and arousal, the opposite of drowsiness."}}, {"q": "Learned helplessness is a feature typically seen in:", "concept": "The answer is d: learned helplessness is a feature typically seen in depression. Learned helplessness is the passive giving up that follows experience with events one cannot control. Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967) gave one group of dogs shocks they could stop by pressing a panel, a yoked group the same shocks with no way to stop them, and a third group no shocks. Later, in a shuttle box where jumping a low barrier ended the shock, most yoked dogs lay down and whined instead of escaping, while the other dogs learned quickly. The lack of control, not the shock itself, produced the passivity. Seligman (1975) proposed this as a model of depression: like the dogs, depressed people show passivity, low motivation, slowness to learn that their actions can work, and sad mood. Because the animal model ignored how people explain events, Lyn Abramson, Martin Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) reformulated it: depression follows when bad events are blamed on internal ('it's me'), stable ('it will last') and global ('it spoils everything') causes. This later grew into the hopelessness theory of depression. Example: Meera fails mathematics three times despite studying hard. She concludes 'I am stupid and nothing I do matters', stops attending classes, even in subjects she enjoys, and feels low most days. Option d is right because passivity after uncontrollable failure is the core of the helplessness model of depression. Exam trap: anxiety is the near miss, since the helpless dogs were also stressed and anxious people often feel out of control; but the classic model links helplessness to the giving up, low motivation and low mood of depression, not to the tense watchfulness of anxiety.", "traps": {"a": "Anxiety is the anticipation of future threat, with worry, tension, vigilance and avoidance; the person feels at risk but usually keeps trying to cope, unlike the passive giving up that Martin Seligman linked to depression.", "b": "Bipolar disorder is defined by manic or hypomanic episodes, often alternating with depressive ones; mania brings overactivity and inflated self-esteem, the opposite of helplessness, so the helplessness model is not tied to it.", "c": "Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5) produces paralysis, blindness or seizures without a neurological cause, which Sigmund Freud explained as anxiety converted into bodily symptoms; it has nothing to do with learned helplessness."}}, {"q": "Which area is known as the satiety centre?", "concept": "The answer is b: the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) is known as the satiety centre. The hypothalamus, a small structure below the thalamus, controls hunger, thirst, temperature and sexual behaviour. Two classic lesion studies built the dual-centre theory of eating. Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940) destroyed the VMH of rats: the rats overate (hyperphagia) and grew grossly obese, first gaining weight fast and then holding a new, higher weight. The VMH therefore seemed to be the 'stop eating' centre. The Indian physiologist Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck (1951) destroyed the lateral hypothalamus (LH) instead, and their rats refused to eat (aphagia), some starving to death; so the LH seemed to be the 'start eating' or hunger centre. Eliot Stellar (1954) joined the two: the LH switches eating on and the VMH switches it off. The model has since been revised. VMH lesions raise insulin, so food is stored as fat, and part of the effect comes from cutting fibres of the nearby paraventricular nucleus. Today the arcuate nucleus is seen as the hub where signals such as leptin from fat cells, insulin and the hunger hormone ghrelin are combined. Example: at a wedding feast Riya stops eating after the main course even when sweets arrive; signals of fullness acting on her hypothalamus have switched hunger off. Option b is right because the VMH is the structure textbooks label the satiety centre, its lesion causing overeating. Exam trap: the two centres are easily swapped. Lateral means hunger (lesion: the rat will not eat); ventromedial means satiety (lesion: the rat cannot stop). A memory aid: Ventromedial is 'Very Much' full.", "traps": {"a": "The lateral hypothalamus is the hunger or feeding centre of the dual-centre theory; Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck (1951) found that its lesions stop rats eating, the reverse of satiety.", "c": "The cerebrum, made up of the two cerebral hemispheres, handles thought, perception and voluntary action; it shapes eating through learned cues but is not the hypothalamic satiety centre.", "d": "The basal ganglia (striatum, globus pallidus and related nuclei) select and gate movement and learn habits; their disorders are Parkinson's and Huntington's disease, not overeating."}}, {"q": "Interpersonal therapy was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is c: interpersonal therapy (IPT) was developed by Gerald Klerman. Klerman, an American psychiatrist, and his wife Myrna Weissman built IPT as a brief treatment for depression and set it out in the manual Interpersonal Psychotherapy of Depression (1984). Its roots lie in Adolf Meyer's psychobiology (early 1900s), which saw mental illness as a response to life circumstances, in Harry Stack Sullivan's interpersonal theory of psychiatry (1953) and in John Bowlby's attachment theory (1969). IPT assumes that depression, whatever its causes, happens in a social context and is kept going by current relationship problems. It does not dig into childhood or interpret the transference; it treats depression as a medical illness and gives the patient the sick role. Therapy runs about 12 to 16 weekly sessions in three phases. After reviewing important relationships (the interpersonal inventory), therapist and patient choose one or two of four problem areas: grief, role disputes (conflict with a spouse, parent or boss), role transitions (marriage, a new job, retirement) and interpersonal deficits (isolation, few close ties). In the large NIMH depression trial (Irene Elkin and colleagues, 1989) IPT did about as well as cognitive behaviour therapy. Example: Kabir became depressed after retiring from the railways; his therapist names the problem area as a role transition and helps him mourn his old role and build a new one through a neighbourhood group. Option c is right because Klerman, with Weissman, created and manualised IPT. Exam trap: Sullivan's interpersonal theory and Alfred Adler's (1912) stress on social interest both sound interpersonal, so students credit them; Sullivan inspired IPT, but Klerman developed the therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Alfred Adler (1912) founded individual psychology, built on inferiority feelings, striving for superiority, social interest, birth order and style of life; his therapy is social in flavour, but he did not create IPT.", "b": "Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) founded analytical psychology, with the collective unconscious, archetypes and individuation; his therapy works on dreams and symbols, not on current role disputes and transitions.", "d": "Plato (about 427 to 347 BCE) was a Greek philosopher who divided the soul into reason, spirit and appetite in the Republic; he predates psychotherapy by more than two thousand years."}}, {"q": "Positive symptoms of schizophrenia are all except:", "concept": "The answer is a: affective flattening is not a positive symptom of schizophrenia; it is a negative symptom. Symptoms of schizophrenia are sorted by whether something is added to or taken away from normal experience. Positive symptoms are an excess or distortion of normal function: hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech (formal thought disorder) and grossly disorganised or bizarre behaviour. Negative symptoms are a loss or reduction of normal function: affective flattening, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition, anhedonia and asociality. Timothy Crow (1980) linked mainly positive symptoms to an acute Type I illness that responds to antipsychotics, and mainly negative symptoms to a chronic Type II illness with a poor drug response. Nancy Andreasen turned the split into two rating scales, the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS, 1982) and the Scale for the Assessment of Positive Symptoms (SAPS, 1984). The SAPS covers hallucinations, delusions, bizarre behaviour and positive formal thought disorder, and the last includes derailment, tangentiality, incoherence and circumstantiality. Affective flattening, the first domain of the SANS, is reduced emotional expression in face, voice and gesture. Example: Arjun, who has had schizophrenia for ten years, speaks in a flat monotone, rarely smiles or makes eye contact, and shows no change of expression when told of his sister's wedding. Option a is right because flattening is the absence of something normal, which makes it negative. Exam trap: circumstantiality looks like the odd one out, since it is mild and is also seen in obsessional personalities and in epilepsy; but Andreasen rates it as a positive formal thought disorder, because it adds needless detail rather than taking something away.", "traps": {"b": "Bizarre behaviour (odd clothing, agitated or aggressive acts, strange repetitive rituals) is one of the main domains of Nancy Andreasen's SAPS (1984); it is a positive symptom, so it cannot be the exception.", "c": "Circumstantiality is speech that wanders through needless detail before finally reaching its point (tangential speech never gets there); Andreasen's SAPS rates it as a positive formal thought disorder, so it belongs in the list.", "d": "Delusions are fixed false beliefs held despite clear contrary evidence, such as ideas of persecution or reference; they are the most typical positive symptom of all."}}, {"q": "What are the three \"justice rules\" that are employed most often as standards of fairness in social relationships?", "concept": "The answer is a: the three justice rules used most often as standards of fairness are equity, need and equality. They concern distributive justice, the fairness of how rewards and costs are shared out. Morton Deutsch (1975) argued that people rely on three main rules. Under equity, rewards are proportional to contribution, so whoever puts in more gets more; this is the idea behind J. Stacy Adams's equity theory (1965), in which people compare their ratio of outcomes to inputs with other people's. Under equality, everyone gets the same share whatever they put in. Under need, rewards go to those who need them most. Deutsch's central point was that the rule chosen depends on the goal of the relationship. Equity dominates where economic productivity is the aim, as in a business; equality where the aim is harmony and good relations, as among friends or teammates; and need where the aim is personal welfare and care, as in a family. Distributive justice differs from procedural justice, the fairness of the methods used to decide, studied by John Thibaut and Laurens Walker (1975). Example: Meera, Kabir and Riya earn Rs 3,000 from a stall at their college fest. Meera worked twice as many hours, so equity gives her the largest share; equality gives each Rs 1,000; need gives more to Riya, who must pay her hostel fee this week. Option a is right because equity, equality and need are the three standard distributive rules. Exam trap: each wrong option keeps one or two real rules and adds a familiar social psychology term that is not a justice rule at all (facilitation, interdependence, conformity); 'contribution' is simply equity under another name.", "traps": {"b": "Contribution is equity renamed and equality is real, but facilitation refers to social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898; Robert Zajonc, 1965), the change in performance when others are present, which is not a rule for sharing rewards.", "c": "Need and equality are genuine justice rules, but interdependence is the idea, from John Thibaut and Harold Kelley (1959) and from Morton Deutsch's own theory of cooperation and competition (1949), that people's outcomes depend on one another; it describes a relationship, not a fairness rule.", "d": "Contribution (equity) and need are real rules, but conformity, shown by Solomon Asch (1951), is yielding to group pressure, a form of social influence, not a standard for dividing rewards."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a behaviour therapy technique?", "concept": "The answer is b: de-reflection is not a behaviour therapy technique; it belongs to Viktor Frankl's logotherapy. Viktor Frankl (1946), a Viennese psychiatrist who survived the Nazi camps and wrote Man's Search for Meaning, founded logotherapy, an existential therapy built on the will to meaning. He described two special techniques. In paradoxical intention the person deliberately wishes for the feared symptom, which breaks the cycle of anticipatory anxiety. In de-reflection the person is helped to stop watching himself. Frankl argued that problems such as insomnia and sexual difficulties are made worse by hyper-reflection, anxious attention to one's own performance; de-reflection turns attention away from the self and the symptom towards meaningful tasks and other people. Its basis is existential, not learning theory. Behaviour therapy, by contrast, applies the laws of classical and operant conditioning. Systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) pairs relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared scenes. Flooding, close to the implosive therapy of Thomas Stampfl (1967), exposes the client to the most feared situation at full strength until anxiety falls. Counterconditioning, first shown by Mary Cover Jones (1924), replaces fear with a response incompatible with it. Example: Arjun lies awake each night checking whether he is falling asleep yet, which keeps him awake. Using de-reflection, his therapist asks him to stop monitoring his sleep and to spend the time planning the story he is writing for his niece; sleep comes once he stops chasing it. Option b is right because de-reflection comes from existential logotherapy, while the other three rest on conditioning. Exam trap: behaviour therapists borrowed Frankl's paradoxical intention for insomnia, so his methods can look behavioural, and 'reflection' may recall Carl Rogers' (1951) reflection of feeling; neither makes de-reflection a behaviour therapy technique.", "traps": {"a": "Flooding is a behaviour therapy technique based on the extinction of conditioned fear: the client stays in the most feared situation, in reality or imagination, until anxiety falls, with no escape allowed.", "c": "Counterconditioning, from Mary Cover Jones (1924) and Joseph Wolpe's reciprocal inhibition, replaces a conditioned fear with an incompatible response such as relaxation or eating; it is a core behaviour therapy principle.", "d": "Systematic desensitisation, from Joseph Wolpe (1958), combines relaxation training with a graded fear hierarchy to treat phobias by reciprocal inhibition; it is the classic behaviour therapy technique."}}, {"q": "Token Economy is an influential intervention based upon:", "concept": "The answer is a: the token economy is based on operant conditioning. Operant conditioning, set out by B. F. Skinner (1938) in The Behavior of Organisms, holds that behaviour is shaped by its consequences: a behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more frequent. A token economy applies this to a whole group, such as a hospital ward, a classroom or a hostel. Staff define clear target behaviours (making one's bed, attending a group, finishing homework), and each time one occurs the person receives a token: a point, a star or a plastic chip. Tokens have no value in themselves; they are secondary (conditioned) reinforcers that are later exchanged for backup reinforcers such as snacks, outings or privileges. Because they can buy many things they act as generalised reinforcers, like money, and they bridge the delay between a behaviour and the final reward. Tokens can also be taken back for problem behaviour, which is response cost. Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) ran the classic programme at Anna State Hospital, Illinois, where long-stay psychiatric patients earned tokens for self-care and ward jobs. When tokens were handed out regardless of work, the work almost stopped; when the contingency returned, so did the work. Example: in a children's home, Kabir earns a star each morning he is dressed and ready for school on time, and ten stars buy an extra hour of cricket on Sunday. Option a is right because the method works through contingent reinforcement, the heart of operant conditioning. Exam trap: tokens gain their value by being paired with backup rewards, which sounds like classical pairing, but the programme as a whole changes voluntary behaviour through its consequences, so it is operant; and Skinner supplied the principles, while Ayllon and Azrin built the token economy.", "traps": {"b": "Implicit learning, studied by Arthur Reber (1967) with artificial grammars, is picking up complex patterns without awareness; it is a cognitive phenomenon, not a system of planned reinforcement.", "c": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) links a neutral stimulus to one that already triggers a reflex and underlies systematic desensitisation and aversion therapy; the token economy changes voluntary behaviour through its consequences instead.", "d": "Client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) offers empathy, genuineness and unconditional positive regard in a non-directive relationship; rewards given only for target behaviours are the opposite of unconditional regard."}}, {"q": "Citing only positive attributes in a self-report measure of personality is a phenomenon referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is a: citing only positive attributes in a self-report measure of personality is called social desirability. Self-report inventories ask people to describe themselves, so a score can reflect how a person wants to look as well as what they are really like. Social desirability is the tendency to choose the answers that present oneself favourably, endorsing good qualities and denying faults, often without any deliberate intent. Allen Edwards (1957) showed that how socially desirable an item is strongly predicts whether people endorse it, so the bias can swamp the trait being measured. Douglas Crowne and David Marlowe (1960) built the Marlowe-Crowne Social Desirability Scale from items describing behaviour that is admirable but rare, such as never having disliked anyone, which almost nobody can honestly claim. Delroy Paulhus (1984) split the tendency into two parts: impression management, a conscious effort to look good to others, and self-deceptive enhancement, an honestly held but inflated view of oneself. Test makers control it with forced-choice items matched for desirability, as in the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule, with validity scales such as the MMPI's L (lie) and K (correction) scales, and with assurances of confidentiality. Its deliberate form is faking good; the reverse, exaggerating problems for gain, is faking bad or malingering. Example: Riya, filling in a personality questionnaire for a job she badly wants, marks that she is never late, never loses her temper and always finishes her work. Option a is right because social desirability is the established name for this one-sided positive self-presentation. Exam trap: self-deception (option d) is a real term, but it is only one of Paulhus's two components; the umbrella name for citing only positive attributes is social desirability.", "traps": {"b": "'Amplifying' is not a recognised response set; the nearest real ideas, symptom amplification and faking bad, mean exaggerating problems, the opposite of citing only positive attributes.", "c": "Projecting is a defence mechanism described by Sigmund Freud, attributing one's own unacceptable impulses to other people; the word also recalls projective tests, but neither means presenting oneself favourably on an inventory.", "d": "Self-deception, or self-deceptive enhancement, is one of Delroy Paulhus's (1984) two components of socially desirable responding, an honest but inflated self-view; it is a part of social desirability, not the name of the whole phenomenon."}}, {"q": "Functional analysis is a therapy based on:", "concept": "The answer is c: functional analysis is based on operant conditioning. Operant conditioning, set out by B. F. Skinner (1938), holds that behaviour is kept going by its consequences. Functional analysis asks what a problem behaviour does for the person: what it gets them, or what it lets them escape. Its simplest form is the ABC model. The antecedent is what happens just before the behaviour (a demand, a setting, a cue); the behaviour is defined so precisely that two observers would agree when it occurs; and the consequence is what follows and maintains it (attention, escape, a tangible reward or sensory stimulation). Frederick Kanfer and George Saslow (1965) widened the model to include the person's own state. Brian Iwata and colleagues (1982) made it experimental: they placed children who injured themselves in conditions offering attention, escape from tasks, being alone or play, and saw which condition raised the behaviour, revealing its function. Treatment then changes the contingencies: the payoff for the problem behaviour is removed (extinction), and a better behaviour that earns the same payoff is taught. Example: Kabir, aged seven, throws a tantrum whenever homework is set, and his mother then lets him watch television instead. The analysis shows that the tantrum is maintained by escape from homework, so she keeps the task in place, allows short breaks only when he asks calmly, and praises him for starting work. Option c is right because the analysis rests on the reinforcing consequences of behaviour, which is operant conditioning. Exam trap: antecedents can look like the stimuli of classical conditioning, but in functional analysis they are discriminative stimuli that signal when a behaviour will be reinforced; the consequences carry the explanation.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov, 1927) explains reflexive responses that become tied to a preceding stimulus; functional analysis instead explains voluntary behaviour by the consequences that follow it.", "b": "Humanistic principles (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) stress subjective experience, self-actualisation and the therapeutic relationship, and do not break behaviour down into antecedents and consequences.", "d": "Psychodynamic principles (Sigmund Freud) explain symptoms by unconscious conflict and childhood experience; functional analysis looks only at the observable events that trigger and maintain behaviour now."}}, {"q": "Procedural memory can be affected by damage to the:", "concept": "The answer is b: procedural memory can be affected by damage to the cerebellum. Procedural memory is memory for how to do things: motor skills such as cycling, typing or playing an instrument, and habits learned by repetition. It is a kind of implicit (nondeclarative) memory, shown in better performance rather than in conscious recall, and it is separate from declarative memory for facts and events, which depends on the hippocampus. The best evidence comes from patient H.M. (Henry Molaison). After William Scoville removed his medial temporal lobes in 1953 he could form no new conscious memories, yet Brenda Milner (1962) found that he improved day by day at mirror drawing, tracing a star while watching his hand in a mirror, though each time he denied having done the task before. His cerebellum and basal ganglia were intact. The cerebellum compares intended with actual movement and corrects the error, so it is vital for learning and timing motor skills; Richard Thompson (1980s) showed that eyeblink conditioning depends on it. Damage causes ataxia and poor motor learning. Example: after a cerebellar stroke, Meera, a tabla player, can still describe every rhythm she knows, but her strokes become uneven and she struggles to relearn even simple patterns. Option b is right because the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie most closely to procedural skill learning. Exam trap: the limbic system (hippocampus) is the memory answer for facts and events, not for skills; H.M. is the proof. Key note: procedural memory depends mainly on the basal ganglia (striatum) and the cerebellum, with the motor cortex involved; the basal ganglia are not offered, and the cerebellum is the structure textbooks tie to motor skill learning (H.M. still learned mirror drawing), so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "The cerebrum is the whole of both cerebral hemispheres, including the cortex, hippocampus and basal ganglia; it is far too broad to name the structure tied to procedural memory.", "c": "The motor cortex sends commands for voluntary movement and does change as skills are practised, so it is involved; but it executes movement rather than being the classic site of skill memory, which is the cerebellum.", "d": "The limbic system, especially the hippocampus and amygdala, supports declarative and emotional memory; H.M. lost most of his hippocampi yet still learned mirror drawing, which shows that skills do not depend on it."}}, {"q": "The term IQ was invented by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the term intelligence quotient (IQ) was invented by William Stern. The story begins in Paris. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test to pick out children who needed special schooling, using reasoning and judgement items of rising difficulty. In their 1908 revision the items were grouped by age, and a child's mental age was the age level of the items he or she could pass. A mental age alone is hard to compare across ages: being two years behind means far more at 5 than at 15. The German psychologist William Stern (1912) solved this by dividing mental age by chronological age, a ratio he called the intelligence quotient (Intelligenzquotient). Lewis Terman (1916) at Stanford University adapted the Binet scale for American children as the Stanford-Binet, multiplied Stern's ratio by 100 to remove the decimal, and made the abbreviation IQ famous. So ratio IQ = (mental age ÷ chronological age) × 100. Example: Arjun, aged 10, passes the items typical of 12-year-olds, so his ratio IQ is 12 ÷ 10 × 100 = 120. The ratio fails for adults, because mental age stops growing in the late teens, so David Wechsler (1939) replaced it with the deviation IQ, which has a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15 at every age. Option c is right because Stern coined both the quotient and the term. Exam trap: Terman is the trap, since his Stanford-Binet spread IQ testing and the abbreviation across the world. Key note: William Stern (1912) coined the intelligence quotient (mental age over chronological age); Lewis Terman (1916, Stanford-Binet) multiplied it by 100 and popularised the abbreviation IQ, so Terman is the trap; Stern stands.", "traps": {"a": "Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied Stern's ratio by 100 in the Stanford-Binet and popularised the abbreviation IQ in America, but he adopted the quotient rather than inventing it.", "b": "Gordon Allport (1937) was a trait theorist of personality, known for cardinal, central and secondary traits and the idiographic approach; he had no role in coining IQ.", "d": "John B. Watson (1913) founded behaviourism and rejected mental concepts in favour of observable behaviour; he did not invent the intelligence quotient."}}, {"q": "Skinner box is also known as:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Skinner box is also known as the operant chamber. B. F. Skinner (1938), in The Behavior of Organisms, needed a way to study operant behaviour, which an animal emits freely rather than as a reflex to a stimulus. His operant conditioning chamber is a small, sound-shielded box with a response device (a lever for a rat, a lit key for a pigeon), a dispenser that delivers food or water as the reinforcer, and often lights, a speaker or an electrified floor for cues or punishment. Every response is logged on a cumulative recorder, which plots the rate of responding over time, so schedules of reinforcement, shaping, extinction and discrimination can be measured precisely. Unlike Edward Thorndike's (1898) puzzle box, where a cat escaped once per trial, the chamber lets the animal respond again and again, so response rate becomes the main measure. Skinner himself preferred the name operant chamber to the nickname. Example: in a college laboratory, Riya watches a rat press a lever for a food pellet; within an hour its cumulative record climbs steeply. Option b is right because operant chamber is the formal name for the Skinner box. Exam trap: every distractor is a real term from learning theory. A simple latch was the release in Thorndike's puzzle box, omission training is a punishment procedure, and the 'black box' is the behaviourists' name for the unobservable mind, not an apparatus.", "traps": {"a": "A simple latch was the release mechanism of Edward Thorndike's (1898) puzzle boxes, which cats learned to work by trial and error; it belongs to a different apparatus and is not another name for the Skinner box.", "c": "Omission training is a procedure in which a response causes a pleasant reward to be withheld, a form of negative punishment; it is a technique that can be run in the chamber, not its name.", "d": "'Black box' is the behaviourists' description of the mind, treated as unobservable between stimulus and response; it is a metaphor, not Skinner's apparatus."}}, {"q": "Desensitisation is primarily used for:", "concept": "The answer is d: desensitisation is used mainly to alleviate phobias. Systematic desensitisation was developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958) in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition. It rests on classical conditioning: a phobia is a learned fear, so it can be unlearned. The central principle is reciprocal inhibition, the idea that two incompatible states, such as deep relaxation and anxiety, cannot occur at the same time. Treatment has three steps. First, the client learns progressive muscle relaxation, the method of Edmund Jacobson (1929). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a ladder of feared situations rated from least to most frightening on Wolpe's 0 to 100 Subjective Units of Disturbance Scale. Third, the relaxed client imagines each step in turn and moves up only when the current step no longer arouses anxiety. The roots go back to Mary Cover Jones (1924), who removed little Peter's fear of rabbits by bringing a rabbit closer while he ate. Example: Meera fears dogs. She first pictures the word 'dog', then a puppy across the road, then a dog on a lead nearby, and finally stroking one, relaxing at every step until the fear fades. Graded exposure, in imagination (in vitro) or in real life (in vivo), is still a first-line treatment for specific phobias. Option d is right because desensitisation was designed for, and works best on, specific learned fears such as phobias. Exam trap: examiners offer 'hysteria' because it sounds like an anxiety problem; hysteria (now conversion and dissociative disorders) was the classic target of hypnosis and the Freudian talking cure, not of graded relaxation. Also keep desensitisation apart from flooding, which puts the client at the top of the hierarchy at once, with no relaxation.", "traps": {"a": "Hallucinations and delusions are positive symptoms of psychosis, treated mainly with antipsychotic drugs and cognitive behaviour therapy for psychosis; relaxation plus a fear hierarchy does nothing to false perceptions or beliefs.", "b": "Hysteria, the old name for conversion and dissociative symptoms, was the target of the cathartic method and hypnosis of Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (Studies on Hysteria, 1895), not of Joseph Wolpe's graded relaxation.", "c": "Depression is treated behaviourally by behavioural activation (Peter Lewinsohn, 1970s) and cognitively by Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (1979); it is a disorder of low mood and lost reinforcement, not a conditioned fear."}}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques is a client-centered therapist most likely to use?", "concept": "The answer is c: a client-centred therapist is most likely to use reflection. Carl Rogers (1942, 1951) built client-centred, later person-centred, therapy on the belief that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive to grow, and that distress arises when the self-concept clashes with experience (incongruence). The therapist therefore does not diagnose, interpret or instruct. Instead, Rogers (1957) named the core conditions that let clients heal themselves: empathy (sensing the client's inner world as if it were one's own), unconditional positive regard (accepting the client without judgement) and congruence or genuineness (being real, not hiding behind a professional role). Reflection is the technique that shows empathy in action. The therapist restates the feeling behind the client's words, so the client feels understood and explores further. Example: Kabir says, 'My father keeps comparing me with my cousin.' The therapist replies, 'You sound hurt, and maybe tired of never being enough for him.' No advice, no theory; the direction stays with Kabir. This is why Rogers first called the approach non-directive. Option c is right because reflection of feeling is the signature, non-directive skill of the client-centred method. Exam trap: examiners surround reflection with directive look-alikes. Analysis and interpretation belong to Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, giving advice to directive counselling, and aversive conditioning to behaviour therapy; only reflection keeps the locus of evaluation inside the client.", "traps": {"a": "Analysis, the interpretation of free associations, dreams, resistance and transference, is the core of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (1900 onwards), in which the expert explains hidden meanings; Carl Rogers rejected the therapist as interpreter.", "b": "Giving advice is directive counselling, as in Edmund Williamson's trait-and-factor approach (1939), which puts the counsellor's judgement in charge; Carl Rogers held that advice undermines the client's own capacity to find answers.", "d": "Aversive conditioning pairs an unwanted behaviour with an unpleasant stimulus (for example, disulfiram for drinking) and belongs to behaviour therapy; its controlling, technique-led stance is the opposite of Carl Rogers's accepting relationship."}}, {"q": "In which disorder is the comprehension or the expression of language impaired?", "concept": "The answer is d: aphasia is the disorder in which the comprehension or expression of language is impaired. Aphasia (Greek a, without, and phasis, speech) is an acquired loss of language caused by brain damage, most often a stroke in the left hemisphere, which is dominant for language in about 95% of right-handers. It affects language itself, not just the muscles of speech, so reading, writing and naming usually suffer too. Paul Broca (1861) described expressive (non-fluent) aphasia after damage to the left inferior frontal lobe: halting, effortful speech with fair comprehension. Carl Wernicke (1874) described receptive (fluent) aphasia after damage to the left superior temporal lobe: fluent but empty speech with poor understanding. Global aphasia hits both. Example: after a stroke, Arjun's grandfather understands every word of the news but can only say 'water... go... no', a Broca-type aphasia. Option d is right because only aphasia names a disorder of language comprehension or expression. Exam trap: the four 'a-' words look alike, so learn the root of each. Apraxia (praxis, action) is loss of skilled, purposeful movement despite normal strength; agnosia (gnosis, knowledge) is failure to recognise objects despite working senses; aphagia (phagein, to eat) is inability to swallow. Do not mix aphasia with dysarthria either, which is slurred articulation from weak speech muscles with language intact.", "traps": {"a": "Apraxia is the loss of learned, skilled movements (waving goodbye, using a comb) despite intact strength and understanding, usually after left parietal or frontal damage; it is a disorder of action, not of language.", "b": "Agnosia, a term coined by Sigmund Freud (1891), is failure to recognise objects, faces (prosopagnosia) or sounds even though the senses work; it is a disorder of recognition, not of language.", "c": "Aphagia is an inability or refusal to swallow or eat; in biopsychology it is the classic result of lateral hypothalamic lesions in rats (Bal Anand and John Brobeck, 1951), not a language disorder."}}, {"q": "\"Hallucinations\" are examples of disorders of:", "concept": "The answer is b: hallucinations are disorders of perception. Descriptive psychopathology, laid out by Karl Jaspers (1913) in General Psychopathology, sorts symptoms by the mental function they disturb: consciousness, perception, thought (form and content), mood, memory and so on. A hallucination is a perception without an object: the person sees, hears, smells, tastes or feels something when no matching external stimulus is present, yet it is as vivid as a normal perception and seems to come from outside. Jean-Etienne Esquirol (1838) gave this modern definition and separated it from an illusion, which is a misperception of a real stimulus (a coat on a hook seen as a man). Auditory hallucinations, such as voices commenting on the person, are the commonest in schizophrenia; visual ones point more towards delirium or other organic causes. Example: Riya hears a man's voice calling her name in an empty room every night. Nothing in the room produced the sound, so it is a hallucination, a fault in perception. Option b is right because a hallucination is by definition a false perception, so in a mental status examination it is recorded under perception. Exam trap: examiners offer 'thinking' because hallucinations and delusions travel together in psychosis. A delusion is a false, fixed belief, a disorder of thought content; a hallucination is a false perception. Judgement (deciding how to act) and intelligence are separate headings altogether.", "traps": {"a": "Judgement is the ability to weigh a situation and act sensibly, tested by asking what the person would do on finding a stamped, addressed letter in the street; a hallucinating person may judge well or badly, but the symptom itself is perceptual.", "c": "Intelligence is general reasoning ability measured by tests such as the Wechsler scales; hallucinations occur in people of any IQ and are not a fault of intellect.", "d": "Disorders of thinking are delusions (thought content) and formal thought disorder such as loosening of associations (thought form); hallucinations often accompany them in psychosis but are classed under perception."}}, {"q": "Counterconditioning is an exposure therapy technique which involves:", "concept": "The answer is c: counterconditioning involves learning that an event or situation is no longer threatening. Counterconditioning is a behaviour therapy method built on the classical conditioning of Ivan Pavlov (Conditioned Reflexes, 1927). A feared stimulus is paired, again and again, with a new response that cannot coexist with fear, such as relaxation or eating something pleasant. The new response gradually replaces the old fear, so the stimulus stops signalling danger. The first clear case came from Mary Cover Jones (1924), a student of John B. Watson. She treated Peter, a boy of almost three who feared rabbits: while he ate a favourite food, a caged rabbit was brought a little closer each day until he let it nibble his fingers. Joseph Wolpe (1958) named the principle reciprocal inhibition and turned it into systematic desensitisation, pairing a graded hierarchy of feared scenes with deep muscle relaxation. Example: Kabir panics in lifts. While practising slow breathing and relaxation, he first stands near a lift, then rides one floor with his brother, then rides alone; calm replaces panic, and he learns that lifts are safe. Option c is right because the end point of counterconditioning is a changed meaning: the once-feared situation now brings calm and is learned to be harmless. Exam trap: option b, exposing the client to the feared stimulus, is the shared ingredient of every exposure therapy, flooding included; it is necessary but does not define counterconditioning. Key note: b describes what every exposure therapy does, so it is not false in plain terms; counterconditioning adds a new response incompatible with fear (relaxation, eating) so that the client learns the situation is no longer threatening, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Learning that one negative event is linked to another is how a fear is acquired in the first place, as in John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner's Little Albert study (1920); it is the opposite of what counterconditioning does.", "b": "Exposure to the feared stimulus is the common core of all exposure therapies, including flooding; counterconditioning also requires pairing that stimulus with an incompatible response, so b is incomplete as a definition.", "d": "Helping a client see that a behaviour is counterproductive is a cognitive or insight-oriented aim, closer to the disputing of beliefs in Albert Ellis's rational emotive therapy (1955), and involves no conditioning at all."}}, {"q": "Loss of insight is a feature of:", "concept": "The answer is c: loss of insight is a feature of schizophrenia. In the mental status examination, insight means the person's awareness that he is ill, that the symptoms come from his own mind, and that he needs treatment. It is graded on six levels, from (1) complete denial, through (3) awareness blamed on others or on physical causes and (5) intellectual insight, to (6) true emotional insight that changes behaviour. Poor insight is one of the commonest features of schizophrenia and other psychoses. The World Health Organization's International Pilot Study of Schizophrenia (1973) found lack of insight in about 97% of patients, the most frequent single symptom. Xavier Amador and colleagues (1991) likened it to anosognosia, the unawareness of deficit that Joseph Babinski (1914) described after right-hemisphere stroke, and poor insight predicts poor adherence to medication. Example: Arjun believes his neighbours are beaming thoughts into his head. When his sister suggests a doctor, he says, 'I am not ill, they are the problem.' That is insight grade 1. Option c is right because psychosis typically removes the ability to recognise one's own symptoms as illness. Exam trap: this is the old neurosis versus psychosis split. In anxiety and most depression (the old neuroses) the person knows something is wrong and often seeks help, so insight is kept; severe psychotic depression can lose it, but that is not the typical picture. Malingering is deliberate faking of symptoms for gain, so the person knows exactly what is going on.", "traps": {"a": "Anxiety disorders are the classic neurotic conditions: the person usually knows the fear is excessive and seeks help, so insight is preserved.", "b": "In most depression insight is kept and the person recognises being unwell; only severe psychotic depression with delusions may lose it, which is not the typical feature the stem is after.", "d": "Malingering is the conscious, deliberate production of false symptoms for an external gain such as money or escaping duty; the person is fully aware, so there is deception, not a genuine loss of insight."}}, {"q": "The main function of the ascending reticular activating system is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the main function of the ascending reticular activating system is the regulation of various degrees of arousal. The reticular formation (Latin reticulum, little net) is a net-like column of neurons running through the core of the medulla, pons and midbrain. Its ascending part, the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS), projects through the thalamus and directly to the whole cortex and sets the overall level of alertness, from coma and deep sleep through drowsiness to full wakefulness and focused attention. It also filters input, which is why a mother may sleep through traffic noise yet wake at her baby's faint cry. Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949) showed this in cats: electrical stimulation of the brainstem reticular formation switched the EEG from the slow, synchronised waves of sleep to the fast, desynchronised pattern of alertness, while damage to the system leaves an animal or a person in coma. Hans Eysenck (1967) later used ARAS arousal to explain introversion and extraversion. Example: Meera, dozing in a lecture, jolts awake when her name is called; her ARAS has raised cortical arousal in an instant. Option b is right because the ARAS acts as the brain's arousal dial, grading states of wakefulness. Exam trap: each distractor is the job of a nearby structure. Movement coordination belongs to the cerebellum, hunger and thirst to the hypothalamus, and pressure and pain to the dorsal column and spinothalamic pathways that reach the somatosensory cortex.", "traps": {"a": "Movement coordination, balance and the timing of skilled movement are the work of the cerebellum, which sits behind the pons in the hindbrain, not of the reticular arousal system.", "c": "Hunger, thirst, body temperature and sexual behaviour are regulated by the hypothalamus (its lateral and ventromedial nuclei for feeding), part of the diencephalon, not by the brainstem reticular formation.", "d": "Pressure and pain travel up the dorsal column and spinothalamic tracts, via the thalamus, to the somatosensory cortex in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe; the ARAS sets alertness, not specific sensations."}}, {"q": "Damage to Wernicke's area, situated in the left temporal lobe, causes:", "concept": "The answer is a: damage to Wernicke's area causes impaired understanding of speech and written language. Carl Wernicke (1874), a German neurologist, described patients with damage to the back of the left superior temporal gyrus, next to the primary auditory cortex. This area links speech sounds to their meanings. When it is damaged the result is Wernicke's (receptive, fluent or sensory) aphasia. Speech flows easily with normal rhythm, but it is empty or makes little sense: words are swapped (paraphasias), new words are made up (neologisms) and, in severe cases, the result is word salad. The person understands little of what is said or read and is often unaware of the errors. Compare Paul Broca (1861), whose patient Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan', had damage to the left inferior frontal lobe: Broca's aphasia gives slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with fairly good understanding. Norman Geschwind (1965) linked the two areas through the arcuate fasciculus. Example: after a stroke, Kabir's grandmother chats fluently but says, 'The blue glass went to the doctor's cooking,' and cannot follow a simple request to close the door. Option a is right because Wernicke's area is the comprehension area, so its loss hits the understanding of spoken and written language. Exam trap: examiners swap Broca and Wernicke. Remember 'Broca, broken speech (production); Wernicke, wordy but without meaning (comprehension)'. Impaired articulation (option c) is Broca's aphasia or dysarthria.", "traps": {"b": "Impaired vision follows damage to the occipital lobe (primary visual cortex) or the visual pathways, not to the temporal language area.", "c": "Impaired speech production and articulation is Broca's (expressive, non-fluent) aphasia after damage to the left inferior frontal lobe, described by Paul Broca (1861); this is the classic reversal.", "d": "Impaired movement follows damage to the primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe, the basal ganglia or the cerebellum, not to the temporal comprehension area."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a component of executive function?", "concept": "The answer is c: contralateral neglect is not a component of executive function. Executive functions are the top-down control processes, based mainly in the prefrontal cortex, that let us plan, stay on task, switch goals and stop ourselves acting on impulse. Akira Miyake and colleagues (2000) gave adults a battery of tasks and found three related but separable core components: updating (holding and refreshing information in working memory), shifting (switching flexibly between tasks or mental sets) and inhibition (suppressing a strong but wrong response). Classic tests match each one: the n-back for working memory, the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (David Grant and Esta Berg, 1948) for set shifting, and the Stroop test (John Ridley Stroop, 1935) for response inhibition. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that frontal patients keep sorting cards by an old rule, the perseveration of the dysexecutive syndrome. Contralateral, or hemispatial, neglect is something different. After damage, usually to the right parietal lobe, the person ignores the opposite (left) side of space: eats from only the right half of the plate, shaves half the face and crowds all the numbers onto the right of a clock drawing. It is a disorder of spatial attention, not of executive control. Example: Riya's grandfather, after a right parietal stroke, keeps bumping into the left door frame, yet he plans his day and switches tasks normally. Option c is right because neglect is a parietal attention syndrome, outside the frontal executive triad. Exam trap: 'contralateral' sounds technical and brain-related, so students assume it belongs; ask whether the item is a control process (it is not) and which lobe it involves (parietal, not prefrontal).", "traps": {"a": "Working memory updating, holding and refreshing information online (tested by the n-back), is one of the three core executive functions in Akira Miyake's (2000) model, and the central executive of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) sits at its heart.", "b": "Set shifting, switching flexibly between rules or tasks (tested by the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test and Trail Making Part B), is a core executive function; perseverative errors after frontal damage show it failing.", "d": "Response inhibition, suppressing a strong automatic response (the Stroop colour-word test, go/no-go tasks), is a core executive function; its loss gives the impulsive, disinhibited behaviour seen in Phineas Gage (1848)."}}, {"q": "Apparent motion seen in movies and on television is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the apparent motion seen in movies and on television is stroboscopic motion. A film is a series of still pictures, each slightly different from the last. Cinema shows about 24 frames a second (television about 25 to 30), and the brain fills the gaps so that we see smooth, continuous movement where there is none. This is one kind of apparent motion, the perception of movement when no object actually moves. Its laboratory version is the phi phenomenon of Max Wertheimer (1912): two lines flashed one after the other in different places, at the right interval (about 60 milliseconds), look like a single line moving from one place to the other. That finding launched Gestalt psychology, because the movement exists in the whole pattern, not in either flash. Example: Arjun makes a flip book, drawing a stick figure with its arm slightly higher on each page; flicked quickly, the figure seems to wave. Option c is right because films and television create motion purely by the rapid succession of still frames, which is the definition of stroboscopic motion. Exam trap: induced movement (option d) is also apparent motion but has a different cause: a still object seems to move because its frame moves, as when the moon seems to race through drifting clouds. The autokinetic effect, a still light in a dark room that seems to wander, which Muzafer Sherif (1935) used to study group norms, is a third type.", "traps": {"a": "Real motion is the actual movement of an object through space, signalled by motion-detecting neurons in area MT (V5); on a screen nothing moves, the still frames are only replaced.", "b": "Visual deprivation is the withholding of normal visual experience, as in the kittens of Torsten Wiesel and David Hubel (1963) or Colin Blakemore and Grahame Cooper (1970); it is a research condition, not a kind of perceived motion.", "d": "Induced movement is apparent motion of a still object caused by the movement of its surrounding frame (the moon seeming to sail through moving clouds); films work instead by rapid still frames."}}, {"q": "Those who argue that innate or inborn processes affect perception are called:", "concept": "The answer is a: those who argue that innate or inborn processes shape perception are called nativists. The nativism versus empiricism debate is one of psychology's oldest questions: is the ability to see depth, form and size built in, or learned from experience? Nativists hold that the mind arrives with inborn structures. Rene Descartes (1641) wrote of innate ideas, and Immanuel Kant (1781) argued that space and time are built-in forms through which all experience is organised. Empiricists answer that perception is learned: John Locke (1690) called the newborn mind a tabula rasa, a blank slate, and Hermann von Helmholtz (1867) described perception as unconscious inference from past experience. The best-known evidence for the nativist side is the visual cliff of Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960): most crawling infants aged 6 to 14 months refused to cross a glass sheet over an apparent drop, even when their mothers called, and newborn chicks and goats avoided the deep side at once. Example: baby Meera, just crawling, stops at the edge of the stairs without ever having fallen; a nativist says her sense of depth was inborn. Option a is right because 'nativist' is the precise label for the innate, inborn position. Exam trap: the Gestalt psychologists (Max Wertheimer, 1912) leaned nativist, holding that the brain organises wholes by built-in laws, so 'Gestaltist' is the near miss; but the general position is called nativism, and Gestalt is one school that drew on it. Behaviourists were radical empiricists.", "traps": {"b": "Gestaltists (Max Wertheimer, Kurt Koffka and Wolfgang Kohler, from 1912) held that the brain organises wholes by built-in laws and so leaned nativist, but 'Gestaltist' names a school of perceptual organisation, not the general innate-versus-learned position.", "c": "Behaviourists such as John B. Watson (1913) were extreme empiricists who held that behaviour, perception included, is learned through conditioning; they are the opposite of nativists.", "d": "Psychoanalysts follow Sigmund Freud (1900) and study unconscious drives and conflicts; the debate over whether perception is innate or learned is not what defines them."}}, {"q": "Which construct from Sternberg's triarchic theory of intelligence includes the ability to find the best fit between the individual and the environment?", "concept": "The answer is b: practical intelligence is the triarchic construct that includes finding the best fit between the individual and the environment. Robert Sternberg (1985), in Beyond IQ, proposed that intelligence has three linked parts. Analytical (componential) intelligence is the ability to analyse, compare and evaluate, the skill that ordinary IQ and school tests measure; it runs on metacomponents (planning and monitoring), performance components and knowledge-acquisition components. Creative (experiential) intelligence is the ability to cope with novelty and to make familiar tasks automatic. Practical (contextual) intelligence is 'street smarts': using one's abilities in everyday life through three moves, adapting to the environment, shaping it to suit oneself, or selecting a new one when the first does not fit. Much of it rests on tacit knowledge, the unspoken know-how of a job or setting. Example: Riya joins a busy Mumbai office. First she adjusts to its pace (adapting); then she persuades her manager to let her work early hours (shaping); finally, finding the culture still wrong for her, she moves to a smaller firm (selecting). Option b is right because adapting to, shaping and selecting environments for the best person-environment fit is exactly how Sternberg defines practical intelligence. Exam trap: students confuse creative with practical because both sound like real-world skills; creative intelligence is about novelty and insight, practical intelligence about fitting one's context. 'Linguistic' comes from Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences, and 'incremental' from Carol Dweck's theories of intelligence, not from Sternberg.", "traps": {"a": "Creative (experiential) intelligence is Robert Sternberg's ability to handle novel tasks and to automatise familiar ones, shown in insight and invention; it concerns novelty, not person-environment fit.", "c": "Incremental comes from the implicit theories of Carol Dweck and Ellen Leggett (1988): an incremental theorist believes intelligence can grow with effort, unlike an entity theorist; it is not part of the triarchic theory.", "d": "Linguistic intelligence is one of Howard Gardner's (1983) multiple intelligences in Frames of Mind, the sensitivity to the sounds and meanings of words seen in poets; Robert Sternberg's theory has no linguistic component."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following is NOT an example of cognitive learning?", "concept": "The answer is b: programmed learning is not an example of cognitive learning. Cognitive learning theories hold that learning changes mental representations and understanding, not just stimulus-response bonds, and that it can happen without reinforcement or any visible behaviour. Three classic examples fill the other options. Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930) found that rats allowed to explore a maze without food learned it anyway; once food was added they ran it as well as rats rewarded all along (latent learning). Edward Tolman (1948) argued that the rats had formed a cognitive map, a mental layout of the maze. Wolfgang Kohler (1917) watched the chimpanzee Sultan suddenly join two sticks to reach a banana, insight learning by restructuring the whole problem. Programmed learning comes from the opposite camp. B. F. Skinner (1954), in The Science of Learning and the Art of Teaching, applied operant conditioning to the classroom: material is broken into small steps (frames), the learner responds actively at each step, gets immediate feedback that acts as reinforcement, and moves at his own pace. Teaching machines and many computer drills follow this design. Example: Kabir learns spellings on an app that shows one word at a time and flashes 'Correct!' the moment he answers; that is programmed learning, behaviourist through and through. Option b is right because programmed learning rests on Skinner's operant reinforcement, not on cognitive maps or insight. Exam trap: because programmed learning is used in classrooms and seems mental, students file it under cognitive; check the mechanism instead, which is reinforcement of small responses.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive maps are Edward Tolman's (1948) mental layouts of an environment, inferred from rats that took new shortcuts in a maze; they are the textbook example of cognitive learning.", "c": "Latent learning is learning that stays hidden until there is a reason to show it, as in the unrewarded rats of Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930); it shows learning without reinforcement and so is cognitive.", "d": "Insight learning is the sudden restructuring of a problem, shown by Wolfgang Kohler's (1917) chimpanzee Sultan joining two sticks; it is the Gestalt, cognitive form of learning."}}, {"q": "The simplest form of thinking is:", "concept": "The answer is d: perceptual thinking is the simplest form of thinking. Thinking is the mental handling of images, concepts and symbols to understand, solve problems or create. Many educational psychology texts used in India arrange the types of thinking as a ladder from simple to complex. At the bottom is perceptual or concrete thinking, which works directly on what the senses present here and now (objects, pictures, events), with little use of language or general ideas. Young children think this way, which echoes the stages of Jean Piaget (1952) in which thought is tied to what can be seen and handled. Next comes conceptual or abstract thinking, which uses general concepts and symbols that are not present, such as 'justice' or 'number'. Higher still is reflective or logical thinking, the careful, step-by-step reasoning that John Dewey (1910) described in How We Think, and at the top is creative thinking, which produces new and useful ideas, the divergent production of J. P. Guilford (1956). Example: five-year-old Meera counts toffees by touching each one (perceptual); her elder brother Arjun works out a sum written only in numbers (abstract). Option d is right because perceptual thinking needs only sense impressions and so comes first and simplest. Exam trap: 'abstract' can sound basic because it is vague, but it is a higher form; logical and creative thinking build on it. Key note: the ranking (perceptual or concrete thinking as the simplest, then conceptual or abstract, reflective or logical, creative) comes from Indian educational psychology texts such as S. K. Mangal, not from Western cognitive psychology, which does not rank thinking this way; perceptual thinking is the answer those texts give, and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Abstract (conceptual) thinking uses general ideas and symbols that are not physically present, such as 'freedom' or an algebraic x; it builds on perceptual thinking and is the next, higher level.", "b": "Logical (reflective) thinking is deliberate, step-by-step reasoning towards a conclusion, as in John Dewey's (1910) reflective thinking; it needs concepts and rules, so it is a higher form.", "c": "Creative thinking generates original, useful ideas through divergent production (J. P. Guilford, 1956) and sits at the top of the ladder as the most complex form, not the simplest."}}, {"q": "Self-monitoring is a form of clinical observation involving which of the following?", "concept": "The answer is c: self-monitoring is a form of clinical observation in which clients observe and record their own behaviour. In behavioural assessment the clinician wants a functional analysis of the problem: what happens just before it (antecedents), the behaviour itself, and what follows (consequences), the ABC model. Watching a client in the clinic shows only a thin slice of life, and many target behaviours (bingeing, purging, urges, worrying thoughts) are private or happen at home. So the client becomes the observer, keeping a structured diary, a counter or an app and noting each episode as it happens, with time, place, intensity, thoughts and what came next. Frederick Kanfer (1970) made self-monitoring the first step of self-regulation, followed by self-evaluation and self-reinforcement. It has a useful side effect called reactivity: recording a behaviour tends to change it, usually reducing unwanted acts and increasing wanted ones. That spoils a clean baseline but helps treatment. Example: Kabir, who smokes, writes down every cigarette, the time and the trigger; within a week he sees that most follow tea breaks with colleagues, and his count is already dropping. Option c is right because self-monitoring is defined as clients systematically observing and recording their own behaviour. Exam trap: two look-alikes. Clients measuring their own heart rate or galvanic skin response is psychophysiological recording, the basis of biofeedback (Neal Miller's operant control of autonomic responses, 1969). And the self-monitoring of Mark Snyder (1974) is a personality trait from social psychology, how much a person tailors self-presentation to the situation, not a clinical recording method.", "traps": {"a": "Measuring one's own galvanic skin response is psychophysiological recording used in biofeedback; it tracks arousal through the skin's electrical conductance, not the target behaviour that self-monitoring records.", "b": "Measuring one's own heart rate is again a physiological index, typical of biofeedback training, not the observation and logging of one's own actions, thoughts and situations.", "d": "Recording dreams for interpretation belongs to psychodynamic therapy, from Sigmund Freud's The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) to Carl Jung's dream analysis; it is not a behavioural observation method."}}, {"q": "The process by which a stimulus is attended to, identified, studied and incorporated into memory is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: the process by which a stimulus is attended to, identified, studied and incorporated into memory is called encoding. Memory is usually described in three stages. Encoding turns incoming information into a form the memory system can hold, a mental code that may be visual (how it looks), acoustic (how it sounds) or semantic (what it means). Storage keeps that code over time, and retrieval brings it back when needed. Encoding needs attention: Raymond Nickerson and Marilyn Adams (1979) found that fewer than half of adults could pick out the correct drawing of a coin they had handled thousands of times, because the details had never been encoded. Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968) built the multi-store model on this flow: attention moves information from sensory memory into short-term memory, and rehearsal transfers it to long-term memory. Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972) showed that depth of encoding matters: judging a word's meaning gives better recall than judging its sound or typeface, the levels of processing effect. Example: Meera meets the term 'prosopagnosia', notices it, sounds it out, links it to the Greek prosopon (face) and thinks of her aunt who cannot recognise faces; each step is encoding, and the deeper links make it last. Option c is right because attending to, identifying and processing a stimulus so that it enters memory is exactly what encoding means. Exam trap: storage is the next stage, the holding of what has already been encoded, so students who latch on to 'incorporated into memory' pick it. Chunking (George Miller, 1956) is one strategy within encoding, and reasoning is a thinking process.", "traps": {"a": "Storage is the second stage of memory, the retention of already encoded information over time; attending, identifying and studying describe the earlier step of getting information in.", "b": "Chunking, described by George Miller (1956), groups separate items into larger meaningful units (98 45 61 23 instead of eight single digits) to fit more into short-term memory; it is an encoding strategy, not the name of the whole process.", "d": "Reasoning is drawing conclusions from premises or evidence by deduction or induction; it is a thinking process, not a stage of memory."}}, {"q": "Memories outside of conscious awareness are called:", "concept": "The answer is d: memories that operate outside conscious awareness are called implicit memories. Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter (1985) defined implicit memory as an influence of past experience on behaviour, feeling or thought without conscious recollection. Explicit memory is the opposite: you deliberately and consciously retrieve something, as when you recall a list or recognise a face, so it is tested by recall and recognition. Larry Squire (1992) used the parallel terms declarative (explicit) and nondeclarative (implicit) memory. Implicit memory includes procedural skills (typing, cycling), priming (a word seen earlier is completed faster on a word-fragment test even if you do not remember seeing it), classically conditioned responses and habituation. The strongest evidence comes from amnesia. Henry Molaison (patient H.M.) could not consciously remember new events after hippocampal surgery in 1953, yet Brenda Milner (1962) showed that he improved day by day at mirror drawing while insisting he had never done the task. So explicit memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe, while implicit memory relies on other systems such as the striatum and cerebellum. Example: Arjun types his email password perfectly every morning without thinking, but if asked to say the characters aloud he has to mime typing to recover them; his fingers hold an implicit memory. Option d is right because working outside conscious awareness is the defining feature of implicit memory. Exam trap: options a and b borrow from interference theory, where proactive interference (old learning disrupts new) and retroactive interference (new disrupts old) cause forgetting; 'proactive memories' and 'reactive memories' are not memory systems at all.", "traps": {"a": "'Proactive' belongs to proactive interference, where older learning disrupts recall of newer material (shown by Benton Underwood in 1957); it is a cause of forgetting, not a kind of unconscious memory.", "b": "There is no 'reactive memory'; the term echoes retroactive interference, where new learning disrupts recall of older material, a cause of forgetting rather than a memory system.", "c": "Explicit memory is the opposite: conscious, deliberate recollection of facts and events (Larry Squire's declarative memory), tested by recall and recognition."}}, {"q": "The Bobo doll experiment, famously conducted in the 1960s, was done by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the Bobo doll experiment was conducted by Albert Bandura. Albert Bandura, with Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), tested whether children learn aggression simply by watching. Seventy-two nursery-school children, aged about 3 to 6, were taken one at a time to a playroom where an adult model either attacked a large inflatable Bobo doll (punching it, hitting it with a mallet, kicking it and shouting 'Sock him in the nose!') or played quietly with toys; a control group saw no model. Later, after being mildly frustrated, each child was left with toys that included a Bobo doll. Children who had watched the aggressive model copied many of its exact acts and words, and boys showed more physical aggression than girls. No one had rewarded them. A 1963 follow-up showed that filmed and cartoon models worked almost as well as live ones, and a 1965 study showed that seeing the model punished reduced imitation but not learning: once offered a reward, those children reproduced the acts too. These studies founded social learning theory, later social cognitive theory, with its four processes of attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. Example: after watching an older cousin slam a door in anger, little Arjun slams his toy cupboard the same way the next day. Option b is right because Bandura designed and ran the Bobo doll studies at Stanford in the early 1960s. Exam trap: 'James Lange' is not one person but a merged label for the James-Lange theory of emotion (William James, 1884; Carl Lange, 1885), and the other two names are famous psychologists from unrelated fields.", "traps": {"a": "James Lange is a garbled merger of William James (1884) and Carl Lange (1885), who independently proposed that emotion is the perception of bodily changes; neither ran any study of imitated aggression.", "c": "Daniel Kahneman, with Amos Tversky (1974), studied heuristics and biases in judgement and won the 2002 Nobel prize in economics; he did not study the imitation of aggression.", "d": "Abraham Maslow (1943) proposed the hierarchy of needs and self-actualisation, a humanistic theory of motivation; he ran no observational learning experiments."}}, {"q": "'Ethics' can be defined as the formulation of principles to:", "concept": "The answer is d: ethics is the formulation of principles that guide behaviour, telling people what they ought and ought not to do. The word comes from the Greek ethos, meaning character or custom. Ethics is wider than any one tool for changing behaviour: it is a set of standards of right conduct that people use to decide how to act, especially when interests conflict. Professional ethics turns these standards into codes. In research, modern codes grew out of abuse: the Nuremberg Code (1947) after Nazi experiments, the Declaration of Helsinki (1964) and the Belmont Report (1979), which named three principles: respect for persons (applied through informed consent), beneficence (do no harm and maximise benefit) and justice (fair selection of participants). Psychologists follow the American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016), and Indian researchers follow the Indian Council of Medical Research's National Ethical Guidelines (2017). These codes do not mainly punish or reward; they guide decisions in advance. Example: Meera plans to study anxiety in college students. Ethical principles guide her to obtain informed consent, tell students they may withdraw at any time, keep their answers confidential, and offer debriefing and help if a questionnaire upsets anyone. Option d is right because guiding behaviour is the purpose of ethical principles; reducing bad behaviour or rewarding good behaviour are at most side effects. Exam trap: the distractors borrow the language of operant conditioning (reinforce, punish, reduce behaviour), which makes them sound psychological and precise; but ethics is a guide to conduct, not a reinforcement schedule.", "traps": {"a": "Reducing inappropriate behaviour is the goal of behaviour-modification techniques such as extinction or response cost; ethical principles may have that effect, but their purpose is to guide all conduct.", "b": "Punishing inappropriate behaviour is the job of law or of disciplinary bodies that enforce a code after a breach; ethics itself sets out the standards before anyone acts.", "c": "Reinforcing appropriate behaviour is the operant language of B. F. Skinner (1938); ethics states what appropriate behaviour is but does not work by rewarding it."}}, {"q": "The most basic perceptual organisation is:", "concept": "The answer is c: figure-ground is the most basic perceptual organisation. When we look at a scene, the first thing the visual system does is split it into a figure, the object that stands out, and a ground, the background it stands against. Only after this split can elements be grouped, depth judged or shapes recognised. Edgar Rubin (1915), a Danish psychologist, described the rules: the figure looks thing-like, has a clear contour, seems nearer and is better remembered; the ground looks shapeless, seems to continue behind the figure and lies farther away. His famous Rubin's vase is a reversible figure: you see either a white vase or two dark faces in profile, never both at once, although the picture never changes. That shows the brain, not the stimulus, does the organising. The Gestalt psychologists, Max Wertheimer (1923) and Kurt Koffka (1935), built their grouping laws of proximity, similarity, closure and continuity on top of this basic split. Example: Riya reads black letters on a white page; the letters are figure and the page is ground. If the page were covered in busy patterns she would struggle to read, which is how camouflage works. Option c is right because figure-ground segregation comes first and every other organisation depends on it. Exam trap: the law of similarity is a real Gestalt principle, but it groups figures that have already been separated from the ground, and shape constancy and depth perception are later processes that need objects to exist first.", "traps": {"a": "Shape constancy is seeing an object's shape as stable despite a changing retinal image (a door still looks rectangular as it swings open); it needs an object already segregated from its ground.", "b": "Depth perception judges distance from binocular cues (retinal disparity, convergence) and monocular cues (interposition, linear perspective), tested on the visual cliff of Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960); it works on figures already formed.", "d": "The law of similarity is a Gestalt grouping principle of Max Wertheimer (1923) by which alike items are seen as belonging together; it groups figures after figure and ground have been separated, so it is not the most basic."}}, {"q": "The degree of attraction among group members relates to the dimension of:", "concept": "The answer is c: the degree of attraction among group members relates to the dimension of cohesiveness. Group cohesiveness is the sum of the forces that keep members in a group: liking for one another, commitment to the group's task and pride in belonging. Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back (1950) defined it as the total field of forces acting on members to remain in the group, in their study of student housing at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, where friendships and shared opinions formed most among near neighbours. Cohesive groups have better attendance, more satisfied members and stronger pressure to conform. They perform better only when their norms favour hard work, and very high cohesion is one of the conditions for groupthink, described by Irving Janis (1972), in which a tight group suppresses doubt and makes poor decisions. Example: Kabir's college cricket team practises together, eats together and defends one another; members rarely quit and feel proud wearing the team cap, a sign of high cohesiveness. Option c is right because cohesiveness is specifically the attraction of members to one another and to the group. Exam trap: conformity sounds close, because cohesive groups produce more conformity; but conformity is a member's change in behaviour to match the group, an effect, while cohesiveness is the attraction itself. Structure refers to roles, status and norms, and compatibility to how well members' needs mesh.", "traps": {"a": "Compatibility is how well members' interpersonal needs fit together, as in William Schutz's (1958) FIRO theory of inclusion, control and affection; well-matched members may like each other, but the attraction itself is called cohesiveness.", "b": "Group structure is the stable pattern of roles, status, norms and communication links within a group (who leads, who keeps the records); it describes organisation, not how strongly members are drawn to one another.", "d": "Conformity is changing one's behaviour or belief to match the group, studied by Muzafer Sherif (1935) and Solomon Asch (1951); cohesiveness increases conformity, but conformity is the effect, not the attraction."}}, {"q": "To reduce the Type I error, an experimenter:", "concept": "The answer is a: to reduce the Type I error, an experimenter changes the alpha level from 0.05 to 0.01. A Type I error is rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true: a false positive, claiming an effect that does not exist. Its probability is alpha, the significance level the researcher fixes before collecting data. With alpha at 0.05 the researcher accepts a 5% risk of a false positive whenever the null is true; at 0.01 the risk falls to 1%. The two-error framework comes from Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933). A Type II error is the reverse, keeping a false null (a false negative), with probability beta; power, 1 minus beta, is the chance of detecting a real effect. The two trade off: a stricter alpha makes the null harder to reject, so Type I risk falls but Type II risk rises unless the sample is enlarged. Example: Riya tests whether a memory app improves exam scores. If she demands p below 0.01 rather than 0.05, she is less likely to announce a benefit that is not there, but more likely to miss a small real one. Option a is right because the Type I error rate equals alpha, so lowering alpha lowers it directly. Exam trap: option d sounds good because bigger samples improve almost everything, but a larger sample raises power and cuts Type II error; the Type I rate stays at whatever alpha is set. Raising alpha to 0.10 (option b) doubles the Type I risk.", "traps": {"b": "Changing alpha from 0.05 to 0.10 makes the test more lenient: rejecting the null becomes easier, so the risk of a Type I error doubles, the opposite of what the stem asks.", "c": "Beta is the probability of a Type II error (missing a real effect); holding it constant does nothing to the false-positive rate, which is set by alpha alone.", "d": "A larger sample shrinks the standard error and raises power, so it reduces Type II error; the Type I rate stays fixed at whatever alpha the researcher chooses."}}]});
